{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_78","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/zsh -lc \"cd /home/hanqing/agents/ai-slop/research/incremental-causal-20261004-case-047/auditor2/workspace/output/alias-3389297662643f29363dbb4b && python3 - <<'PY'\nimport hashlib, os, json\n\nBASE = os.getcwd()\ndef h(p):\n    return hashlib.sha256(open(os.path.join(BASE,p),'rb').read()).hexdigest()\n\nR = {\n \\\"gh_global\\\":\\\"receipts/gh_global_advisory.json\\\",\n \\\"gh_repo\\\":\\\"receipts/gh_repo_advisory.json\\\",\n \\\"nvd\\\":\\\"receipts/nvd_cve.json\\\",\n \\\"osv\\\":\\\"receipts/osv_cve.json\\\",\n \\\"fixdiff\\\":\\\"receipts/fix_c898d34.diff\\\",\n \\\"pr36011\\\":\\\"receipts/pr_36011.json\\\",\n \\\"pr36314\\\":\\\"receipts/pr_36314.json\\\",\n \\\"pr36494\\\":\\\"receipts/pr_36494.json\\\",\n \\\"pr36011c\\\":\\\"receipts/pr36011_commits.json\\\",\n \\\"c473\\\":\\\"receipts/commit_473f72e.json\\\",\n \\\"c820\\\":\\\"receipts/commit_820f247.json\\\",\n \\\"cc898\\\":\\\"receipts/commit_c898d34.json\\\",\n \\\"c2978\\\":\\\"receipts/commit_2978a92.json\\\",\n \\\"githist\\\":\\\"receipts/git_history.txt\\\",\n \\\"tags\\\":\\\"receipts/tags_versions.txt\\\",\n \\\"repo\\\":\\\"receipts/repo.json\\\",\n}\nH = {k:h(v) for k,v in R.items()}\n\nINTRO = \\\"37a22acf6f527870d4873c33c67a1078dc20f52b\\\"\nINTRO_PARENT = \\\"c94a8d6514936164ef869a6dda8bb7897b3958c2\\\"\nCLAUDETRAILER = \\\"Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\\\"\nFIX = \\\"c898d341c02299cf2506d0d8e84cc67953043593\\\"\nREPAIR = \\\"298fb8ce56099774f310594c9970cc24a9b8f900\\\"\nFIX_SHAS = [\\\"473f72e63a9777d793fbbf57194d8ec4fb97bc1b\\\",\n            \\\"820f247a6abba55cd87d130bef7bba7be3b29d37\\\",\n            \\\"b504a0eeb6f8a26daae3a2ebca12ccc2cacd3a2f\\\"]\n\nreport = f\\\"\\\"\\\"# Radar audit report — alias-3389297662643f29363dbb4b\n\nCase: \"'`alias-3389297662643f29363dbb4b` (task 9cbd6d3c50f480354060dc757232fb55412e401b640ad03a8456a68c99552e2a)\nAdvisories: GHSA-3cv6-jpf6-8222 (keyed) / CVE-2026-84377; OSV alias PYSEC-2026-4066\nRepository (advisory-side): `BerriAI/litellm` (pip package `litellm`)\nContract: audit protocol 2026-09-27.3; research contract `causal-work-v1`\nInvestigated: 2026-10-04, source coverage cutoff 2026-10-04T08:13:00Z (all evidence below is\nwithin the window except the tag/ref HTTP checks and the scratch clone, captured the same day).\nRole: auditor (independent attempt 1). Verdict: **AI_CODE_FLAWED**.\n\n## 1. Advisory status (Step 0)\n\nThe keyed advisory is **ACTIVE**. GitHub global REST `/advisories/GHSA-3cv6-jpf6-8222`\nreturned `published_at=2026-09-30T21:11:25Z`, `withdrawn_at=null` (explicit null => ACTIVE\nper DATA-SCHEMA), `type=reviewed`, severity medium, CVSS 3.1 `AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N` = 6.5,\nCWE-918. The repository advisory endpoint `/repos/BerriAI/litellm/security-advisories/GHSA-3cv6-jpf6-8222`\nreturns explicit `state=published` (=> ACTIVE), `published_at=2026-08-26T20:00:03Z`.\nNVD marks CVE-2026-84377 `Awaiting Analysis` with the same CVSS/CWE; OSV returns the same CVE with\nalias GHSA-3cv6-jpf6-8222 / PYSEC-2026-4066. No withdrawal, rejection or duplicate state anywhere;\nthe two identifiers are one vulnerability, and one is not used to refute the other.\n\n*Receipts:* `{R['\"'gh_global']}\"'` sha256 `{H['\"'gh_global']}\"'`; `{R['\"'gh_repo']}\"'` sha256 `{H['\"'gh_repo']}\"'`;\n`{R['\"'nvd']}\"'` sha256 `{H['\"'nvd']}\"'`; `{R['\"'osv']}\"'` sha256 `{H['\"'osv']}\"'`.\n\n## 2. Cause and activation (Step 1)\n\nAuthority: any **authenticated proxy user** (a valid virtual/API key) against the LiteLLM proxy.\nNo admin role is required for the curl-visible variants; the advisory'\"'s prerequisites are only\n\\\"authenticated LiteLLM proxy user\\\".\n\nMechanism. LiteLLM's proxy authenticates a request, then rebuilds the upstream call from the\nrequest body plus the operator's configured deployment. Request-parameter safety is enforced by\n\"'`is_request_body_safe()` in `litellm/proxy/auth/auth_utils.py`, which denies a fixed list of\nsensitive fields (`_BANNED_REQUEST_BODY_PARAMS`: `api_base`, `api_key`, Langfuse/Langsmith/\nobservability hosts, `s3_endpoint_url`, provider credential fields, ...) at the request-body root,\nwalked into a known nested-config list, and walked into `_NESTED_METADATA_KEYS`\n(`metadata` / `litellm_metadata`) — all gated by the admin opt-in\n`general_settings.allow_client_side_credentials` (`configurable_clientside_auth_params` per\ndeployment). A separate guard `_reject_url_valued_destinations()` rejects URL-valued\n`_URL_DESTINATION_REQUEST_FIELDS` unless the host is in `litellm.provider_url_destination_allowed_hosts`.\n\nThe defect the advisory describes is the union of several incomplete controls at the pre-fix\nrevision (parent of the fix):\n1. `is_request_body_safe` parsed the metadata fields as dict/JSON only. Multipart callers express\n   the same nesting as bracket keys (`litellm_metadata[api_base]=https://attacker`); those keys\n   were never inspected, so a banned destination/credential parameter reached the upstream call.\n2. The destination guard ran on the request-body fields but **not** on a deployment/model name\n   supplied via the URL path (`common_request_processing`, image endpoints), so a path-supplied\n   URL-valued `model` reached provider routing unchecked.\n3. `test_model_connection` in `litellm/proxy/health_endpoints/_health_endpoints.py` merged the\n   configured deployment parameters *under* the caller'\"'s request parameters\n   (\"'`litellm_params = {{**config_litellm_params, **request_litellm_params}}`), and did not drop\n   `litellm_credential_name`; a caller could therefore point a connection test at their own\n   endpoint while the operator'\"'s stored \"'`api_key`/credential reference was still merged in.\nEffect: outbound provider call to an attacker-chosen destination carrying the operator'\"'s\nconfigured provider credentials (SSRF + credential exfiltration), and requests to internal\nservices reachable from the proxy. This is consistent with \"'`is_request_body_safe`'\"'s own\nplaceholder-issue text and its documented history (the docstring names prior\n\"'`GHSA-jh89-88fc-qrfp`, `GHSA-3frq-6r6h-7j64` and a VERIA chain). No exploit was executed; the\nmechanism is established from source, tests and the fix diff.\n\nCounterevidence considered: the controls above are *incomplete* rather than absent, and\nlegitimate clientside override is intentionally supported through the two admin opt-ins. The\nfix therefore keeps the opt-ins (see §4) — the vulnerability is the default-closed gap, not the\nopt-in path itself.\n\n## 3. BIC / introduction and landing (Step 2)\n\n`introducer_sha = {INTRO}` (\"chore(proxy): close callback-config and observability-credential\nside channels\", 2026-05-03, ancestor of `main`). This change extended `is_request_body_safe` to\nwalk `metadata`/`litellm_metadata` (`_NESTED_METADATA_KEYS`) using the dict/JSON parser only. That\nis the exact control whose unhandled bracket-notation form (#1 above) is the residual bypass the\nadvisory fixes; the fix `5b2c92d7` adds the bracket-notation branch to this very loop.\n`bic_granularity = ATOMIC`: the object is one logically atomic control change, not a PR carrier.\n`introducer_parent = {INTRO_PARENT}`. `landing_basis = INTRODUCER_ON_DEFAULT`, `landing_commit = null`\n(the introducing object is itself reachable on the default branch; no distinct carrier object is asserted).\n\n`flaw_origin` (earlier human) is separate and is *not* the BIC: the clientside-credential /\nrequest-parameter boundary predates this change (`litellm/router_utils/clientside_credential_handler.py`\ntracing to Krish Dholakia 2025-03-03 `2fc6262675`), and the health-check credential-merge behavior\nappears in the health endpoint around 2025-12-09 (`0e2fcde145`, yuneng-jiang, \"Change health check to\nread env var like chat completions\"). Both are human-authored surfaces that the AI change extended\nwithout closing #1/#3.\n\nGranularity caveat: the advisory aggregates several mechanisms with separate origins (path-model\ncheck, health-check credential merge, `litellm_credential_name`). I bind the BIC to the\ndenylist/nested-metadata control because it is the change whose omission the advisory'\"'s own text\nnames (\\\"did not inspect parameters nested inside other request fields\\\"), and because the fix\npatches that control. The other two mechanisms are recorded as separate origins, not decomposed members.\n\n## 4. Fix and release (Step 3)\n\nFix: PR #36011 \\\"fix(proxy)\"'!: apply request-parameter checks consistently across body, path and form\ninputs\", merged to `litellm_internal_staging` as `{FIX}` (2026-08-05T23:23:17Z). The PR is six\ncommits; its tip member is `{REPAIR}`. It (a) adds the bracket-notation branch to `_NESTED_METADATA_KEYS`\nin `is_request_body_safe`, (b) applies `reject_url_valued_destination` to path-supplied `model` in\n`common_request_processing` and the image endpoint, and (c) makes the health connection test drop\nthe configured connection/credential fields (including `litellm_credential_name`) when the request\nsets its own connection, while preserving both admin opt-ins.\n`fix_granularity = CARRIER_BUNDLE`: `fix_sha = {FIX}` (first default-branch closure),\n`atomic_repair_sha = {REPAIR}` (repairing member), and `fix_shas` lists the verified other landings\n`{FIX_SHAS[0][:12]}` (stable/1.88.x), `{FIX_SHAS[1][:12]}` (stable/1.96.x), `{FIX_SHAS[2][:12]}` (== v1.88.6 tag).\n\nRelease membership:\n- Global advisory \"Patches\": fixed in 1.96.2, 1.95.1, 1.94.3, 1.93.2, 1.92.2, 1.91.5, 1.90.7, 1.89.7, 1.88.6;\n  repo advisory: \"Fixed in v1.96.2 with fixes back ported to 1.88.x\".\n- Verified: tag `v1.88.6` resolves to `b504a0eeb6f8a26daae3a2ebca12ccc2cacd3a2f`, which is exactly the\n  merge_commit of PR #36314 (the 1.88.x backport), so v1.88.6 *is* the backport merge (source-release\n  fact, not a label). `v1.96.2` -> `83d6d84b...`, `v1.95.1` -> `02dd853b...` (tag objects resolved via\n  first-party git refs). `v1.96.1` tag returns 404 even though PR #36494'\"'s body says it \\\"cuts 1.96.1\\\" —\n  the released patched version on that line is 1.96.2.\n- **Qualified**: the local blobless single-branch clone does not contain the 1.96.x/1.95.x tag lineage,\n  so tag-containment of \"'`820f247`/`83d6d84b` is asserted from tags+PR metadata, not re-verified by local\n  ancestry (see versions research-work route).\n\n## 5. AI participation (Step 4)\n\nThe introducing object `{INTRO}` is authored by `user <70670632+stuxf@users.noreply.github.com>` and its\ncommit message ends with the trailer:\n\n    {CLAUDETRAILER}\n\nSame trailer appears on the sibling hardening commits `0d4875dec978e7b458336e943b89c31efa92a4a2`,\n`90fd791e0d`, and `01323e8903`. The trailer is a first-party VCS marker on the BIC object itself\n(`ai_marker.sha == introducer_sha`), not copied from a fix. `ai_admissibility`: the trailer binds the\nchange to Claude Opus 4.7 for that commit'\"'s content; it establishes AI *participation* in the change,\nnot that every added line was AI-written, and it is admitted only for the causal change it actually\ncarries (the \"'`_NESTED_METADATA_KEYS` walk). The narrow defect is that this AI-assisted control parsed\none representation (dict/JSON) and omitted the bracket-notation representation of the same nesting —\na plausible AI-authored oversight, not an exogenous human edit of that hunk. Fix-side markers are\nabsent (author Yuneng Jiang, human), and fix-side AI never substitutes for BIC attribution.\n`ai_on_bic = true`, `ai_marker.state = PRESENT`.\n\n## 6. Versions (Step 5)\n\nAffected ranges (GitHub advisory): `< 1.88.6`; `>=1.89.0,<1.89.7`; `>=1.90.0,<1.90.7`;\n`>=1.91.0,<1.91.5`; `>=1.92.0,<1.92.2`; `>=1.93.0,<1.93.2`; `>=1.94.0,<1.94.3`;\n`>=1.95.0,<1.95.1`; `>=1.96.0,<1.96.2`. First patched: 1.88.6, 1.89.7, 1.90.7, 1.91.5, 1.92.2,\n1.93.2, 1.94.3, 1.95.1, 1.96.2. OSV additionally lists GIT fix commits b504a0ee, 83d6d84b,\n473f72e, 820f247, c898d341. OSV'\"'s \"'`introduced` anchor `2978a92bdb26db9676b3a265ee09078aa252e258`\nis a \"Add Claude Fable 5\" model-catalog commit (2026-06-10, Mateo Wang) unrelated to this\nmechanism — a tooling merge-base artifact, not the causal origin, and it is not an ancestor of the BIC.\n\n## 7. Residual, flip condition and limits\n\n`remaining_gap`: no known residual on the default branch for the three closed mechanisms; the\ncaller-visible behavior stays default-closed while the two documented admin opt-ins\n(`allow_client_side_credentials`, `configurable_clientside_auth_params`) intentionally re-open\nclientside override, which is by design and not a defect. The only qualified surface is\nbinary/tag containment on the 1.95.x/1.96.x lines, which I did not re-derive locally.\n\n`flip_condition`: the verdict would change if (a) the introducing `_NESTED_METADATA_KEYS` change were\nshown to be human-authored rather than carrying the Claude trailer (=> downgrade of `ai_on_bic`), or\n(b) the advisory were shown to target only the human health-check credential-merge surface without the\ndenylist/nested bypass (=> NOT_AI with the AI change as unrelated hardening), or (c) a further open\nbypass of these controls were demonstrated on a released line (=> residual / BLOCKED).\n\nLimits/failures: `git log -S` pickaxe over the blobless clone was abandoned (lazy-fetch blow-up); I\nused tree-only path history plus binary-searched blob reads and the GitHub API instead. Local Git\noperations hit a transient \"unable to create thread / pack-objects\" resource warning under the 8 GiB\nlimit; reads that mattered completed. No exploit was executed and no target was contacted.\n\n## 8. Gate runs\n\nSee `gate_runs.txt` (appended). Frozen gates: `audit_record_gates.py --strict` and `--strict --repo`.\n`check_research_work` on `research.json`. Results recorded verbatim there; failures qualified, not hidden.\n\"\"\"\nopen(\"REPORT.md\",\"w\").write(report)\nprint(\"REPORT.md bytes\", os.path.getsize(\"REPORT.md\"))\n\nrecord = {\n  \"class_id\": \"alias-3389297662643f29363dbb4b\",\n  \"verdict\": \"AI_CODE_FLAWED\",\n  \"advisory_ids\": [\"GHSA-3CV6-JPF6-8222\", \"CVE-2026-84377\"],\n  \"advisory_disposition\": \"ACTIVE\",\n  \"advisory_aliases\": [\n    {\"id\": \"GHSA-3CV6-JPF6-8222\", \"state\": \"ACTIVE\"},\n    {\"id\": \"CVE-2026-84377\", \"state\": \"ACTIVE\"}\n  ],\n  \"summary\": (\"Authenticated SSRF + provider-credential exfiltration through an incomplete request-parameter \"\n              \"control in the LiteLLM proxy: nested metadata supplied as bracket-notation form keys escaped \"\n              \"the banned-parameter check, path-supplied model names escaped the URL-destination guard, and \"\n              \"the health connection test merged configured credentials under caller-supplied connection \"\n              \"parameters.\"),\n  \"cause\": (\"An authenticated proxy user controls request parameters that are merged into the upstream call. \"\n            \"is_request_body_safe() denies sensitive fields at root and walks metadata/litellm_metadata, but \"\n            \"only parses them as dict/JSON; bracket-notation form keys (litellm_metadata[api_base]) bypass it. \"\n            \"The URL-valued destination guard is not applied to path-supplied model, and test_model_connection \"\n            \"merges configured credentials under caller connection params without dropping litellm_credential_name.\"),\n  \"mechanism\": (\"Attacker-controlled routing/credential parameter -> is_request_body_safe/denylist or destination \"\n                \"guard -> upstream LiteLLM provider call -> operator'\"'s stored provider credentials sent to the \\\"\n                \\\"attacker destination, and SSRF to internal services.\\\"),\n  \\\"bug_semantics\\\": (\\\"Denylist/representation mismatch (bracket-notation multipart metadata not inspected), a guard \\\"\n                    \\\