## 5. AI participation (Step 4)

The introducing object `37a22acf6f527870d4873c33c67a1078dc20f52b` is authored by `user <70670632+stuxf@users.noreply.github.com>` and its
commit message ends with the trailer:

    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

Same trailer appears on the sibling hardening commits `0d4875dec978e7b458336e943b89c31efa92a4a2`,
`90fd791e0d`, and `01323e8903`. The trailer is a first-party VCS marker on the BIC object itself
(`ai_marker.sha == introducer_sha`), not copied from a fix. `ai_admissibility`: the trailer binds the
change to Claude Opus 4.7 for that commit's content; it establishes AI *participation* in the change,
not that every added line was AI-written, and it is admitted only for the causal change it actually
carries (the `_NESTED_METADATA_KEYS` walk). The narrow defect is that this AI-assisted control parsed
one representation (dict/JSON) and omitted the bracket-notation representation of the same nesting —
a plausible AI-authored oversight, not an exogenous human edit of that hunk. Fix-side markers are
absent (author Yuneng Jiang, human), and fix-side AI never substitutes for BIC attribution.
`ai_on_bic = true`, `ai_marker.state = PRESENT`.

