{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/zsh -lc 'numactl --cpunodebind=1 --membind=1 cat sealed-auditors/auditor1/REPORT.md'","aggregated_output":"# Independent audit: GHSA-8w8g-wq8h-fq33\n\nTask `incremental-causal-20261004-case-043-auditor1`, independent attempt 1; class `alias-28f8ceffd7fbb625211260b2`. Recipient: `incremental-causal-20261004T081300Z-lead`. Research handoff only. Task binding: `9d0e605247e6f9712f04ef8d2e5ebdcd3a1b0c5c3a33e1233274dd0c4bb5ead4`. Protocol `2026-09-27.3`, research contract `causal-work-v1`.\n\n## Finding\n\n**BLOCKED on atomic introduction/provenance; supported vulnerability and released repair.** Dulwich's public Python `porcelain.checkout(..., paths=[...])` API writes regular blobs through filesystem symlinks in the affected versions. The keyed advisory is active. Its cited commit repairs the final-component case, but closure of its explicitly reported intermediate-directory case requires an additional repair. Both protections are in the 1.2.8 source distribution. No contribution-specific AI disclosure was found on the recovered origin, hardening or repair objects; this is not a finding that AI was absent.\n\nThe introducing path-checkout change is identifiable inside an LFS porcelain bundle, with immediate-parent and landing comparisons and identical causal content across two public histories. A smaller logically atomic introducing object was not recovered by the permitted bounded member probe. The protocol therefore calls for BLOCKED, rather than declaring that bundle atomic or treating its human signature as proof of human authorship of the causal hunk.\n\n## Evidence boundary and integrity\n\nThe manifest hash is `d2301acac03bb2736f18f79b08da0f7d66ef06cb2d2d3f5e2b900e62a3e705d2` and contains this one class. Retained source hash `a7f4859fcfb5d9488519e3d5e9e286f96a154a44be020490ecdc3badb9f082ac` agrees with SOURCES.json. See [input pins](evidence/input-pins.stdout), [retained advisory](evidence/retained-00-github.json), and [source inventory](evidence/retained-SOURCES.json).\n\nThe source coverage cutoff remains **2026-10-04T08:13:00Z**. New primary-source retrievals occurred after that cutoff, on 2026-10-04 starting at 16:54Z; their individual capture times are recorded in `evidence/*.receipt.json`. The decisive introducing objects date to July 2025, repair objects to June/July 2026, release publication to July 2026, and the retained advisory publication to October 2. These are later acquisitions of historically dated evidence, not evidence that these sources were captured in the frozen window. Current branch/ref metadata can postdate the cutoff; later product code is not used to establish the affected or fixed implementation. Public historical Git objects and July release bytes provide the comparisons. No other lane, prior judgment, host memory or production data was consulted.\n\nEach capture records its exact argument vector, time, repository, exit, stdout/stderr filenames and hashes. All shell invocations used `numactl --cpunodebind=1 --membind=1`; Git subprocesses inherited `GIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH=1` and `GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0`. The clone is owned under `output/scratch/dulwich`, non-shallow and not configured as a promisor. Source content was treated only as evidence. Helper scripts and derived observations are retained; they inspect source and archives without executing product code.\n\n## Advisory and actual mechanism\n\nThe retained global REST object identifies exactly `GHSA-8w8g-wq8h-fq33`, with `published_at=2026-10-02T18:53:35Z` and explicit `withdrawn_at=null`; under the frozen normalization this is ACTIVE. `cve_id` is null, and no second alias is inferred. It reports pip package dulwich `>=0.24.0, <=1.2.7`, patched 1.2.8. The advisory's description is a claim to test, not the causal conclusion.\n\nAt affected tag `dulwich-1.2.7` (`e076b16aa4ee77c18fbdb3d639cd037d76daee0b`), and at the cited fix's parent `f321a189f3f8fc94119556e30821925955af3ef7`, the public `checkout` function selects its path-specific branch when `paths is not None`. A caller supplies the paths and target tree/ref; strings become bytes, target defaults to HEAD, the tree resolves each path to a mode and object, and the object is required to be a Blob. It then calls `_checked_worktree_path`, creates leading directories, optionally applies configured smudge normalization, opens a regular-file path with `O_WRONLY|O_CREAT|O_TRUNC`, and writes the blob's bytes. Index staging occurs afterwards. No permission check or symlink check is interposed between the name validator and the open. Source: [affected porcelain](evidence/affected-porcelain.stdout), [source comparisons](evidence/source-comparisons.stdout), [cited repair](evidence/fix.stdout).\n\nThe trust boundary is attacker-controlled repository tree/blob content and an existing worktree symlink being consumed with the local caller's filesystem authority. The protected resource is any reachable writable target outside the intended worktree file, including repository administrative files. Both a final-component symlink and a symlink in an existing leading directory redirect the open; `makedirs(..., exist_ok=True)` does not validate such an existing directory. Name validation excludes literal absolute paths and invalid components, but cannot constrain a symlink's destination. The raw write bypasses `build_file_from_blob` completely. Configured filters may change the bytes; an ordinary non-LFS blob with no transforming filter suffices for the static causal argument.\n\nActivation requires a nonempty chosen path, a regular blob in the source tree and a preexisting usable symlink at that destination or a parent, plus target filesystem write permission. Repository-controlled symlinks can be materialized in symlink-capable worktrees; the affected `build_file_from_blob` has an `S_ISLNK` branch that creates the link from blob content. A configuration/platform that materializes links as ordinary files does not, by that action alone, establish this precondition. An empty `paths=[]` iterates no files and causes no write despite the shorthand in the advisory title. The public API is the exposed product operation; the inspected CLI `cmd_checkout` does **not** pass a paths argument, so this report does not claim that a plain Dulwich CLI checkout invokes this branch.\n\nThe security effect directly supported by source is file creation/truncation/overwrite under the caller's authority. `os.open`'s supplied mode applies to newly created files, subject to the umask; it does not unconditionally reset permissions on an existing target. Subsequent hook execution is a conditional secondary effect requiring an executable hook at the correct repository location and an operation that executes it. The advisory's illustrative relative symlink destination depends on repository layout; it is not universally the current repository's hook directory. These qualifications do not negate the write-through primitive. No malicious repository, hook, exploit payload or deployed target was exercised.\n\n## Introduction and integration\n\n`a83ac1e7e3258c0a663f229ddb9fb9007642b60e` (July 11, 2025, “Add LFS support to porcelain layer”) adds the `paths` argument, its target-defaulting branch, generic selected-file restoration and the raw `os.open` write. Its immediate parent is `1711d50fa57580741ab9239cbd31688bb034148e`: that checkout has no paths parameter and no corresponding raw-open branch. The equivalent original-history object is `2fd4b52d984c00822cf6baf54dd9a8f17257f721`, parent `dd12b736b05357454219b0aa651c70b786b08497`. Both children have tree `8b3734cdc26a7193d25625f3bc971e43f302b07f` and porcelain blob `8eda68697ca3833b0cce859f5f90667ca0a6056c`.\n\nThe current default-branch integration is `4b0718cf8a64f02111d4159a90df09c20c56d3c3`, first parent `a9444400f807fe7c9ce2219b401b9afc0a7c7129`. Its first-parent diff imports the same path branch; that parent lacks it. The exact extracted path branch SHA256 is `1f9ee206a8d693a34f9387e8422604d4e9483689513b6cb20abe80eee7115e98` in both introducing children and this integration. The GitHub PR metadata gives an older-history integration `815b59c1784f3c282cbf0466102c02b31a94840e`; the two integration objects have the same complete tree `6d8204e582e9c4beddc69e5fd5793194755adcbf`. We preserve both identities and do not conflate the API's historical merge SHA with the current default first-parent history. See [origin patch](evidence/path-introduction.stdout), [PR binding](evidence/original-pr.stdout), [landing history](evidence/landing-intro.stdout), and [parent/child and content checks](evidence/closure-verification.stdout).\n\nThe earlier May 2025 “Add dulwich.porcelain.checkout” object `e875e3125aadbefd7d580e02b2470193df997e28` lacks this path branch and is not its BIC. It is retained as a rejected candidate in [earlier checkout introduction](evidence/introduction.stdout).\n\nThe July porcelain member bundles several separable logical changes: generic path-selected checkout, LFS tracking/migration/fetch/push APIs, filter integration and import refactoring. Its generic checkout branch accepts ordinary non-LFS blobs and can be changed independently of those LFS APIs; four files or one parent would not establish atomicity. The authoritative PR member list and one bounded `fetch --no-tags origin refs/pull/1688/head:refs/research/pr1688` succeeded, but expose the same coarse member, not a smaller paths-only authoring object. All local commit objects were also enumerated and compared at both historical porcelain paths. No finer introducing object was recovered. The member search stops at this frozen boundary. [PR members](evidence/pr-members.stdout), [bounded fetch](evidence/member-probe.receipt.json), [object universe](evidence/all-object-universe.txt).\n\nAccordingly `introducer_sha` and `introducer_parent` are null, with `bic_granularity=AGGREGATE_MEMBERS_UNREACHABLE`, a located integration, and the known coarse origin recorded separately. The missing object is the logically independent path-checkout contribution within the July 11 LFS porcelain bundle, path `dulwich/porcelain.py`, first publicly integrated July 12, 2025. This is a granularity/provenance boundary, not a claim that the known bundle or its parent is unreadable.\n\n## AI participation and competing explanations\n\nBoth coarse introducing objects name Jelmer Vernooĳ `<jelmer@jelmer.uk>` and contain no AI marker. The PR member REST response also binds that identity to the `jelmer` account. The PR body describes LFS work; the captured PR discussion is empty. These are not contribution-specific AI disclosures. Contemporary `CONTRIBUTING.rst` states development conventions but no AI attribution convention. Nearby commit metadata and historical AI-guidance mentions demonstrate why repository-level AI activity cannot be transferred to this contribution. Attempts to read `CLAUDE.md` at each introducing object returned exit 128 (file absent); the tree inventory and actual CONTRIBUTING file supplied the follow-up observation. [Convention source](evidence/convention-guidance.stdout), [failed probes](evidence/convention.stderr), [original-history failure](evidence/convention-original.stderr), [discussion](evidence/pr-discussion.stdout).\n\nThe carrier inspection enumerated **43,183 objects, 12,641 commits**, including locally present commits outside ordinary branch reachability; it compared checkout bodies at `dulwich/porcelain.py` and `dulwich/porcelain/__init__.py`. **1,950** commits retain the specific raw-open hunk. Full metadata and path/blob identities are retained in [carrier metadata](evidence/carrier-metadata.json). Six objects carry the exact initial porcelain blob. Pattern hits on vulnerability carriers are dependency-bot messages, not causal AI coding disclosures; unrelated AI-guidance messages elsewhere in the repository do not bind this checkout hunk. This is a bounded inspection of the acquired object universe, not proof that unadvertised remote objects or private authoring history do not exist.\n\nLater name validation `5ebfa95818ba9a69378dc4654b7e4a8a5e38245b` adds lexical validation to checkout, restore and reset_file but leaves symlink traversal possible. Later leaf protection `fdf31e308c1cdbca813b470029ae09d64e09840e`, integrated by `d09f8af21d789255473904892bb198223912f7bd`, hardens only `build_file_from_blob`; checkout(paths) still does not call it. Neither inspected change carries an AI marker, and neither permits AI-attributed incomplete-remediation classification. The final repair objects likewise have no AI marker in their inspected metadata. [Name-guard diff](evidence/name-guard-diff.stdout), [helper repair comparison](evidence/closure-verification.stdout).\n\nHuman coding without disclosure, undisclosed AI assistance, and a coarser imported/assembled contribution remain distinguishable explanations. A named human bundle plus marker absence cannot settle hunk authorship. No `ai_on_bic:false`, NOT_AI or AI-positive verdict is asserted. The next decisive provenance observation is the original paths-only authoring diff/object or a first-party disclosure explicitly bound to that causal contribution; the leader owns routing if that evidence becomes available. No additional member fetch is requested within this frozen attempt.\n\n## Repair, closure and shipped scope\n\nThe repair chain is:\n\n1. `fdf31e308c1cdbca813b470029ae09d64e09840e` adds leaf-symlink removal to `build_file_from_blob`; default integration `d09f8af21d789255473904892bb198223912f7bd`. The helper uses `lstat`, removes a preexisting leaf symlink before reading/writing a regular file, and then creates the replacement. This alone leaves the advisory's raw-open caller untouched.\n2. Advisory-cited `9389fcb5cb9113adfc7f207d8be86a56904db3e8` routes checkout's regular-file writes through that helper and adds symlink-mode handling. It retains smudge conversion. Default integration `9995d0777b71ff2718ad87095f9916eab997378c` imports it relative to `f321a189f3f8fc94119556e30821925955af3ef7`. This closes the existing-leaf case, but its name validator still lets intermediate symlinks reach makedirs and the helper.\n3. `0d44c67e3a3af02031f49a9203a72df778672d70` reuses the stash leading-directory validator in `index.verify_leading_dirs` and invokes it from `_checked_worktree_path` before callers create directories or write. An empty safe-prefix cache is passed on each porcelain call. Each existing leading component is examined with `lstat`; a symlink raises `InvalidPathError`, converted to porcelain `Error`. That exception is not swallowed by checkout's object-lookup `KeyError` handling. The same validator reaches restore and reset_file, and the stash caller retains its guard after the helper move. This is the coherent final closing change for the advisory's directory case.\n\nThe first default-branch full closure is **`2ed5853e869a580eded4e6f63e02ed7555cf2705`**, integration of the last member, with first parent `cb1900d5696969beace7c85b742dffdc5d75a74d`. The first-parent diff shows the validator call being added to previously unguarded porcelain, not just later ancestry. Therefore `fix_sha` names this integration, `atomic_repair_sha` names `0d44c67e...`, and earlier prerequisite repairs are in `minimum_fix_set`, not mislabeled as other landings in `fix_shas`. See [directory repair](evidence/directory-closure.stdout) and [integration comparisons](evidence/closure-verification.stdout).\n\nThis closes the reported static/preexisting-symlink mechanism in the inspected source. It does not claim race-free resistance to a separate attacker concurrently mutating the worktree: path checks and subsequent opens are distinct filesystem operations. Nor does it claim every Dulwich operation or all platform alias/reparse semantics were audited. These are scope limits, not evidence that the reported fixed release is unpatched.\n\nAll available stable source tags from 0.24.0 through 1.2.7 retain the raw-open path branch; 0.23.2 has no paths argument. The tag scan records every SHA and the relevant source properties. 1.2.8 (`e5bc9a4604e1699509447bc09760850062382d8d`) has the helper routing plus leading-directory validation and leaf protection. The advisory's range is supported for this path-specific source mechanism. The complete semantic trace uses the boundary releases and repair parents; the intermediate tag scan checks persistence of the identified raw-open operation, rather than pretending to dynamically test every release.\n\nOfficial [release metadata](evidence/release-metadata.stdout) resolves the actual tag `dulwich-1.2.8`, marks it neither draft nor prerelease, records creation `2026-07-05T15:17:52Z` and publication `2026-07-05T15:28:36Z`, and describes both symlink protections. The PyPI sdist uploaded `2026-07-05T15:58:09.647501Z` is retained, not installed. Its SHA256 is **`dbf45651d6efd4696af1d9fca1a76d91135bbfcf4d50bc58410c60521899d5aa`**, matching official metadata. Archive members for porcelain, index, CLI and stash are byte-identical to the 1.2.8 Git tag. [Source distribution comparison](evidence/release-content.stdout), [PyPI metadata](evidence/pypi-fixed.stdout). This establishes published source-distribution content, not every platform wheel or a deployment.\n\n## Work status, validation and handoff\n\n[research.json](research.json) records all six dimensions. Advisory, scoped mechanism, repair and source-release findings are bound. Introduction and AI provenance are qualified boundaries after the recorded bounded primary-source inquiry. No lack of runtime exploit testing is presented as an unexplained evidence gap: such execution is prohibited and unnecessary for the source-bound overwrite conclusion. No product code or vulnerability regression payload was executed.\n\nExact strict structural and local-object gate results, sidecar validation, receipt integrity and report/record agreement are recorded in [VALIDATION.md](VALIDATION.md) and their raw receipts. The local gate has limited coverage when atomic introduction is null and AI markers are unknown/absent; independent parent/diff/content observations carry the causal evidence. A passing schema check does not accept this research.\n\nPreserved probe failures: two `git show <introducing-object>:CLAUDE.md` attempts exited 128 because that path is absent. The configuration grep exited 1 because no promisor/partial-clone configuration matched; this is a negative configuration observation, not a retrieval failure. No network denial, rate limit, timeout or exploit safety refusal occurred. Initial terminal display truncation did not truncate captured source receipts. Gate failures, if any, remain in the validation history rather than being erased by retries.\n\nFlip condition: a recovered logically atomic path-checkout contribution and its immediate parent, content-matched to the July bundle, could settle introduction/provenance; a contribution-specific AI disclosure could change attribution even without an on-object trailer if admissible under the contract. A counterexample in the retained released caller/validator chain, or different published source bytes, could change the bounded repair conclusion. No such counterexample was observed. Leader handoff is to assess the aggregate boundary and route any new provenance evidence; this sealed attempt does not authorize claims, ledger changes, publication or self-acceptance.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
