{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/zsh -lc \"numactl --cpunodebind=1 --membind=1 sed -n '28,57p' sealed-auditors/auditor1/REPORT.md\"","aggregated_output":"\nThe security effect directly supported by source is file creation/truncation/overwrite under the caller's authority. `os.open`'s supplied mode applies to newly created files, subject to the umask; it does not unconditionally reset permissions on an existing target. Subsequent hook execution is a conditional secondary effect requiring an executable hook at the correct repository location and an operation that executes it. The advisory's illustrative relative symlink destination depends on repository layout; it is not universally the current repository's hook directory. These qualifications do not negate the write-through primitive. No malicious repository, hook, exploit payload or deployed target was exercised.\n\n## Introduction and integration\n\n`a83ac1e7e3258c0a663f229ddb9fb9007642b60e` (July 11, 2025, “Add LFS support to porcelain layer”) adds the `paths` argument, its target-defaulting branch, generic selected-file restoration and the raw `os.open` write. Its immediate parent is `1711d50fa57580741ab9239cbd31688bb034148e`: that checkout has no paths parameter and no corresponding raw-open branch. The equivalent original-history object is `2fd4b52d984c00822cf6baf54dd9a8f17257f721`, parent `dd12b736b05357454219b0aa651c70b786b08497`. Both children have tree `8b3734cdc26a7193d25625f3bc971e43f302b07f` and porcelain blob `8eda68697ca3833b0cce859f5f90667ca0a6056c`.\n\nThe current default-branch integration is `4b0718cf8a64f02111d4159a90df09c20c56d3c3`, first parent `a9444400f807fe7c9ce2219b401b9afc0a7c7129`. Its first-parent diff imports the same path branch; that parent lacks it. The exact extracted path branch SHA256 is `1f9ee206a8d693a34f9387e8422604d4e9483689513b6cb20abe80eee7115e98` in both introducing children and this integration. The GitHub PR metadata gives an older-history integration `815b59c1784f3c282cbf0466102c02b31a94840e`; the two integration objects have the same complete tree `6d8204e582e9c4beddc69e5fd5793194755adcbf`. We preserve both identities and do not conflate the API's historical merge SHA with the current default first-parent history. See [origin patch](evidence/path-introduction.stdout), [PR binding](evidence/original-pr.stdout), [landing history](evidence/landing-intro.stdout), and [parent/child and content checks](evidence/closure-verification.stdout).\n\nThe earlier May 2025 “Add dulwich.porcelain.checkout” object `e875e3125aadbefd7d580e02b2470193df997e28` lacks this path branch and is not its BIC. It is retained as a rejected candidate in [earlier checkout introduction](evidence/introduction.stdout).\n\nThe July porcelain member bundles several separable logical changes: generic path-selected checkout, LFS tracking/migration/fetch/push APIs, filter integration and import refactoring. Its generic checkout branch accepts ordinary non-LFS blobs and can be changed independently of those LFS APIs; four files or one parent would not establish atomicity. The authoritative PR member list and one bounded `fetch --no-tags origin refs/pull/1688/head:refs/research/pr1688` succeeded, but expose the same coarse member, not a smaller paths-only authoring object. All local commit objects were also enumerated and compared at both historical porcelain paths. No finer introducing object was recovered. The member search stops at this frozen boundary. [PR members](evidence/pr-members.stdout), [bounded fetch](evidence/member-probe.receipt.json), [object universe](evidence/all-object-universe.txt).\n\nAccordingly `introducer_sha` and `introducer_parent` are null, with `bic_granularity=AGGREGATE_MEMBERS_UNREACHABLE`, a located integration, and the known coarse origin recorded separately. The missing object is the logically independent path-checkout contribution within the July 11 LFS porcelain bundle, path `dulwich/porcelain.py`, first publicly integrated July 12, 2025. This is a granularity/provenance boundary, not a claim that the known bundle or its parent is unreadable.\n\n## AI participation and competing explanations\n\nBoth coarse introducing objects name Jelmer Vernooĳ `<jelmer@jelmer.uk>` and contain no AI marker. The PR member REST response also binds that identity to the `jelmer` account. The PR body describes LFS work; the captured PR discussion is empty. These are not contribution-specific AI disclosures. Contemporary `CONTRIBUTING.rst` states development conventions but no AI attribution convention. Nearby commit metadata and historical AI-guidance mentions demonstrate why repository-level AI activity cannot be transferred to this contribution. Attempts to read `CLAUDE.md` at each introducing object returned exit 128 (file absent); the tree inventory and actual CONTRIBUTING file supplied the follow-up observation. [Convention source](evidence/convention-guidance.stdout), [failed probes](evidence/convention.stderr), [original-history failure](evidence/convention-original.stderr), [discussion](evidence/pr-discussion.stdout).\n\nThe carrier inspection enumerated **43,183 objects, 12,641 commits**, including locally present commits outside ordinary branch reachability; it compared checkout bodies at `dulwich/porcelain.py` and `dulwich/porcelain/__init__.py`. **1,950** commits retain the specific raw-open hunk. Full metadata and path/blob identities are retained in [carrier metadata](evidence/carrier-metadata.json). Six objects carry the exact initial porcelain blob. Pattern hits on vulnerability carriers are dependency-bot messages, not causal AI coding disclosures; unrelated AI-guidance messages elsewhere in the repository do not bind this checkout hunk. This is a bounded inspection of the acquired object universe, not proof that unadvertised remote objects or private authoring history do not exist.\n\nLater name validation `5ebfa95818ba9a69378dc4654b7e4a8a5e38245b` adds lexical validation to checkout, restore and reset_file but leaves symlink traversal possible. Later leaf protection `fdf31e308c1cdbca813b470029ae09d64e09840e`, integrated by `d09f8af21d789255473904892bb198223912f7bd`, hardens only `build_file_from_blob`; checkout(paths) still does not call it. Neither inspected change carries an AI marker, and neither permits AI-attributed incomplete-remediation classification. The final repair objects likewise have no AI marker in their inspected metadata. [Name-guard diff](evidence/name-guard-diff.stdout), [helper repair comparison](evidence/closure-verification.stdout).\n\nHuman coding without disclosure, undisclosed AI assistance, and a coarser imported/assembled contribution remain distinguishable explanations. A named human bundle plus marker absence cannot settle hunk authorship. No `ai_on_bic:false`, NOT_AI or AI-positive verdict is asserted. The next decisive provenance observation is the original paths-only authoring diff/object or a first-party disclosure explicitly bound to that causal contribution; the leader owns routing if that evidence becomes available. No additional member fetch is requested within this frozen attempt.\n\n## Repair, closure and shipped scope\n\nThe repair chain is:\n\n1. `fdf31e308c1cdbca813b470029ae09d64e09840e` adds leaf-symlink removal to `build_file_from_blob`; default integration `d09f8af21d789255473904892bb198223912f7bd`. The helper uses `lstat`, removes a preexisting leaf symlink before reading/writing a regular file, and then creates the replacement. This alone leaves the advisory's raw-open caller untouched.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
