adds the causal exposure path of a\nReconcile recovered original fetch member versus squash, real default selection versus explicit Node fetch selection, actual maxRedirects-zero expectation and recipient prerequisites. Inspect Request and no-Request branches and second init override at repair; positive finite redirect-count parity is a distinct question. Auditor2 inspected temporary /tmp/axfull Git which may not survive sandbox; preserve absence truthfully and use available retained primary objects, never fabricate a clone check. Reconcile v1.7.0 versus v1.10.0 source introduction and actual package bytes. No redirect server, network test, exploit, deployed target or installation of missing runtime to execute attack regressions. Existing runtime failure remains visible.\n# Independent audit: alias-2a1ef1a8d7e0ad48955683d1\n\nTask: incremental-causal-20261004-case-044-auditor1, independent attempt 1.\nBinding: f08131287b1e2a1cc56d8d1630a5f93158358fa80b0b88efb2de4403fc86e44a.\nRecipient: incremental-causal-20261004T081300Z-lead. Research handoff only.\nContract: 2026-09-27.3; research sidecar: causal-work-v1.\n\n## Finding\n\n**NOT_AI under the frozen evidentiary threshold.** The conditional redirect-policy bypass is supported by product source, its real adapter dispatch, and affected npm package content. The introducing fetch feature is a recovered, named human contribution from April 2024 with no AI marker on its introducing object or matching original content carriers. Contemporary metadata contains no observed AI disclosure convention. This is a bounded attribution finding, not proof that undisclosed assistance never occurred.\n\nThe advisory's lower bound of 1.17.0 is too late for the source mechanism: the same omission is already present in the stable v1.7.0 source release. The advertised repair in v1.20.0 is supported both in tagged source and the actual npm Node bundle. This research did not run an exploit, a redirect server, or any target request.\n\n## Advisory and coverage\n\nThe keyed CVE-2026-101907 is PUBLISHED in the matching CVE record (`evidence/cve.raw`, cveMetadata). GHSA-r4gj-5m52-g5wh is ACTIVE: the retained matching global advisory has published_at 2026-09-30T15:31:58Z and explicit withdrawn_at null. The NVD record says Awaiting Analysis; this is not rejection. The two identifiers explicitly reference the same issue. No alias is excluded.\n\nThe retained inputs' fixed coverage ends at 2026-10-04T08:13:00Z. Their SHA256 values match SOURCES.json. Manifest SHA256 matches aacd761b6682da87403dea97a9df8aa0f509d1db4b1d819aac36d6385dd5f6bd. Fresh Git, API, and registry observations were captured later on October 4, as recorded individually. They are additional decisive evidence acquired after that window, not part of the original capture coverage. The implicated commits and releases have historical dates preceding the cutoff; a later capture does not prove that identical API metadata was visible at the cutoff.\n\n## Mechanism, authority, and contrary explanations\n\nAt v1.18.1 the application supplies an Axios request config. `lib/core/dispatchRequest.js` resolves `config.adapter || defaults.adapter`, invokes that adapter, transforms its response data, and returns the response to the application. `lib/adapters/adapters.js` resolves the known fetch adapter and its environment support; defaults prefer xhr, then http, then fetch. Explicit `adapter: 'fetch'` selects this path in a supporting Node runtime. Ordinary Node default HTTP use is not affected. Evidence: `dispatch.raw` contains the dispatch, selector, defaults, resolveConfig and mergeConfig files from this exact tag, not another mode or revision.\n\n`maxRedirects` survives config merging, but the affected `lib/adapters/fetch.js` does not extract or consume it. Its request options copy fetchOptions and set signal, method, headers, body, duplex, and credentials without setting redirect. The normal Request path calls `_fetch(request, fetchOptions)`; the no-Request path calls `_fetch(url, resolvedOptions)`. With no caller override, standard Fetch redirect behavior is follow. After the request completes, the adapter reads the response body with its resolver and supplies `data`, status, headers and config to settle; dispatch returns that response to the application. The URL's origin has no per-redirect Axios authorization check on this path. See `parent-fetch.raw` and `release-118.raw` (identical fetch source), especially config destructuring and the resolvedOptions/_fetch block.\n\nAn attacker needs control over the initial URL or a redirect response from an allowed source; the application must rely on numeric `maxRedirects: 0` and use the fetch adapter without equivalent runtime redirect protection. A compliant underlying fetch can then access a redirected resource with the application's network reachability. Disclosure to the attacker additionally requires the application to expose the returned data; a state change additionally requires a reachable target that acts on the resulting request. These deployment conditions are not demonstrated against a real service. Browser CORS/opaque responses, environment egress restrictions, redirect method rewriting, and target authorization can limit effects. This is not unconditional SSRF, arbitrary credential theft, or proof of a deployed compromise.\n\nCounterevidence has been retained and scoped. README describes maxRedirects as a Node option, and the original fetch feature aimed at XHR parity. That narrows expectations and excludes an assertion that every browser request has this guard. It does not refute the actual Node fetch request's silent bypass when an application trusts the option. `http.raw` selects native HTTP/HTTPS when maxRedirects === 0, avoiding follow-redirects; a 3xx may then reject under default status validation. The security distinction is absence of following, not that every HTTP request necessarily throws. Explicit fetchOptions.redirect manual/error is an existing mitigation. Positive finite redirect-count parity is a separate question: this repair enforces numeric zero, not an arbitrary positive count.\n\nStatic source and package inspection support these findings. The retained WHATWG Fetch standard (`fetch-standard.raw`, concept-request-redirect-mode) supplies the normative default-follow and manual-mode semantics. Upstream tests are retained but were not executed or adopted as local test results. No local runtime behavior is claimed.\n\n## Introduction and content identity\n\nThe causal introducing member is **1e5552ac0e3293b666c1b87cdebac7c455f93270**, parent **72c888c6b3d1be3c11932c81afa9f5418871357b**, in axios/axios. Its metadata identifies DigitalBrainJS <robotshara@gmail.com>. The landed feature is **a3ff99b59d8ec2ab5dd049e68c043617a4072e42**, whose public metadata gives Dmitriy Mozgovoy with that same address. Granularity is SQUASH_DECOMPOSED; the landing and member are distinct.\n\nThe member's parent has http/xhr adapters but no fetch.js. The child adds the fetch adapter, registration/default fallback, and supporting signal, progress, stream, type and tests work. This is a coherent fetch feature, not an inference of atomicity from its file count. The newly reachable request constructor omits any maxRedirects mapping and passes its Request to fetch. Under the same explicit fetch selection, the parent has no such adapter; the child exposes the bypass. See `intro-parent.raw`, `intro-parent-tree.raw`, `intro-member.raw`, `intro-fetch.raw` and `intro-pr.raw`.\n\nThe bounded PR-head fetch recovered PR 6371 members. `intro-content-identity.raw` compares the member and landing at fetch.js, adapters.js and defaults/index.js. The request construction/fetch call and omission are unchanged; the few fetch-file differences concern progress scheduling, capability detection and FormData headers. Thus content identity of the causal hunk, rather than PR title or membership alone, binds the member to the landing. Full fetch blobs differ; this report does not claim whole-file identity. The clone is non-shallow (`git-state.raw`), and actual trees/blobs were read. The parent contains the same tree as the default-branch pre-landing parent, strengthening the same-conditions comparison.\n\n`history-fetch.raw` traces the feature back to the 2024 addition. `redirect-history.raw` finds the first later maxRedirects read in the 2026 repair. It does not independently prove marker absence; object coverage is described separately below.\n\n## AI participation and competing causal changes\n\nThe BIC's full object has a named account/address and no AI identity, trailer, or disclosure. The source identity is bound through the original PR and landed author metadata, not guessed from a display name. `convention.raw` reads 15 preceding default-branch commit messages around the original contribution epoch; no AI disclosure convention is observed there (CONVENTION_NOT_OBSERVED). `intro-members-metadata.raw` also retains the original feature iterations. The empty captured original issue-comments response provides no additional attribution, rather than affirmative proof of human-only work.\n\nThe local-object survey used `git cat-file --batch-all-objects --batch-check` after both bounded PR-ref recoveries, not only reachable-history pickaxe. It enumerated **2,382 commit objects**, inspected historical `lib/adapters/fetch.js` at every commit (including objects regardless of reachability), and retained **44 unique source blobs**, all path mappings, and full metadata for **650** objects whose fetch source contains no maxRedirects reference. This intentionally broad superset includes unchanged descendants. The source path begins with the added fetch.js; --follow history shows no earlier rename to omit. Evidence: `carrier-object-universe.txt`, `carrier-path-map.json`, `carrier-metadata.json`, `fetch-blob-*.txt`, `carrier-scan.raw`, and the sealed scanner source. No all-public-object completeness is implied by this local universe.\n\nTwenty broad AI-term metadata hits were inspected with full metadata and changed-file summaries (`marker-*.raw`). Many are documentation, tests, dependency, or unrelated code changes inheriting the fetch blob. A marker on a descendant does not attribute inherited 2024 code. Two relevant competing explanations received further attention:\n\n* **6bb12c191f5380fad321322fb90216ae0dc36985** discloses Claude Sonnet 4.6/cubic assistance and adds custom sensitive-header stripping for Node HTTP redirects. Its full diff explicitly scopes that feature to the HTTP adapter and states it is unused with maxRedirects zero. It neither alters fetch.js nor promises a fetch zero-redirect guard. Under the same valid URL, fetch selection, and numeric zero, behavior is unchanged. It is not incomplete remediation of this fetch mechanism (`redirect-auth-hardening.raw`).\n* The April 2026 fetch hardening members **e5540dcafe423909527c0af097fb6ae7f68c08f3** and **d8165e9f2c4a73176a372d5d84f43c5fd166026b** add content/body size accounting, not redirect authorization. Their intended invariant and omitted redirect handling are different. Their full relevant diffs and metadata are retained (`later-hardening.raw`). The parseProtocol AI-marked fix rejects malformed scheme syntax and does not change handling of a valid redirect source; FormData/header and cookie changes likewise do not introduce this plain request path.\n\nRepair-side bot reviews are real, but are not defect-side attribution: `repair-comments.raw` and `repair-reviews.raw` show Greptile/cubic reviews bound to repair PR revisions. BIC and repair commit metadata were read separately. The repair commits themselves have no AI marker; their code-generation provenance beyond that remains undisclosed. The later removal of an AI-marker policy (`source-policy-change.raw`, August 2026) cannot attribute or exonerate the April 2024 feature. No fix marker is copied into the BIC.\n\n## Repair, integration, and shipment\n\nFirst default-branch closure is **d19040bda7a8be2f82c3c6e1a5bc03917daee39a**, immediate default-branch parent **e0a02dd16671deabe2b809334d4c2ebede29a233**. The official repository default branch is v1.x (`repo-metadata.raw`); its first-parent history contains the landing (`mainline.raw`). This is a runtime-hardening bundle, not an atomic redirect-only repair.\n\nRecovered PR 11141 member **745160bc8a3cadf4180d0abccaf87aed8555485e**, parent **2c94646eb7cb7ab9dcb2aefdb04ab1b040c28e16**, first reads maxRedirects and sets resolvedOptions.redirect = 'manual' for numeric zero before constructing Request or calling the no-Request path. It also removes the second init argument on the Request path. Member **4bee948a922627f281d7c6ca8655245f38998630** restores sanitized second options and sets their redirect to manual as well, preventing that second init from overriding the already-resolved Request. The final fetch source in this member exactly equals the landed fetch source (`repair-members-diff.raw`, empty successful diff). `repair-member-history.raw` retains both causal diffs. Neither member is a redirect-only logical unit; therefore atomic_repair_sha is null rather than pretending a standalone minimal commit was recovered. The repair is fully identified at hunk granularity within these two members, and fix_granularity is CARRIER_BUNDLE.\n\nThe default parent omits maxRedirects; the landing applies the guard on both live invocation branches. Manual overrides fetchOptions.redirect even if the caller supplied follow. The safeFetchOptions copy preserves unrelated options while preventing a second init from reversing the guard. When Request is absent, resolvedOptions supplies manual directly. Compliant custom Fetch implementations receive the authoritative options; a malicious or noncompliant custom implementation is outside this guarantee. Browser manual responses may be opaque (status zero) rather than inspectable Node 3xx responses. The guard acts before the network operation; response size guards after fetching are not its security justification.\n\nOfficial release metadata resolves v1.20.0 and says it was published **2026-08-24T13:40:40Z** (created August 19); it is a stable release. The source tag contains the guarded code (`release-fetch.raw`). v1.19.0, published July 26, retains the omission. v1.7.0, published May 19, 2024, already contains the fetch adapter with the omission; v1.6.8 has no fetch adapter (`early-release.raw`, `pre-fetch-tree.raw`, official release metadata). The observed source history supports introduction before the advisory's >=1.17.0 range; the report does not blindly repeat that lower bound.\n\nActual npm 1.19.0 and 1.20.0 tarballs were obtained from each exact registry version's dist.tarball URL. Both SHA512 integrity values match registry metadata. Read-only archive member inspection shows each packaged fetch.js exactly equals its corresponding Git tag and the packaged Node CJS bundle has the same before/after behavior. See `package-content-check.raw`, `npm-*.raw`, and `package-*.js`/`package-*.cjs`. This is archive member reading, not a Git object read or execution. This verifies npm source plus Node CJS shipment; no claim is made about every CDN build, browser bundle, or deployed installation.\n\n## Limits, failures, and handoff\n\nNo decisive question remains open within the stated source, causal, attribution-threshold and npm shipment scope. Unknown undisclosed AI assistance is not evidence of absence in an absolute sense. A contribution-specific disclosure tied by content to this feature, a later AI change proven to create this same exposure, an earlier causal source, or contradictory shipped code would change this finding. The leader/reducer owns any such expanded investigation and acceptance; this submission is not self-acceptance.\n\nFailures are preserved. The first test source lookup used obsolete singular `test/unit/adapters/fetch.js` and exited 128; the corrected `tests/unit/adapters/fetch.test.js` read succeeded. Tagged Git trees do not contain dist/node/axios.cjs (both lookups exit 128); actual npm archives resolved that shipment question. A proposed ordinary, network-free Request-default regression did not start because `node` is absent: the initial capture helper raised FileNotFoundError; a corrected receipt records node --version exit 127, and runtime discovery finds no node/nodejs/bun/deno/qjs. No test passed is claimed. Missing root cgroup memory.max/memory.high paths were observed during environment inspection; they are not source denials. Node-1 affinity was used throughout. Display truncation of large terminal reads was addressed with focused source reads; retained raw evidence is complete.\n\n`research.json` records all six contract dimensions, evidence hashes and decisive route outcomes. The optional runtime-check failure is recorded separately as an execution limitation, not an unanswered decisive source question or an exhausted source route. That testing limitation does not erase the bound static mechanism finding. `record.json` is derived after this report. Strict record and local-object gates, research-sidecar/hash validation and readback results are retained in the case checks and evidence receipts. SEAL.json hashes all stable case files except itself; runtime-home and runner-final are outside this payload. No claims, database, canonical, or deployment writes were made.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
