{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/zsh -lc \"numactl --cpunodebind=1 --membind=1 sed -n '36,115p' sealed-auditors/auditor2/REPORT.md\"","aggregated_output":"(`evidence/receipt_omission_history.txt`). So the omission is real and long-lived.\n\nCounterpoint / scope: this is not unconditional SSRF. It requires (a) a caller that trusts\n`maxRedirects: 0` as a redirect guard, (b) the fetch adapter (explicit `adapter:'fetch'` or a\nruntime where adapter resolution selects fetch), and (c) an attacker-influenced redirect\nsource (attacker server, open redirect). The HTTP adapter is not affected: it selects the\nnative `http`/`https` transport when `own('maxRedirects') === 0` (present since\n`a04077a1`, 2016) so no redirect is followed. The advisory itself states this qualification.\n\n## 2. BIC — introduction and exposure (Step 2)\n\n- `introducer_sha`: `a3ff99b59d8ec2ab5dd049e68c043617a4072e42` — `feat(adapter): add fetch\n  adapter; (#6371)`, 2024-04-28, one parent `751133eb9ed794c6f6634c52f4fe116e33bf5f09`.\n- `bic_granularity`: **ATOMIC**. The introduced content is the new `lib/adapters/fetch.js`\n  plus its registration (`knownAdapters.fetch`, default `adapter: ['xhr','http','fetch']`).\n  The parent has no `lib/adapters/fetch.js` and `defaults.adapter` is `['xhr','http']`, so the\n  parent/child comparison under identical conditions shows the redirect-following-but-unconstrained\n  code path entering at this object. It is a coherent single feature, not a separable bundle.\n- `landing_commit`: `null`; `landing_basis`: `INTRODUCER_ON_DEFAULT`. `a3ff99b5` is itself the\n  squash-merged object on the default branch (author Dmitriy Mozgovoy, committer\n  `GitHub <noreply@github.com>`, single parent), so no distinct carrier object exists.\n\nAdvisory-range vs. origin (contrary-evidence check): the published range is `>= 1.17.0`. Direct\nevidence contradicts 1.17.0 as the *origin*: the omission is already present at `v1.16.1` and at\nthe fetch adapter's own introduction `v1.10.0`, and no fetch-adapter redirect change exists at\n1.17.0. Files touched between `v1.16.1..v1.17.0` in the adapter/config path are unrelated\n(basic-auth #10896, error-handling cleanup #10922, RN FormData #10898, resolveConfig export\n#10891). The advisory's lower bound is therefore conservative/approximate (the reported/test\nversion), not the causal introduction; it does not change the verdict. Retained as a qualified\nfinding rather than a closed exclusion of any earlier evidence.\n\n## 3. Repair and release (Step 3)\n\n- `fix_sha`: `d19040bda7a8be2f82c3c6e1a5bc03917daee39a` — `fix: harden runtime option\n  handling (#11141)`, 2026-08-12; first default-branch mechanism-closing change.\n- `fix_granularity`: **CARRIER_BUNDLE**. #11141 is carrier-shaped (23 files; prototype-pollution\n  hardening, HTTP/2, proxy CIDR, data-URI on top of the fetch change), so the object is not\n  atomic. `atomic_repair_sha` names the same object `d19040bd`, because the redirect repair was\n  squash-collapsed into the carrier and no separate member commit carries the fix content\n  (`git log -S maxRedirects -- lib/adapters/fetch.js` = only `d19040bd`).\n- Mechanism closure: the commit destructures `maxRedirects` from `resolveConfig`, and adds\n  `if (maxRedirects === 0) { resolvedOptions.redirect = 'manual'; }` (also applied to the second\n  `fetchOptions` argument), so the runtime no longer follows redirects when the caller set 0.\n- Shipment: `v1.20.0` is the first release containing `d19040bd` (`git tag --contains`), and the\n  official v1.20.0 release notes list \"#11141\" under Security Fixes. This matches the advisory's\n  `first_patched_version: 1.20.0`. Publication (2026-08-24) is distinguished from the commit date\n  (2026-08-12).\n- `remaining_gap`: none for the reported mechanism. Later commits\n  (`f8df2e24` #11194, `56a5f1ac` #11179, `2498c233` #11242) refine the same area but are not\n  required to close the redirect mechanism.\n\n## 4. AI participation (Step 4)\n\nApplied to the BIC object, not the fix.\n\n- `a3ff99b5` carries a recovered named human identity: `Dmitriy Mozgovoy <robotshara@gmail.com>`;\n  committer `GitHub <noreply@github.com>` (squash). `identity_class = human`.\n- Marker absence on the causal object: the commit body is empty; no AI co-author trailer, no\n  `[AI]`, no tool disclosure. A scan of all local commit messages touching `lib/adapters/fetch.js`\n  returns only human `Co-authored-by` trailers (Jason Saayman, cyphercodes, Mostafa-Khairy0,\n  Ofek Danny) — no AI tool identity.\n- Carrier coverage: `git log --all --find-object=cf1c6802...` (the introduced blob) selects\n  `a3ff99b5` (adds it) and `d1d359da` (#6377, removes/replaces it); `d1d359da` is likewise a\n  human-authored squash (`Dmitriy Mozgovoy`) with an empty body and no AI marker. 2,332 local\n  commit objects exist. One bounded PR-ref probe fetched `refs/pull/6371/head` and\n  `refs/pull/6377/head`: the PR #6371 branch tip carries the landed blob, its member commit\n  `1e5552ac` (`DigitalBrainJS <robotshara@gmail.com>`, self-committed, empty body) also has no\n  marker, and a metadata scan of every unique branch commit found no AI marker. A scan of all\n  refs for commits touching `lib/adapters/fetch.js` finds no AI tool identity\n  (`evidence/receipt_pr_probe.txt`).\n- Disclosure-convention spot check at the BIC date (2024-04-28): tree contains no `AGENTS.md`\n  and no `.github/copilot-instructions.md` (those appear only in 2026: `90ae1993`, `3f83143b`).\n  `disclosure_convention = CONVENTION_NOT_OBSERVED` for that date.\n- Fix side (informational): `d19040bd` author `Jay <jasonsaayman@gmail.com>`, committer GitHub,\n  empty body, no AI marker → `fix_ai_marker ABSENT`. This is not used for BIC attribution.\n\n## 5. Versions\n\n- Affected (advisory): npm `axios >= 1.17.0, < 1.20.0`; first patched `1.20.0`.\n- Verified introduction in source: fetch adapter exists from `v1.10.0` with the omission; the\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
