{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/zsh -lc \"numactl --cpunodebind=1 --membind=1 sh -c 'sed -n \\\"1,80p\\\" contract/docs/AUDIT-PROTOCOL.md; sed -n \\\"95,137p\\\" contract/docs/DATA-SCHEMA.md; sed -n \\\"105,170p\\\" contract/scripts/README.md; cat retained-git/auditor1/SOURCES.json | head -70; cat retained-git/auditor2/SOURCES.json | head -60; cat contract/AGENTS.md'\"","aggregated_output":"# Audit protocol\n\nContract version: `2026-09-27.3`. Existing batches retain their frozen contracts.\n\nN auditors independently research the same cases. Each auditor completes every step\nbelow; do not divide the research chain between auditors. Work in a fresh context per\ncase and save your findings before reading any other auditor's report or prior verdict.\nUse neutral primary sources, not another agent's conclusions. Retain every case.\n\nFor each step, state **what you conclude, what you observed, and where the evidence is**.\nIf a decisive fact is missing after the bounded probe, name it and the next question;\nnever fill a field by guessing. Execution failure is not a researched EVIDENCE_GAP.\n\n[Data fields](DATA-SCHEMA.md#audit-records) define the output.\nThe local [research-work contract](DATA-SCHEMA.md#research-work-contract) separates\nsupported conclusions from unfinished investigation; record it in the owned sidecar.\n[Audit commands](../scripts/README.md) cover claiming, dispatch and submission.\nOne adjudicator subsequently reviews all reports and completes unresolved research\nunder [ADJUDICATION-PROTOCOL](ADJUDICATION-PROTOCOL.md).\n\n## Research steps\n\n0. **Advisory status:** read the advisory record itself (CVE JSON in cveawg, OSV, GHSA)\n   before the mechanism. State governs: a `REJECTED`, withdrawn or duplicate advisory is\n   `FALSE_POSITIVE` and gets no mechanism work, and a live advisory whose own text\n   contradicts its label is not thereby dead. A dead alias does not reject a live keyed\n   advisory, and two identifiers referring to one vulnerability are not by themselves a\n   false positive. A refuted mechanism or wrong-product report needs affirmative primary\n   evidence and `false_positive_ground`; inability to establish the mechanism is\n   `EVIDENCE_GAP`, never proof of `FALSE_POSITIVE`.\n   For a supported FALSE_POSITIVE, causal introducer/repair fields are explicitly\n   null (not applicable), as defined by DATA-SCHEMA. Preserve examined objects in\n   evidence without assigning them vulnerability roles. An excluded advisory's\n   disposition does not change the verdict of its broader live alias cluster.\n\n1. **Cause:** trigger → vulnerable code → security impact, with preconditions and\n   counterevidence. Separate distinct mechanisms.\n   For resource-exhaustion claims, trace resource accounting from the controlled input\n   through allocation or acquisition to release or reuse. Distinguish requested and\n   returned lengths from allocated or retained capacity; inspect active limits,\n   allocation tiers or pool classes and lifetimes at the affected revision. Bound\n   per-operation and repeated-operation costs against legitimate use, without\n   inventing runtime measurements.\n\n2. **BIC:** the smallest logically atomic introducing change, which is not always the commit\n   it landed in. `introducer_sha` is the atomic change, `landing_commit` the merge/squash\n   object that carried it; compare its immediate parent, trace moves, and separate\n   introduction from later exposure. Declare `bic_granularity`:\n\n   - `ATOMIC`: the object is a logically atomic causal change, not its own `landing_commit`.\n     One parent, an initial commit or a bulk-import label does not establish atomicity.\n     A root tree can be the earliest observable boundary: identify the causal path/hunk and\n     explain minimality in `landing_note`. Do not infer pre-import origin or authorship\n     from that boundary. A known aggregate follows the next two rules.\n   - `SQUASH_DECOMPOSED`: members reconstructable; list their 40-hex shas.\n   - `AGGREGATE_MEMBERS_UNREACHABLE`: members still unavailable after one bounded PR-ref\n     fetch. That probe is the terminus, not the start of a hunt: name the unreachable object\n     (subject, path, epoch, first public appearance) and what would flip the read, then land\n     the row as `BLOCKED`. For an import without a PR, record the authoritative source-history\n     probe and its limit. Push counts and missing public events alone cannot establish\n     content identity, atomicity or the absence of earlier history.\n   - `NON_GIT_BOUNDARY`: the change predates VCS, so `introducer_sha` is null.\n\n   Membership is located by content identity (`--find-object`, pickaxe on the introduced\n   blob), never by PR number or title: the object that landed the content is a carrier, not a\n   member, and a member whose added lines the landing tree does not carry is a candidate from\n   another epoch. An aggregate's trailers, co-authors and badges belong to the aggregate, not\n   to a hunk: they are never BIC attribution. If history is unavailable, isolate the causal\n   diff and say what that costs commit-level minimality.\n\n   Compare behavior in the immediate parent and child, not just the first pickaxe hit.\n   Check shallow boundaries before treating a root or a failed ancestry walk as evidence.\n   Missing local objects are not proof that public history is unavailable: distinguish a\n   commit, its trees/blobs and the product repository from a reporter or umbrella clone.\n   Recover missing objects through one bounded authoritative probe in an owned scratch\n   clone, preserving shared read-only clones; retain its command and output. If the BIC\n   itself is unknown, use the explicit nulls allowed for open records by DATA-SCHEMA.\n\n3. **Fix:** the minimum repairing hunks/commit set and why it closes the defect; follow\n   partial repairs through the residual defect to closure; verify affected/fixed versions\n  official source format, not a universal requirement for a literal `state` field.\n  For GitHub REST global advisories (`/advisories/{ghsa_id}`, or the matching\n  item of `/advisories`), require the matching `ghsa_id`, a valid non-null\n  `published_at`, and a present `withdrawn_at`: explicit null maps to `ACTIVE`,\n  a valid withdrawal timestamp to `WITHDRAWN`. These are date-time fields;\n  absent, empty, boolean or malformed values are not substitutes. This format\n  has no `state` property; null withdrawal alone does not establish publication.\n  Repository advisories (`/repos/{owner}/{repo}/security-advisories/...`) use\n  their explicit `state`: `published` maps to `ACTIVE`, `withdrawn` to\n  `WITHDRAWN`. `draft`, `triage` and `closed` stay `UNKNOWN` in this vocabulary;\n  retain the raw state and do not infer rejection, duplication or withdrawal.\n  Repository timestamps permit null; do not impose the global publication\n  rule on that format or infer its state from timestamps alone.\n  Match the exact advisory identifier and endpoint; a shared CVE, repository\n  or reference URL is not sufficient. Do not transfer a GHSA's state to a\n  different keyed advisory. Preserve source fields, capture time, identifier\n  and receipt/hash in evidence. Local OSV/GraphQL projections returned by a\n  helper are not captured REST responses; omitted fields do not become null.\n  Missing required evidence, malformed fields or unresolved identity/lifecycle\n  conflicts use `UNKNOWN` pending resolution. Honor each format's permitted\n  nulls, including global `identifiers` and `references`; null auxiliary fields\n  alone do not invalidate otherwise supported lifecycle evidence. Different\n  aliases may have different states; compare captures of the same identifier\n  with their dates before treating a state change as a contradiction.\n  Reviewed versus unreviewed is separate from lifecycle. A failed lookup is\n  not an exclusion state, lifecycle does not prove the mechanism, and prose\n  such as \"open\" or \"live\" is not a schema value.\n- `landing_commit` is the merge/squash object that carried the BIC;\n  `landing_basis` records how it was located (`PROSE_CARRIER_ON_DEFAULT`,\n  `PROSE_CARRIER_NO_CONTENT_MATCH`, `INTRODUCER_ON_DEFAULT`, `NO_LANDING_NAMED`).\n  `landing_commit` carries a sha only for a located carrier. When the\n  introducer is itself on the default branch and no distinct carrier object\n  exists, the basis is `INTRODUCER_ON_DEFAULT` and `landing_commit` stays\n  **null**; the strict gate rejects an `ATOMIC` record that sets\n  `landing_commit` equal to `introducer_sha`, because that re-asserts a\n  landing the granularity denies.\n  A `SQUASH_DECOMPOSED` record carries either the carrier or `landing_note`\n  saying why none is resolvable, plus what a member-table read showed when the\n  carrier is asserted rather than content-matched.\n- `decomposition_probe` records the one bounded fetch run for an\n  `AGGREGATE_MEMBERS_UNREACHABLE` BIC and what it returned; naming the\n  unreachable object there and in `remaining_gap`, with `flip_condition` saying\n  what would resolve it, is where that search ends.\nbatch identity in `research/r23/index.json`. Existing directories and occupied cases\nare refused. Creation does not claim cases; the leader then claims the copied manifest\nas above. Keep the returned receipt in leader-only storage, outside auditor inputs and\nshared batch files. The index stores only its token hash. A batch name, owner name or\ntask binding cannot substitute for this receipt.\n\nThe leader registers each assignment with that receipt; agents receive only their own\ntask binding for execution/resumption. Registration stores immutable bindings under\n`dispatch` in the same index, preserving its other fields. Relative paths resolve from\nthe registration cwd. Example task spec (replace the batch and discovered pane):\n\n```json\n{\n  \"batch\": \"r23\", \"task\": \"review-a\", \"agent\": \"w8:pEXAMPLE\",\n  \"role\": \"auditor\", \"leader\": \"leader\", \"endpoint\": \"research\",\n  \"manifest\": \"research/r23/manifest.txt\",\n  \"cwd\": \"/home/hanqing/agents/ai-slop\",\n  \"output\": \"research/r23/auditors/a\",\n  \"brief\": \"research/r23/neutral/review-a.md\",\n  \"skill\": \"research/r23/contract/skills/radar-audit/SKILL.md\",\n  \"inputs\": [\"research/r23/neutral/evidence\", \"research/r23/contract\"],\n  \"command\": [\"herdr\", \"agent\", \"prompt\", \"w8:pEXAMPLE\", \"{brief}\", \"--wait\"]\n}\n```\n\n```sh\npython3 scripts/audit_dispatch.py register <task-spec.json> --batch-receipt /path/to/leader-private/r23-receipt.json\npython3 scripts/audit_dispatch.py check --batch r23 --task review-a --binding <returned-binding-sha256>\npython3 scripts/audit_dispatch.py run --batch r23 --task review-a --binding <returned-binding-sha256>\n# Only after inspecting the previous attempt and confirming it has stopped:\npython3 scripts/audit_dispatch.py run --batch r23 --task review-a --binding <returned-binding-sha256> --resume\npython3 scripts/audit_dispatch.py retire --batch r23 --task review-a --binding <returned-binding-sha256>\n```\n\nFor new role-based assignments, copy the chosen project `SKILL.md` into the batch's\ncontract snapshot and supply its path in `skill`: `radar-audit` for an auditor,\n`radar-adjudicate` for an adjudicator. Registration pins that file automatically and\nthe dispatched prompt explicitly tells the worker to read it, even in runners without\nnative skill discovery. Referenced files are not copied or pinned recursively: include\nthe frozen protocols, schema, this operating guide and gates in `inputs`. Map their\nabsolute frozen paths in the brief. Keep executable gate dependencies together as\nspecified in DATA-SCHEMA, and keep evaluator keys out of all worker input directories.\nRepository paths in a skill locate source templates; workers use the frozen paths\nsupplied by their assignment. A missing, changed or conflicting binding/contract stops\nthe affected assignment for leader repair or explicit migration; never silently use\nthe current checkout or classify broken setup as a researched case-level gap.\n\n`skill` is optional for compatibility with older task specs. Omission does not inject\na current skill into a legacy task. Existing bindings remain unchanged; adopting a\nnew skill or protocol requires a new assignment after the old work is stopped and sealed.\n\nRegistration verifies the leader receipt and batch allocation, then pins the manifest,\nexact claim acquisitions, brief, supplied skill, inputs and command;\nretain its receipt in the leader's task assignment. Inputs are files or small sealed\ndirectories, including frozen contracts; do not hash an entire source clone. Every\ncheck/run validates those pins and current ownership. Cross-batch case overlap, a second\nactive adjudicator, occupied agents, shared/nested outputs and active writers of inputs\nare refused. A process lock excludes concurrent runs and retirement; a persisted send\nmarker refuses duplicate dispatch even after the foreground command exits. `--resume`\nis explicit recovery, never an automatic retry after a timeout or safety refusal.\n\nThe command is fixed argv, with exactly one `{brief}` argument replaced by the pinned\nbrief and a task-identity header. Use a foreground runner that waits for its actual work;\nHerdr prompts require `--wait`. Follow the Herdr skill's environment/readiness checks;\nthe guard also requires the exact bound pane/cwd to be idle or done. After ambiguous\ndelivery, inspect the actual pane before any resume. Keep NUMA binding\n[\n  {\n    \"original_source\": \"/home/hanqing/agents/ai-slop/research/incremental-causal-20261004-case-044/auditor1/workspace/output/axios.git\",\n    \"retained_directory\": \"01.git\",\n    \"operation\": \"Copy the stopped auditor's complete local Git metadata/object directory; no new retrieval or research.\",\n    \"hashes\": {\n