21517fd9e46801411f9c2e81965bdc947542532a35ff6e34f\"\n            },\n            {\n              \"path\": \"evidence/package-content-check.raw\",\n              \"sha256\": \"a33bfc4dacc7fd448ee101f249e85cb85818af8e7257c6b195d1643ee8555622\"\n            },\n            {\n              \"path\": \"evidence/fetch-standard.raw\",\n              \"sha256\": \"244c881821314291805c4eab53efdda6997f5170a8daf287fc20466395c2f201\"\n            }\n          ],\n          \"auditor2\": [\n            {\n              \"note\": \"BIC metadata, parent, parent-lacks-fetch.js, parent defaults.adapter\",\n              \"path\": \"evidence/receipt_bic_intro.txt\",\n              \"sha256\": \"2cb3da595f992eb5c8243e336db97520ab0062da98f671f3f46cd12a9abbfc30\"\n            },\n            {\n              \"note\": \"per-tag grep proving the omission; -S histories; --find-object carriers\",\n              \"path\": \"evidence/receipt_omission_history.txt\",\n              \"sha256\": \"a4d8c5414f4291019b4c1b4c245d8ab33aaaa35aa25b2c85433a76f2123065fe\"\n            },\n            {\n              \"note\": \"fix metadata, containing tag v1.20.0, redirect hunk\",\n              \"path\": \"evidence/receipt_fix.txt\",\n              \"sha256\": \"8df1fbae2c1cab0d03100ac5f11b86fdcde87ba08b4b97511285a533230af108\"\n            },\n            {\n              \"note\": \"retained GHSA/CVE lifecycle and version range\",\n              \"path\": \"evidence/receipt_advisory.txt\",\n              \"sha256\": \"0256be4c43008e10f08f6b641e049219d28385d2d10cbee86c1929f1d0ac7942\"\n            },\n            {\n              \"note\": \"bounded PR-ref fetch and branch-level AI-marker scan\",\n              \"path\": \"evidence/receipt_pr_probe.txt\",\n              \"sha256\": \"3a13cfe1e51c6f76344f3e7b42b4752c45fbb8b871ea7630f99472c494062449\"\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        \"mechanism\": {\n          \"auditor1\": \"With numeric maxRedirects zero, affected fetch adapter ignores the option and supplies no redirect mode, so compliant Fetch follows redirects. Requires fetch adapter selection, attacker-controlled redirect source, target reachability and application reliance on this guard. Response data returns to the application; attacker disclosure also needs application exposure. Native HTTP adapter does not follow with zero. No exploit or runtime request was executed.\",\n          \"auditor2\": \"In lib/adapters/fetch.js the fetch adapter destructures resolveConfig(config) without reading maxRedirects and builds the fetch options object without a redirect key, so the Fetch API default redirect:'follow' applies and a caller's maxRedirects:0 guard is bypassed. Requires an attacker-influenced redirect source (attacker server or open redirect), a caller that trusts maxRedirects:0, and the fetch adapter (explicit adapter:'fetch' or a runtime that resolves to fetch). The Node HTTP adapter is not affected: own('maxRedirects')===0 selects the native http/https transport.\"\n        },\n        \"reasoning\": {\n          \"auditor1\": \"Supported conditional vulnerability with an older named-human introducing member and no admitted AI contribution to this defect. Complete source-bound reasoning, competing explanations and failure qualifications are in REPORT.md. This verdict applies the frozen attribution threshold; it does not prove no undisclosed AI use.\",\n          \"auditor2\": \"Step 0: both advisories are ACTIVE (GHSA published_at set, withdrawn_at null; CVE published, not rejected). Step 1: the fetch adapter omits maxRedirects and the redirect option; the HTTP adapter enforces it via the native transport, matching the advisory. Step 2: the omission enters with the fetch adapter itself (a3ff99b5), not at 1.17.0. Step 3: d19040b adds redirect:'manual' for maxRedirects:0 and ships in v1.20.0. Step 4: the causal object has a recovered named human identity, no AI marker, and no contemporaneous disclosure convention. No AI-attributed contribution to the defect.\"\n        }\n      },\n      \"residual\": {\n        \"flip_condition\": {\n          \"auditor1\": \"Contribution-specific AI disclosure bound by content to the introducing feature or a later change that causes the same exposure; an earlier source origin; or contradictory shipped request/redirect behavior would require revision. A new undisclosed-assistance claim needs primary evidence rather than repository AI activity.\",\n          \"auditor2\": \"Primary evidence that lib/adapters/fetch.js enforced maxRedirects or set redirect at some revision before the fix, or a distinct 1.17.0-or-later change that introduced or enlarged the fetch redirect exposure, would change the BIC and could change the verdict.\"\n        },\n        \"remaining_gap\": {\n          \"auditor1\": \"\",\n          \"auditor2\": null\n        },\n        \"residual_triage\": {\n          \"auditor1\": {\n            \"basis\": \"The omitted maxRedirects path is in versions before v1.20.0; both active Fetch invocation paths carry manual in the verified repair. Positive redirect counts and HTTP/2 proxy policy are distinct invariants, not an open numeric-zero path.\",\n            \"decision\": \"NOT-RESIDUAL\"\n          },\n          \"auditor2\": null\n        }\n      },\n      \"versions\": {\n        \"affected_versions\": {\n          \"auditor1\": null,\n          \"auditor2\": \"advisory: >= 1.17.0, < 1.20.0; source omission present from v1.10.0\"\n        },\n        \"clone_head_sha\": {\n          \"auditor1\": null,\n          \"auditor2\": \"e1a8a575\"\n        },\n        \"fixed_release\": {\n          \"auditor1\": null,\n          \"auditor2\": \"v1.20.0\"\n        },\n        \"fixed_versions\": {\n          \"auditor1\": null,\n          \"auditor2\": \"1.20.0\"\n        },\n        \"versions\": {\n          \"auditor1\": {\n            \"advisory_range\": \">=1.17.0, <1.20.0\",\n            \"fixed\": \"1.20.0\",\n            \"observed_affected\": [\n              \"v1.7.0\",\n              \"v1.17.0\",\n              \"v1.18.1\",\n              \"v1.19.0\"\n            ],\n            \"observed_earlier_affected_source\": \"v1.7.0\",\n            \"publication\": \"2026-08-24T13:40:40Z\",\n            \"shipment_scope\": \"Official tagged source, npm packaged source and Node CJS bundle; actual npm 1.19.0/1.20.0 SHA512-verified archives inspected. No all-platform deployment assertion.\"\n          },\n          \"auditor2\": null\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"scope\": \"comparison only; use the adjudication gate and manifest for acceptance\",\n  \"shared\": 1,\n  \"signals\": {\n    \"auditor1\": {},\n    \"auditor2\": {}\n  },\n  \"strict_flags\": {\n    \"auditor1\": {},\n    \"auditor2\": {}\n  },\n  \"union\": 1\n}\n\nAn attacker needs control over the initial URL or a redirect response from an allowed source; the application must rely on numeric `maxRedirects: 0` and use the fetch adapter without equivalent runtime redirect protection. A compliant underlying fetch can then access a redirected resource with the application's network reachability. Disclosure to the attacker additionally requires the application to expose the returned data; a state change additionally requires a reachable target that acts on the resulting request. These deployment conditions are not demonstrated against a real service. Browser CORS/opaque responses, environment egress restrictions, redirect method rewriting, and target authorization can limit effects. This is not unconditional SSRF, arbitrary credential theft, or proof of a deployed compromise.\n\nCounterevidence has been retained and scoped. README describes maxRedirects as a Node option, and the original fetch feature aimed at XHR parity. That narrows expectations and excludes an assertion that every browser request has this guard. It does not refute the actual Node fetch request's silent bypass when an application trusts the option. `http.raw` selects native HTTP/HTTPS when maxRedirects === 0, avoiding follow-redirects; a 3xx may then reject under default status validation. The security distinction is absence of following, not that every HTTP request necessarily throws. Explicit fetchOptions.redirect manual/error is an existing mitigation. Positive finite redirect-count parity is a separate question: this repair enforces numeric zero, not an arbitrary positive count.\n\nStatic source and package inspection support these findings. The retained WHATWG Fetch standard (`fetch-standard.raw`, concept-request-redirect-mode) supplies the normative default-follow and manual-mode semantics. Upstream tests are retained but were not executed or adopted as local test results. No local runtime behavior is claimed.\n\n\n## Repair, integration, and shipment\n\nFirst default-branch closure is **d19040bda7a8be2f82c3c6e1a5bc03917daee39a**, immediate default-branch parent **e0a02dd16671deabe2b809334d4c2ebede29a233**. The official repository default branch is v1.x (`repo-metadata.raw`); its first-parent history contains the landing (`mainline.raw`). This is a runtime-hardening bundle, not an atomic redirect-only repair.\n\nRecovered PR 11141 member **745160bc8a3cadf4180d0abccaf87aed8555485e**, parent **2c94646eb7cb7ab9dcb2aefdb04ab1b040c28e16**, first reads maxRedirects and sets resolvedOptions.redirect = 'manual' for numeric zero before constructing Request or calling the no-Request path. It also removes the second init argument on the Request path. Member **4bee948a922627f281d7c6ca8655245f38998630** restores sanitized second options and sets their redirect to manual as well, preventing that second init from overriding the already-resolved Request. The final fetch source in this member exactly equals the landed fetch source (`repair-members-diff.raw`, empty successful diff). `repair-member-history.raw` retains both causal diffs. Neither member is a redirect-only logical unit; therefore atomic_repair_sha is null rather than pretending a standalone minimal commit was recovered. The repair is fully identified at hunk granularity within these two members, and fix_granularity is CARRIER_BUNDLE.\n\nThe default parent omits maxRedirects; the landing applies the guard on both live invocation branches. Manual overrides fetchOptions.redirect even if the caller supplied follow. The safeFetchOptions copy preserves unrelated options while preventing a second init from reversing the guard. When Request is absent, resolvedOptions supplies manual directly. Compliant custom Fetch implementations receive the authoritative options; a malicious or noncompliant custom implementation is outside this guarantee. Browser manual responses may be opaque (status zero) rather than inspectable Node 3xx responses. The guard acts before the network operation; response size guards after fetching are not its security justification.\n\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Check a reducer's evidence-backed decisions against exact input/output bytes.\n\nThis checks delivery integrity, not the truth of a causal judgment. See\ndocs/ADJUDICATION-PROTOCOL.md. It never assembles records or writes the ledger.\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport collections\nimport hashlib\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nfrom audit_record_gates import AI_VERDICTS, check_record, check_record_strict, load_record, review_signals\nfrom compare_slots import RESEARCH_FIELDS, lane_paths, load_snapshot, research_differences_many\n\nPIN_ROLES = ('manifest', 'audit_protocol', 'reducer_protocol', 'schema', 'brief',\n             'record_gate', 'comparison', 'reduction_gate')\nRESEARCH_CONTRACT = 'causal-work-v1'\nWORK_DIMENSIONS = tuple(name for name in RESEARCH_FIELDS if name != 'residual')\nTERMINAL_ROUTES = {'ANSWERED', 'UNAVAILABLE', 'EXHAUSTED', 'PROHIBITED'}\n\n\ndef text(value) -> bool:\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
