{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/zsh -lc \"numactl --cpunodebind=1 --membind=1 /bin/sh -c 'cat sealed-auditors/auditor1/REPORT.md'\"","aggregated_output":"# Independent audit: CVE-2026-84292 / GHSA-qw65-cvwx-89v3\n\nTask `incremental-causal-20261004-case-045-auditor1`, independent attempt 1; class `alias-2a78acdd5266abc0e8f506fa`; binding `fadea4f9c521a73e99c00cbf7dcd1080f1ceb79ddd21333f357024dde252b59d`. Recipient `incremental-causal-20261004T081300Z-lead`; research handoff only. Protocol `2026-09-27.3`; sidecar contract `causal-work-v1`. This report precedes the derived record; `research.json` covers all six dimensions.\n\n## Finding\n\n**NOT_AI under the frozen attribution threshold; the vulnerability is supported.** Fast-uri emits caller-supplied string ports without grammar validation. Delimiters in that component can become URI authority syntax. Impact requires a consumer to trust the intended host, accept untrusted port data, and use the resulting URI across a destination-policy boundary. The library does not itself perform a request. No specific deployed consumer exposure is assumed.\n\nThe recoverable atomic origin is **garycourt/uri-js `abbbcc742b694160b463d9c700953975bbd0b893`**, authored by **Marek Brieger <m.brieger@ra-micro.de>** on 2019-09-02, parent **`4f6f600fade03398c08adf2755c3a2ad66d31b3c`**. It extends numeric-only port emission to arbitrary strings, without checking the new input domain. Fast-uri inherited this hunk in its 2021 implementation. Attribution to the whole initial fast-uri import would miss the available earlier causal change.\n\nMain-branch repair: **fastify/fast-uri `820e8478dd7da3d09f187bfaf35d50b71f67b8b4`**, parent **`e00815236bc94107e44ef1b2f5318a06bac225c0`**. Relevant npm package files match official source tags in **2.4.6, 3.1.7, 4.1.4**. Their immediate predecessor packages lack the guard. Ordinary isolated checks passed 73 assertions across 13 source revisions. No authority-injection payload, exploit, live target, or downstream network request was executed.\n\nA real later AI marker was found and assessed. It belongs to a parser backslash repair, not to this port-serialization origin, exposure, or repair. NOT_AI is a scoped evidence classification, not proof against undisclosed private AI use.\n\n## Advisory and coverage window\n\nThe fixed cutoff is **2026-10-04T08:13:00Z**. Retained GHSA/NVD objects and their extraction/window metadata are copied unchanged under `evidence/retained/`. Additional retrievals occurred later on October 4, starting around 17:29Z; receipts retain actual times. These are later observations of historical sources, not original-window captures. All decisive object/contribution/release events cited here predate the cutoff. Current availability or mutable tag identity at the earlier cutoff is not independently reconstructed.\n\nThe exact retained global GHSA has publication `2026-09-28T21:24:39Z` and explicit `withdrawn_at: null`, establishing ACTIVE. It explicitly identifies CVE-2026-84292. The separately retrieved matching CVE JSON has `state: PUBLISHED`, publication `2026-09-02T20:09:51.415Z`, and update `2026-09-03T13:16:25.237Z`: ACTIVE. The different CVE and global GHSA publication dates are distinct records, not rejection or duplication. CVE lifecycle confirmation is from the later retrieval. No alias lifecycle is transferred merely from a shared repository.\n\nReceipts: `retained/00-github.json`, `retained/01-nvd.json`, `retained/SOURCES.json`, `cve.json`, `cve.http.json`, `public-sources.receipt.json`, and `input-validation.txt`, all beneath `evidence/`.\n\n## Mechanism and authority\n\nAt the repair parent, exported `serialize` in `index.js` copies `cmpts.port` into a new component. Scheme handling runs first: the HTTP handler only removes its numeric default port or the empty string. Path escaping and host/userinfo normalization do not validate port. The serializer calls `recomposeAuthority` and inserts the returned authority after the scheme/prefix.\n\n`lib/utils.js` independently escapes userinfo and host, then tests only whether port is a number or string. It appends a colon and `String(component.port)` verbatim. Thus a port delimiter can change the parsing role of preceding authority material: an at-sign can make the intended host part of userinfo and subsequent material the actual host. This is a source/grammar inference supported by the advisory, not an executed host-redirection test.\n\nThe trust boundary is **untrusted component data to URI structure consumed by an application**. A fixed host does not protect a separately untrusted port. Possible effects are destination-policy bypass, unintended redirects, or requests to an unintended recipient, conditional on consumer behavior. No application authentication prerequisite or disclosure of specific data is established. Universal Fastify-server exposure does not follow.\n\nObject `normalize` calls `parse(serialize(...))`. Object `equal` reaches `serialize` via `normalizeComparableURI`, which catches serialization errors. Exported `resolveComponent` normalizes object arguments through the same serializer unless `skipNormalization` is explicitly true; that mode returns components, not an emitted URI. Later serialization still validates port. String `resolve` parses its arguments first. The common port guard is not conditional on `skipEscape`, `absolutePath`, or `reference`.\n\nCounterevidence and limits:\n\n- The parser uses a digits-only port capture and converts it to a number, empty string, or omission. The reported arbitrary text must enter through component objects or subsequent assignment. A claim about all parsed string inputs would be too broad.\n- Numeric string ports are legitimate functionality and remain supported after repair. A TypeScript string type does not enforce digits at runtime.\n- The harmless alphabetic port used in ordinary checks can be flagged when reparsed. This does not refute delimiter injection that constructs a different valid authority. Those checks establish missing/repaired validation, not successful exploitation.\n- The library is a URI toolbox. A downstream privileged action is an application condition, not an observed operation here.\n\nEvidence: `source-map.json` binds exact commits, paths, blobs, and saved files; `sources/fast-uri/fix-parent/{index.js,lib/utils.js,lib/schemes.js}`, the corresponding `fix/` files, `causal-diffs.txt`, and `ordinary-regression-final.txt`. Source snapshots are Git blob reads; package extraction is separately identified below.\n\n## Origin and integration\n\nUpstream `abbbcc742b694160b463d9c700953975bbd0b893` changes two production lines and adds a focused seven-line test for string port `9000`. Its parent only emits numeric ports using `toString(10)`. Its child also accepts strings and uses `String(components.port)`. The actual serializer and component-normalization function impose no port grammar. Under the same string input the parent omits port; the child emits it. This is a static parent/child comparison; upstream TypeScript was not compiled or executed.\n\nATOMIC describes the narrow string-port feature and its associated test, not file count, commit age, or number of parents. Earlier 2010 serializer code and the 2015 rewrite also use numeric-only emission. This is the recovered transition to string-port emission in the inherited lineage.\n\nUpstream PR #45 binds account `polmabri` to the exact member and reports one commit. Its API names intermediate merge `13987114cb5314e5dff8cea7abf8f4b6f457edd3`. Default-branch first-parent history instead acquires it at **`c83ee2cb03cb51e3ee2d02782881871ce442ffe0`**; its first-parent diff has the exact source hunk and test. This default-branch integration is the record's `landing_commit`. The later licensed source boundary **`a1acf730b4bba3f1097c9f52e7d9d3aba8cdcaae`** descends from the atomic member and retains the hunk.\n\nFast-uri's initial LI