am exploit tests, package installation scripts or requests were run. Auditor1'\"'s retained 73 assertions over 13 revisions independently corroborate the narrower compatibility/rejection claim; they are not 73 exploit tests.\n+\n+Release-branch integrations \"'`812bd8e2145d68de75092faf7a5e1c70720dc680` and `9f4c943e4d2133e8d78e0941203879216255bb01` carry the same port guard for v2/v3. Their additional IP-literal changes do not change the mainline fix role. Relevant tags resolve to 2.4.6 `ccd1f8dce460fd7ab47767ff11ceeaddb730916a`, 3.1.7 `412e40abd4eb8beabfb952d80abf949a2baf27a3`, and 4.1.4 `a34ced25d015d12dfc2b0cf85ce01a4a0d12ecf6`.\n+\n+The adjudicator independently rehashed all six retained npm tarballs, checked registry SHA512 integrity and compared package.json, index.js, lib/utils.js and lib/schemes.js with their exact tags: **24 byte-for-byte comparisons passed**. The three predecessor packages 2.4.5/3.1.6/4.1.3 lack the guard; the three fixed packages include it. Package content identity, not a release timestamp or ancestry alone, supports shipment. Registry and official-release metadata place these releases on September 2, before cutoff.\n+\n+Advisory ranges remain `<2.4.6`, `>=3.0.0 <3.1.7`, and `>=4.0.0 <4.1.4`. The six downloaded artifacts and inherited source history support this account; every historical package was not downloaded. No reachable primitive-component guard bypass was identified in the inspected emitting callers. Receipts: `main-repair-first-parent.txt`, repair/backport metadata and diffs, exact tag receipts, `package-verification.json`, retained registry/release captures and tarball acquisition receipts, and both benign-regression receipt sets.\n+\n+## Failures, remaining questions and delivery\n+\n+Preserved original failures: auditor1 initially lacked Node; its first harness incorrectly expected equal to throw; a corrected selected-file snapshot omitted v2 scopedChars; and its first report write failed because shell quoting evaluated substitutions outside the intended inner numactl. Original diagnostics and corrected results remain retained. The additional operating clarification records outer runner affinity; neither node0 execution nor successful unassigned reads is inferred. Auditor2 also lacked Node and did not execute its proposed regression. Its source/Node reparse prose is therefore an advisory assertion plus source inference, not local runtime proof. Neither runtime absence nor a failed dependency/write probe proves a source denial.\n+\n+The adjudicator had two nondecisive discovery-command errors: additional-primary/README.md does not exist (the assigned clarification is SOURCE-QUESTIONS.txt), and a /bin/sh brace-form wc path was literal and absent. Reads were corrected without altering evidence. All independent decisive Git, package, official-runtime acquisition and benign-control commands succeeded. There was no observed access denial, rate limit, exploit execution or live-target interaction. Frozen strict/local-object/reduction outputs and command exits are saved outside the sealed case under output/validation; failures, if any, remain alongside corrected runs. The local-object gate uses the product store with the upstream objects as a read-only alternate and records that environment.\n+\n+No available decisive question remains unresolved within the stated static mechanism, recovered lineage, contribution-specific attribution and six-package scope. The next owner is the leader for delivery review. Future facts that would change the finding include authenticated AI evidence tied to the actual string-port origin or a relevant exposure/incomplete repair, a different supported earlier origin, a reachable fixed-version guard bypass, conflicting package bytes, or a lifecycle change in the keyed advisory. Deployed consumer reachability, all uninspected historic packages and undisclosed private authorship are limits, not researched negative facts or pending mandatory routes for the bounded concl