{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/zsh -lc \"numactl --cpunodebind=1 --membind=1 sh -c 'cat contract/docs/AUDIT-PROTOCOL.md; cat additional-primary/SOURCE-QUESTIONS.txt; cat sealed-auditors/auditor1/REPORT.md; cat sealed-auditors/auditor2/REPORT.md'\"","aggregated_output":"# Audit protocol\n\nContract version: `2026-09-27.3`. Existing batches retain their frozen contracts.\n\nN auditors independently research the same cases. Each auditor completes every step\nbelow; do not divide the research chain between auditors. Work in a fresh context per\ncase and save your findings before reading any other auditor's report or prior verdict.\nUse neutral primary sources, not another agent's conclusions. Retain every case.\n\nFor each step, state **what you conclude, what you observed, and where the evidence is**.\nIf a decisive fact is missing after the bounded probe, name it and the next question;\nnever fill a field by guessing. Execution failure is not a researched EVIDENCE_GAP.\n\n[Data fields](DATA-SCHEMA.md#audit-records) define the output.\nThe local [research-work contract](DATA-SCHEMA.md#research-work-contract) separates\nsupported conclusions from unfinished investigation; record it in the owned sidecar.\n[Audit commands](../scripts/README.md) cover claiming, dispatch and submission.\nOne adjudicator subsequently reviews all reports and completes unresolved research\nunder [ADJUDICATION-PROTOCOL](ADJUDICATION-PROTOCOL.md).\n\n## Research steps\n\n0. **Advisory status:** read the advisory record itself (CVE JSON in cveawg, OSV, GHSA)\n   before the mechanism. State governs: a `REJECTED`, withdrawn or duplicate advisory is\n   `FALSE_POSITIVE` and gets no mechanism work, and a live advisory whose own text\n   contradicts its label is not thereby dead. A dead alias does not reject a live keyed\n   advisory, and two identifiers referring to one vulnerability are not by themselves a\n   false positive. A refuted mechanism or wrong-product report needs affirmative primary\n   evidence and `false_positive_ground`; inability to establish the mechanism is\n   `EVIDENCE_GAP`, never proof of `FALSE_POSITIVE`.\n   For a supported FALSE_POSITIVE, causal introducer/repair fields are explicitly\n   null (not applicable), as defined by DATA-SCHEMA. Preserve examined objects in\n   evidence without assigning them vulnerability roles. An excluded advisory's\n   disposition does not change the verdict of its broader live alias cluster.\n\n1. **Cause:** trigger → vulnerable code → security impact, with preconditions and\n   counterevidence. Separate distinct mechanisms.\n   For resource-exhaustion claims, trace resource accounting from the controlled input\n   through allocation or acquisition to release or reuse. Distinguish requested and\n   returned lengths from allocated or retained capacity; inspect active limits,\n   allocation tiers or pool classes and lifetimes at the affected revision. Bound\n   per-operation and repeated-operation costs against legitimate use, without\n   inventing runtime measurements.\n\n2. **BIC:** the smallest logically atomic introducing change, which is not always the commit\n   it landed in. `introducer_sha` is the atomic change, `landing_commit` the merge/squash\n   object that carried it; compare its immediate parent, trace moves, and separate\n   introduction from later exposure. Declare `bic_granularity`:\n\n   - `ATOMIC`: the object is a logically atomic causal change, not its own `landing_commit`.\n     One parent, an initial commit or a bulk-import label does not establish atomicity.\n     A root tree can be the earliest observable boundary: identify the causal path/hunk and\n     explain minimality in `landing_note`. Do not infer pre-import origin or authorship\n     from that boundary. A known aggregate follows the next two rules.\n   - `SQUASH_DECOMPOSED`: members reconstructable; list their 40-hex shas.\n   - `AGGREGATE_MEMBERS_UNREACHABLE`: members still unavailable after one bounded PR-ref\n     fetch. That probe is the terminus, not the start of a hunt: name the unreachable object\n     (subject, path, epoch, first public appearance) and what would flip the read, then land\n     the row as `BLOCKED`. For an import without a PR, record the authoritative source-history\n     probe and its limit. Push counts and missing public events alone cannot establish\n     content identity, atomicity or the absence of earlier history.\n   - `NON_GIT_BOUNDARY`: the change predates VCS, so `introducer_sha` is null.\n\n   Membership is located by content identity (`--find-object`, pickaxe on the introduced\n   blob), never by PR number or title: the object that landed the content is a carrier, not a\n   member, and a member whose added lines the landing tree does not carry is a candidate from\n   another epoch. An aggregate's trailers, co-authors and badges belong to the aggregate, not\n   to a hunk: they are never BIC attribution. If history is unavailable, isolate the causal\n   diff and say what that costs commit-level minimality.\n\n   Compare behavior in the immediate parent and child, not just the first pickaxe hit.\n   Check shallow boundaries before treating a root or a failed ancestry walk as evidence.\n   Missing local objects are not proof that public history is unavailable: distinguish a\n   commit, its trees/blobs and the product repository from a reporter or umbrella clone.\n   Recover missing objects through one bounded authoritative probe in an owned scratch\n   clone, preserving shared read-only clones; retain its command and output. If the BIC\n   itself is unknown, use the explicit nulls allowed for open records by DATA-SCHEMA.\n\n3. **Fix:** the minimum repairing hunks/commit set and why it closes the defect; follow\n   partial repairs through the residual defect to closure; verify affected/fixed versions\n   separately, and substantiate an unpatched state plus a proposed repair when no fix exists.\n   `fix_sha` is the first mechanism-closing change on the default branch, not whichever\n   landing a lane named. If a side-branch repair reaches the default branch through a\n   merge, identify that integration and compare its default-branch parent with the merged\n   content. Later ancestry alone does not make the side-branch commit the default-branch\n   closure; retain its member and other landing roles separately. Fix-side AI is informational: it never substitutes for BIC-side\n   attribution, and a fix-side marker alone does not move the verdict. If the remediation is\n   partial, or a claimed patched release does not contain the fix, `remaining_gap` names the\n   still-open surface and its closing commit, or none.\n\n4. **AI:** tie AI evidence to causal changes across the history, within its disclosed scope,\n   and record how it was admitted (`ai_admissibility`): first establish that the named object\n   carries the causal change at the claimed granularity, then read its actual marker.\n   A first-party disclosure bound to that causal change is admissible once the\n   record says what binds it; repository-level AI activity, changelog habits, labels and org\n   policy are never admissible. A TP needs demonstrated AI contribution to the defect:\n   introduction, new exposure or incomplete remediation can qualify after a human BIC, and a\n   successful repair alone does not. `NOT_AI` needs a recovered named human/organization\n   identity on the BIC object, no marker on that object, and a spot check of the repo's\n   disclosure convention at that date - a placeholder, anonymous or unresolvable identity is\n   `EVIDENCE_GAP`, not `NOT_AI`. Label definitions: [DATA-SCHEMA](DATA-SCHEMA.md).\n   \"No marker\" is a statement about every local object that carries the causal content,\n   including commits no ref reaches (a pre-force-push iteration of the same PR keeps its\n   trailer while the landed squash drops it): enumerate local commit objects with\n   `git cat-file --batch-all-objects --batch-check`, filtering for commits. Compare\n   their trees at relevant historical paths for the exact causal blob or supported\n   hunk, then read every matching commit's full metadata. `--find-object` and pickaxe\n   select content transitions, not unchanged descendants; neither proves complete\n   carrier coverage. Retain the inspected object and path universe. A marker found\n   there attributes the content only once the record shows the same blob or hunk on both\n   objects; a shallow or promisor clone with no such objects is a clone-state gap, not\n   evidence of absence.\n\n   Preserve the exact marker quote and its SHA/source. Read BIC and fix metadata separately;\n   never copy a fix's trailer into the BIC evidence. A quote on an aggregate does not become\n   hunk attribution merely because the record labels the aggregate ATOMIC.\n\n5. **Evidence:** retain a per-case report and primary-source snapshots with exact SHAs,\n   paths/hunks, URLs and capture dates; explain comparisons, counterevidence and how\n   uncertainties affect the conclusion. State `flip_condition`: what evidence would change\n   the verdict. Report, evidence and `remaining_gap` must agree: never carry a closed verdict\n   while the record's own evidence names an unclosed path.\n\n   For each decisive claim retain the command/URL, repository and full object SHA, capture\n   time, exit code and raw-output file. Distinguish observed facts from inferred causal\n   judgments. The minimum evidence covers mechanism, parent/child behavior, AI provenance,\n   repair or confirmed unpatched state, and affected/fixed release membership. \"Verified\"\n   and a PASS field without the underlying observation do not satisfy this requirement.\n\nFinish with a per-case report covering advisory, mechanism, BIC/landing, AI provenance,\nfix, versions and residual/flip condition. Link each conclusion to its decisive receipt.\nKeep observations separate from inferences. Missing evidence is a qualified research\noutcome; a passed format check does not make the conclusion correct.\nResolve exact root versus2013 nested-member recursion parent behavior, separable first-publication bundle versus atomicity, both recursive sites and timing of depth versus output accounting. Honor completed bounded origin inquiry, no repeat via new context. Read genuine Claude-disclosed PR132/133 proposals and actual merged/closed states/content before attributing shipped iterative hardening; unmerged proposals are not automatically product landings. Default finite maxDepth guarantee must stay distinct from runtime-specific stack safety and explicitly unbounded/noncomparable caller options. No stack exhaustion, brace payload or timing experiment; missing Node does not authorize a payload rerun. Original qualified outer runner uses bwrap isolation, despite auditor1 prose saying instruction-only; actual spec/mounts govern and no independence breach is observed. Preserve missing original clone/runtime checks and all actual failures.\n# Independent audit: alias-31b59d85d0a2e70df260e6c1\n\nTask: incremental-causal-20261004-case-046-auditor1; independent attempt 1.\nTask binding: `190563ccf16248883c84909fab8c1c29c7ea0437de7e571d9d30411170cd9f0f`.\nRecipient: incremental-causal-20261004T081300Z-lead. Research handoff only.\nContract: 2026-09-27.3 / causal-work-v1. Source coverage cutoff:\n2026-10-04T08:13:00Z. Manifest SHA256:\n`532d28f0ab86d50e1731965acc6067cb646e44935bd4b1fe3636b7cfeba5d6ac`.\n\n## Finding and its limits\n\n**BLOCKED on atomic introduction/provenance, with the reported nesting mechanism\nand default-mode repair supported by source inspection.** This is not a refutation\nof CVE-2026-102278 / GHSA-qhr7-859c-m2p7. The public history contains an identifiable\n2013 parent/child transition to recursive nested-member expansion. That transition\nis a separable implementation/release bundle, rather than a recovered atomic member.\nThe frozen protocol does not permit turning the bundle into an atomic BIC merely\nbecause the available member search ended. No contribution-specific AI evidence\nwas established for that introducing component or for the shipped changes that\nretained the nesting failure. Two explicitly Claude-assisted proposed backports\nwere recovered and compared; neither merged, and their diffs do not establish\nauthorship of the shipped iterative implementation.\n\nThe first main-branch mechanism repair is\n`935d78f32f335b2ff76578e5c5e877d31ae9888c`. Actual npm artifacts establish that\n5.0.11, 3.0.8, 2.1.6 and 1.1.20 contain the nested-call depth guard and caller\nwiring. Their immediately preceding releases do not. This finding is scoped to\nnormal finite depth limits, especially the shipped default of 1,000. An explicit\nunbounded or non-comparable maxDepth option does not retain that guarantee.\nStatic inspection establishes the resource-accounting failure; no stack-exhaustion\ntest or operational payload was executed. The prepared ordinary regression checks\ncould not start because no Node runtime is available.\n\n## Evidence handling and chronology\n\nAll investigation used assigned material and public upstream GitHub, CVE and npm\nsources. No other lane, prior verdict, host memory, database or deployed target was\nread or changed. Writes are under output/. All shell entry commands use node-1\nNUMA binding; child processes inherit it. Git commands run with\nGIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH=1 and GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0 in an owned scratch clone.\nIsolation is instruction-enforced; no additional filesystem isolation is claimed.\n\n`evidence/pin-verification.stdout` verifies the supplied manifest and both retained\ninput hashes and records the frozen contract/gate hashes. The original snapshots\nand SOURCES.json are copied into evidence. Each network/Git probe has a\n`*.receipt.json` with exact argv, capture time, exit status and raw stdout/stderr\nhashes. HTTP responses retain their headers. Source files extracted from npm\narchives are identified as archive members, not Git object reads.\n\nThe retained advisory snapshots are coverage-window evidence. New network reads\nwere captured on October 4 after the 08:13 cutoff. The decisive Git objects,\npublication timestamps and PR lifecycle events they describe precede that cutoff;\nthese are retrospective observations, not claims that the new captures existed\ninside the frozen window. In particular, CVE lifecycle is newly resolved from\ncveawg rather than inferred from NVD's Deferred processing status. No later-dated\nrepair is used to retroactively close the window's vulnerability.\n\n## Advisory status and authority boundary\n\nThe retained global GitHub object exactly matches GHSA-qhr7-859c-m2p7, has\npublished_at 2026-09-29T23:45:17Z and explicit withdrawn_at null: ACTIVE.\nThe newly captured repository advisory says state published and publication\n2026-09-14T21:50:30Z. The different publication fields belong to different formats,\nnot conflicting withdrawal states. The keyed CVE record from cveawg says PUBLISHED,\ndatePublished 2026-09-28T20:57:09.850Z: ACTIVE. The GHSA explicitly identifies this\nCVE, establishing the alias relationship. The earlier GHSA-mh99-v99m-4gvg was\nconsulted as related history, not assigned as an alias for this different mechanism.\n\nDecisive receipts: `00-github.json`, `01-nvd.json`, `ghsa-live.stdout`,\n`ghsa-repository.stdout`, `cve.stdout` and their capture receipts.\n\nThe exposed product is an npm library. Its public expand function accepts a\nstring; it has no user/role/authentication model or network endpoint itself. A\nconsumer that passes an untrusted pattern grants it access to the synchronous\nparser in the consumer's process. A stack overflow raises RangeError. Process\ntermination additionally depends on the consumer failing to catch that exception;\nthe advisory's remote unauthenticated severity requires an actual application\ninput path and its authentication policy. No particular network service or\nminimatch/glob deployment was inspected. The direct exported library call is\nenough to establish library exposure, not universal remote reachability.\n\n## Mechanism and competing explanations\n\nAt the immediate pre-fix revision\n`df7682f386cdf2d7fef6067bc78ed70d824e1f3f` (5.0.10), src/index.ts exports expand.\nIt destructures max and maxLength, escapes the input, and invokes expand_ before\nunescaping returned strings. expand_ uses balanced-match to separate brace groups.\nparseCommaParts is already iterative in this revision. The tail is also already\niterative; neither fact removes the nested recursive calls.\n\nFor a set with multiple members, the loop calls expand_ on each member before\nthe values-length and output-count checks consume the returned strings. For a\nsingle parsed part, it recursively expands that part and then maps embrace over\nthe result. Neither call has a recursion-depth budget. Nested syntax therefore\nretains a chain of native frames, each holding the current substring/parser state\nand arrays until its child returns. The invariant needed here is a bound on\nin-flight nested calls, not just on completed output. max counts emitted strings;\nmaxLength accounts for accumulated output characters. Neither reserves or checks\nstack capacity before descending. Small-output nested inputs can grow that chain\nlinearly in nesting depth; repeated balanced scans and retained intermediate\nstate also mean output limits are not a proof of constant CPU or total heap use.\n\nThe balanced-match 4.0.2 dependency itself scans iteratively with an array of open\npositions; it is not the recursive caller responsible for this allegation.\nIts retained source is `balanced-4.0.2.cjs`. Exact crash thresholds in the advisory\nare upstream measurements, not measurements reproduced here. Stack sizes depend\non runtime/build and surrounding call depth. Catching RangeError limits the\ntermination consequence but does not add a parser work budget.\n\nCounterevidence considered: the earlier iterative-tail repair, the later iterative\ncomma-parser repair, output limits, and ordinary nested syntax. All allow useful\nshallow nesting. The decisive distinction is the location and timing of the two\nremaining recursive calls before completed-output accounting. We do not combine\nseparate tail recursion, argument spreading, excessive sequences or malformed\nrewrite behavior into this CVE's mechanism.\n\nReceipts: `source-set.stdout`, `main-repair.stdout`, `prior-repair.stdout`,\n`causal-comparisons.stdout`; standalone parent/child source files prefixed by\n935d78f32f335b2ff76578e5c5e877d31ae9888c in evidence. Published 5.0.10 CJS and ESM\nentrypoints independently exhibit the same calls.\n\n## Introduction and granularity\n\nThe repository is non-shallow and not configured as a partial/promisor clone.\nThe root `3624453f42ebf1d5fda9247ec4f87b5821051a27` contains a regex-based brace\nexpander. It recursively expands prefix/postfix but splits the matched body into\nmembers without recursively expanding each member. It is not evidence that the\ncurrent nested-member mechanism was present from the root.\n\nIts immediate child `9a5bef783fad507aed02242ce726f89640bf42d1`, authored by\nJulian Gruber <julian@juliangruber.com> on 2013-10-13, replaces that parser with\nbalanced brace matching and adds `concatMap(n, function(el) { return expand(el) })`.\nBalanced nested groups stay in the member string, so the new call repeatedly\ndescends into them without a depth bound. The parent lacks that call under the\nsame nested-member conditions. This is the earliest observable introduction of\nthe relevant descent, not merely a later exposure or a root-boundary assumption.\n\nThe same commit changes range syntax/implementation, increment and padding\nsemantics, package/dependencies and first-publication material. Nested-member\ndescent can be separated logically from the independent range behavior. The full\ncommit is therefore a supported introducing bundle, not the smallest logically\natomic introducing change. Its subject is \"ready for first publish\"; the relevant\npath is index.js; the epoch/first public-history appearance is 2013-10-13. No\nsmaller member commit is present in the inspected public object graph.\n\n`030a0f89a118dd1f3331526cbf121e0d705433e9`, parent\n`48bb7fa68245b76cabd71e5b5de898d0f3b817c3`, later refactors part parsing and adds\nthe explicit single-set rewrapping branch. Its parent already recursively expands\nthe corresponding nested member. It explains the modern branch shape, rather\nthan moving the origin of unbounded nested descent to 2014.\n\nThe bounded authoritative recovery was a full owned upstream clone, public\ncommit-to-PR lookup for 9a5bef (HTTP 200, empty association array), and one explicit\nfetch of that exact SHA. Fetch succeeded without exposing constituent history;\nthe bundle still has only the original root as parent. This does not prove no\nprivate development history ever existed. Under the frozen rule the granularity\nis AGGREGATE_MEMBERS_UNREACHABLE and the verdict stays BLOCKED. The report does\nnot infer authorship of unavailable member work from the bundle's signature.\n\nReceipts: `historical-source.stdout`, `origins.stdout`, `origin-prs.stdout`,\n`origin-history-probe.*`, `object-coverage.stdout`, `causal-comparisons.stdout`.\n\n## AI participation and later hardening\n\nAll 288 initially cloned commit objects were enumerated with cat-file\n--batch-all-objects, and their complete raw metadata and historical index.js /\nsrc/index.ts blob identities were read. After recovering PR132/133, the final\nuniverse contains 290 commits. `exact-blob-carriers.json` matches the exact\nparent/child source blobs of the investigated origin, refactor, earlier hardening,\nrepair/backports and Claude-disclosed proposals against every local commit tree\nat those paths. Matching metadata is retained, including descendants that did\nnot change those blobs. Metadata for the