not evidence of a product landing. Their Claude disclosure\nis admissible for those proposed diffs, but not for 9a5bef, a1bd339 or this CVE's\nshipped repair. An unmerged proposal retaining an earlier nested defect is not\nproof of an AI-caused shipped vulnerability.\n\nThe repair objects' own metadata have no AI marker. Successful repair-side\nassistance, even if later established, would not by itself attribute the defect.\nReceipts: `object-coverage.stdout`, `causal-comparisons.stdout`,\n`exact-blob-carriers.json`, `search-ai.stdout`, `search-claude.stdout`,\n`ai-backport-132.stdout`, `ai-backport-133.stdout`, `ai-backport-fetch.*`,\n`ai-backport-diffs.stdout`, `prior-prs.stdout`, and the PR129/130/136 captures.\n\n## Repair and publication\n\n`935d78f32f335b2ff76578e5c5e877d31ae9888c` has one parent,\n`df7682f386cdf2d7fef6067bc78ed70d824e1f3f`, and appears directly on main's\nfirst-parent chain. Despite its \"Merge commit from fork\" title, it is not a\ntwo-parent integration for which later reachability alone is being used.\nThe parent has neither maxDepth nor a depth argument. The child adds default\nEXPANSION_MAX_DEPTH=1000, initializes depth=0 in the exported caller, checks\ndepth > maxDepth at entry before parsing, and passes depth+1 at both nested sites.\nAfter the budget is exceeded, the remaining string is returned literally. That\ncaps nested expand_ frames at a finite constant under shipped defaults. The\nchanged source and regression-test additions form one coherent repair; no distinct\nrepair member has been recovered or is required by its logical scope.\n\nThe same depth repair lands on v1 in\n`1efee7c397c191da6287a78ec19512476a966a7b`, v2 in\n`1ba3c71f89fb3448c8be6dcf2d17b543c2738f16`, and v3 in\n`de84f144e9816f30e25fc8179e2e1249ab6df0db`. These are other line landings of the\nsame repair, not prerequisites mislabeled as equivalent fixes.\n\nThe earlier fixes to tail expansion and comma parsing are prerequisites already\npresent in the compared parent. This finding does not assert all earlier DoS\nmechanisms are repaired solely by a depth parameter. It also does not promise\nbounded work for arbitrary input length, a universally safe runtime stack size,\nor safety if callers deliberately set maxDepth to Infinity/NaN or excessive values.\n\nOfficial GitHub releases metadata was consulted first; it does not list these\nnew versions, so no guessed release-tag 404 was used as absence evidence.\nPublisher-controlled npm version metadata supplies actual publication times,\ntarball URLs and gitHead. Actual repository tags resolve to those gitHeads.\nEach downloaded artifact's SHA1 matches the registry shasum. Extracted source and\npackage metadata verify real export wiring, not just tag ancestry:\n\n| npm version | Publication (UTC, 2026-09-14) | gitHead/tag commit |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 5.0.11 | 21:49:31.581 | 82479277b90f2f86263e946f9ff89689b3734568 |\n| 1.1.20 | 21:49:58.092 | c6513ad31e08edb56dc414a629e39cba724b7548 |\n| 3.0.8 | 21:51:10.067 | 8bebb261fd9249c3bac705eea15d06c4ea788d5e |\n| 2.1.6 | 21:51:19.666 | 5a5c07b25ea84a899322ebb820336260277cbe90 |\n\n5.0.11's require/import export maps lead to dist/commonjs/index.js and\ndist/esm/index.js; both compiled files have the guarded calls. Legacy packages'\nindex.js entrypoints likewise contain the guard. Artifacts 5.0.10, 3.0.7, 2.1.5\nand 1.1.19 lack it; 5.0.9 was additionally inspected to distinguish the prior\ncomma-parser state. The advisory's entire historical semver range is retained as\nreported scope, not represented as an exhaustive test of every published version.\nThese are stable version publications, not prereleases. Creation of a Git commit\nis not being substituted for npm publication.\n\nReceipts: `main-repair.stdout`, `causal-comparisons.stdout`, `releases.stdout`,\n`npm.stdout`, `release-content.stdout`, `npm-VERSION.*`, `package-VERSION-*` and\n`tag-VERSION-*`. These preserve the archive bytes and exact inspected members.\n\n## Unresolved question, execution failures, and handoff\n\nThe decisive unresolved question is whether a primary member-level record exists\nfor the nested-member change inside 9a5bef, with its own parent/content and\ncontribution-specific provenance. A recoverable original member or first-party\nchange-specific account could resolve atomicity and attribution. A demonstrated\nAI-assisted shipped hardening/exposure that retains this precise defect could\nalso change the causal verdict; the known unmerged proposals do not do so.\n\nExecution failures are preserved. The first ordinary-regression attempt failed\ninside the receipt helper with FileNotFoundError for node (tool exit 1) before it\ncould write its normal receipt. The helper was corrected to retain spawn failures;\n`ordinary-regression-retry.*` records the same missing executable as exit 127.\n`runtime-availability.*` finds none of node, nodejs, bun or deno and returns 127.\nThe original failure is separately transcribed in `execution-failures.json`.\nNo runtime assertions passed. This optional dynamic check remains unrun, rather\nthan being called an answered source query. The initial `fix-main` path-limited\nGit show returned zero bytes because this line uses src/index.ts; the unrestricted\n`main-repair` capture corrects it without erasing that empty observation.\n\nThe completed account precedes derivation of record.json. `research.json` covers\nall six dimensions and pins its source evidence; its bounded completion statements\ndescribe recorded routes, not omniscient source coverage. `validation-summary.json`\nand the strict/local-gate receipts record actual check outcomes. Readback is retained\nseparately. SEAL.json binds stable case files, excluding itself; scratch Git and\nruntime-home are outside the case payload. No research acceptance, ledger mutation\nor publication is claimed by this submission.\n# Audit report: alias-31b59d85d0a2e70df260e6c1\n\nTask: auditor2, incremental-causal-20261004-case-046, attempt 1\nRole: auditor (independent). Protocol `2026-09-27.3`, research contract `causal-work-v1`.\nCoverage cutoff: 2026-10-04T08:13:00Z. Class id: `alias-31b59d85d0a2e70df260e6c1`.\nVerdict: **NOT_AI** (human-origin defect, no AI marker on the causal object).\n\n## 0. Advisory status\n\n- Keyed advisory GHSA-qhr7-859c-m2p7 is `reviewed`, published 2026-09-29T23:45:17Z,\n  `withdrawn_at: null`; alias CVE-2026-102278 is published 2026-09-28, `Deferred`.\n  Disposition: **ACTIVE** (not rejected/withdrawn/duplicate).\n- Aliases GHSA-qhr7-859c-m2p7 and CVE-2026-102278 describe one vulnerability; a live\n  alias is not a false positive. The advisory text is internally consistent and its\n  claimed mechanism is confirmed below, so no `FALSE_POSITIVE` ground applies.\n- Receipts: `evidence/00-github`-family input `inputs/00-github.json`,\n  `inputs/01-nvd.json` (pinned, hashes in SEAL).\n\n## 1. Mechanism (confirmed by source, not executed)\n\n`expand_()` recurses once per level of brace **nesting** at two sites in the affected\nrevision, before any output bound is reached:\n- comma members: each alternative of a set is expanded by a recursive call\n  (`var expanded = expand(n[j], max, maxLength, false)`);\n- single set: a set whose body parses to a single part is re-wrapped via a recursive\n  call (`n = expand(n[0], max, maxLength, false).map(embrace)`).\n\nDeep nesting therefore consumes native stack proportional to nesting depth. `max` and\n`maxLength` bound output size, not recursion depth, so a payload that expands to almost\nnothing still recurses ~one frame per level and overflows the native stack\n(`RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded`); an uncaught `RangeError` terminates\nthe Node.js process (availability only; no code execution, no data exposure).\nAuthority: an application passing an untrusted pattern to `expand()` directly, or via\n`minimatch`/`glob`, is exposed; no special role or session state is required.\n\nThe mechanism is a distinct, later-surviving recursion from the tail recursion made\niterative by the earlier CVE-2026-14257 fix (`'...{a,b}'.repeat(n)` chained input is\nhandled iteratively) and from the `parseCommaParts()` recursion addressed by\nGHSA-6j4f-fj2g-mc7p. The two surviving sites are directly visible in the repair patch\n(below), which is the decisive primary observation.\n\n- Receipts: `evidence/commit-935d78f32f335b2ff76578e5c5e877d31ae9888c.json` (patch adds\n  `maxDepth` at exactly the two recursion sites), `evidence/commit-1efee7c397c191da6287a78ec19512476a966a7b.json`,\n  `evidence/commit-de84f144e9816f30e25fc8179e2e1249ab6df0db.json`.\n- Not run: no Node.js runtime was exercised (no `node_modules` install, no payload\n  execution; the assignment forbids creating operational payloads). The depth figures\n  are taken from the advisory and the patch message, not independently measured here.\n\n## 2. Origin / BIC\n\n- `bic_granularity`: **ATOMIC**.\n- `introducer_sha`: `9a5bef783fad507aed02242ce726f89640bf42d1` (\"ready for first publish\",\n  Julian Gruber <julian@juliangruber.com>, 2013-10-13T12:56:58Z).\n- `introducer_parent`: `3624453f42ebf1d5fda9247ec4f87b5821051a27` (\"initial commit\",\n  2013-10-09).\n- Evidence: raw `index.js` at the initial commit is 1768 bytes and contains no recursive\n  `expand()` call; at `9a5bef783fad` it is 2332 bytes and `expand()` already recurses on\n  comma members (`n = concatMap(n, function(el) { return expand(el) })`) and on the tail\n  (`expand(m.pre)` / `expand(m.post)`). The recursion that this advisory describes is\n  therefore introduced in the same direct, single-parent commit that first shipped the\n  recursive expansion implementation. It is a root/content boundary, not a merge.\n- The single-set re-wrap site was later refactored (e.g. `030a0f89a118`, isaacs,\n  2014-11-30) but that is a refactor of already-recursive behavior, not the origin of the\n  uncontrolled nesting recursion; the comma-member recursion predates it.\n- `landing_basis`: `INTRODUCER_ON_DEFAULT` — no separate landing/carrier object exists;\n  `9a5bef783fad` is a direct commit on the mainline and is the earliest observable\n  boundary. Minimality: the commit's causal hunk is the recursive `expand()` body; the\n  initial commit's non-recursive `expand` is the immediate parent comparison.\n- Receipts: `evidence/raw-3624453f42eb.js`, `evidence/raw-9a5bef783fad.js`,\n  `evidence/raw-030a0f89a118.js`, `evidence/idx-3.json` (commit list/api metadata).\n- Qualification: GitHub-API reachable history only; no local clone was available, so\n  `--repo` object checks and full `--batch-all-objects` carrier enumeration were not run\n  (see Failures).\n\n## 3. Repair and release\n\n- `fix_sha` (first mechanism-closing change on the default branch `main`):\n  `935d78f32f335b2ff76578e5c5e877d31ae9888c` (parent `df7682f386cdf2d7fef6067bc78ed70d824e1f3f`,\n  the 5.0.10 tree; `src/index.ts` + `test/index.js`, +37/-4).\n- Other landings of the same repair (`fix_shas`):\n  `1efee7c397c191da6287a78ec19512476a966a7b` (parent tree 1.1.19 -> released 1.1.20) and\n  `de84f144e9816f30e25fc8179e2e1249ab6df0db` (parent tree 3.0.7 -> released 3.0.8).\n- The repair threads `maxDepth` (default `EXPANSION_MAX_DEPTH = 1000`) through `expand_()`\n  and, past the cap, returns the group literally instead of recursing. It bounds recursion\n  depth at both vulnerable sites, restoring stack safety while keeping `expand` total and\n  matching `max`/`maxLength` truncation semantics. The cap (1000) is well below the\n  reported crash depths (~3,125 single-set, ~3,907 comma).\n- Released content verified from npm tarballs, not from tags/ancestry:\n  `brace-expansion@5.0.9` and `1.1.18` carry no `maxDepth`; `5.0.11`, `3.0.8`, `2.1.6`\n  and `1.1.20` do. Published: 5.0.11 2026-09-14T21:49:31Z, 3.0.8 21:51:10Z,\n  2.1.6 21:51:19Z, 1.1.20 21:49:58Z — matching GHSA `first_patched_version`.\n- Later releases `5.0.12`, `3.0.9`, `2.1.7`, `1.1.21` add a separate\n  `EXPANSION_MAX_REWRITES` cap for a different defect (unbounded `{a},b}` rewrite passes:\n  quadratic time / event-loop blocking, not stack exhaustion). This is a distinct\n  mechanism, not an incomplete remediation of this advisory's stack-exhaustion defect;\n  both the `maxDepth` cap and the rewrite cap are present in those later tarballs.\n- No unreferenced 2.x fix commit was among the advisory's three referenced SHAs; the\n  2.1.6 release content nevertheless contains `maxDepth`. The 2.1.6 commit object is\n  therefore not pinned in this record (see remaining_gap).\n\n## 4. AI participation\n\n- `ai_on_bic`: **false**. `ai_marker.state`: ABSENT on `introducer_sha`\n  `9a5bef783fad...`; the causal object carries a recovered named human identity\n  (`Julian Gruber <julian@juliangruber.com>`) and no agent/bot author, no AI co-author\n  trailer, and no first-person AI disclosure in its message.\n- `identity_class`: HUMAN; `disclosure_convention`: CONVENTION_NOT_OBSERVED for the BIC\n  date (2013-10-13), which predates contemporary AI-coding-assistant disclosure\n  conventions, so no marker convention existed to be unused.\n- Fix-side: the three repair commits are authored by the same human maintainer with\n  GitHub as committer and no AI trailer (`fix_ai_marker.state`: ABSENT, informational).\n  Fix-side evidence is not used to attribute the BIC.\n- Qualification: local-object enumeration (`--batch-all-objects`) was not possible\n  without a clone; carrier coverage rests on the API commit list for `index.js` and the\n  root/blob reads above. No marker was found on any inspected carrier.\n\n## 5. Versions\n\n- Affected (GHSA/NVD): `< 1.1.20` (1.x), `>= 2.0.0 < 2.1.6`, `>= 3.0.0 < 3.0.8`,\n  `>= 4.0.0 < 5.0.11` (4.x has no in-line patched release; upgrade to 5.0.11).\n- Fixed: 1.1.20, 2.1.6, 3.0.8, 5.0.11 (verified in released tarball content).\n- CSV/SSVC: CWE-400/CWE-674, CVSS 3.1 7.5 (AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H),\n  CISA SSVC exploitation `none`, automatable `yes`, technicalImpact `partial`.\n- Receipts: `evidence/npm-registry.json`, `evidence/tarballs/be-*.tgz`.\n\n## 6. Residual / flip condition\n\n- `remaining_gap`: the unreferenced 2.x backport commit object for 2.1.6, and full\n  local-object carrier enumeration / `--repo` verification, which needed a clone that\n  was not provisioned in this attempt.\n- `flip_condition`: a recovered BIC object carrying an AI marker (bot/agent author,\n  AI co-author trailer, or first-person AI disclosure) at the recursive `expand()` hunk\n  would move the verdict off NOT_AI; conversely, published tarball content for a claimed\n  fixed version lacking `maxDepth`, or evidence that the 1000-level cap can still be\n  driven to stack exhaustion, would reopen mechanism/repair.\n\n## Failures and setup\n\n- No local brace-expansion clone was present in the workspace; `--repo` object\n  verification and local `--batch-all-objects` enumeration could not run.\n- Node.js execution of the regression payloads was not performed (no operational payload\n  execution per assignment); depth/impact figures are the advisory's, corroborated by the\n  repair patch structure.\n- One history probe (`?path=index.js&per_page=1&page=30`) returned an empty array because\n  the correct page count for that page size is 3; re-issued with `per_page=10` and\n  retained as `evidence/idx-1..3.json`, `evidence/oldest-index.json`.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
