{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/zsh -lc 'numactl --cpunodebind=1 --membind=1 cat sealed-auditors/auditor2/record.json'","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"class_id\": \"alias-31b59d85d0a2e70df260e6c1\",\n  \"case_id\": \"GHSA-qhr7-859c-m2p7\",\n  \"advisory_ids\": [\"GHSA-qhr7-859c-m2p7\", \"CVE-2026-102278\"],\n  \"advisory_disposition\": \"ACTIVE\",\n  \"advisory_aliases\": [\n    {\"id\": \"GHSA-qhr7-859c-m2p7\", \"state\": \"ACTIVE\"},\n    {\"id\": \"CVE-2026-102278\", \"state\": \"ACTIVE\"}\n  ],\n  \"verdict\": \"NOT_AI\",\n  \"summary\": \"brace-expansion expand_() recurses once per level of brace nesting at the comma-member and single-set re-wrap sites, so a deeply nested but tiny untrusted pattern exhausts the native stack and terminates the Node.js process (availability only). The origin is the human-authored recursive implementation first published in 2013-10-13; the 2026-09-14 maxDepth repair bounds recursion depth at both sites.\",\n  \"cause\": \"Trigger: an untrusted pattern with deep brace nesting (e.g. nested comma sets or a nested single-part set). Vulnerable code: expand_() recursively calls itself once per nesting level at two sites, and the output caps max/maxLength are only consulted after sub-expansions return. Impact: native stack exhaustion raising RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded before either cap can apply; an uncaught RangeError terminates the Node.js process. Preconditions: the caller passes untrusted input to expand() directly or through minimatch/glob; no extra role, configuration, session or transaction state is required. Counterevidence: chained-input recursion on m.post was already made iterative by the earlier CVE-2026-14257 fix and parseCommaParts recursion by GHSA-6j4f-fj2g-mc7p, so those payload shapes do not establish this defect; the surviving two nesting sites are confirmed by the repair patch.\",\n  \"mechanisms\": [\"uncontrolled recursion on nested brace groups causing native stack exhaustion (CWE-674/CWE-400), denial of service\"],\n  \"mechanism\": \"uncontrolled recursion on nested brace groups causing native stack exhaustion (CWE-674/CWE-400), denial of service\",\n  \"bug_semantics\": \"expand_() recurses on nesting depth, but max and maxLength bound only output size, so recursion depth is unbounded up to native stack limits.\",\n  \"flaw_origin\": \"Recursive expansion implementation first shipped in commit 9a5bef783fad507aed02242ce726f89640bf42d1 (ready for first publish, Julian Gruber, 2013-10-13); the comma-member and tail recursive calls are already present there, while the initial commit 3624453f42ebf1d5fda9247ec4f87b5821051a27 has a non-recursive expand.\",\n  \"introducer_sha\": \"9a5bef783fad507aed02242ce726f89640bf42d1\",\n  \"introducer_parent\": \"3624453f42ebf1d5fda9247ec4f87b5821051a27\",\n  \"bic_granularity\": \"ATOMIC\",\n  \"bic_minimality\": \"The causal hunk is the recursive expand() body itself; its immediate parent 3624453f42eb carries a non-recursive expand, and the child 9a5bef783fad adds recursion. It is the earliest observable boundary (project first publish), not a member of a bundle.\",\n  \"bic_note\": \"9a5bef783fad is the atomic content-introducing change for the nested recursion; it is a direct single-parent commit and the earliest observable boundary for this implementation, so no pre-import origin is inferred.\",\n  \"landing_commit\": null,\n  \"landing_basis\": \"INTRODUCER_ON_DEFAULT\",\n  \"landing_note\": \"The introducer is itself a direct commit on the mainline with no separate merge/squash carrier, so no landing object is named; this is a root/content boundary rather than a decomposed bundle.\",\n  \"decomposed_shas\": [],\n  \"decomposition_probe\": \"GitHub commits API path=index.js per_page=10 pages 1-3 (evidence/idx-1.json, evidence/idx-2.json, evidence/idx-3.json): the oldest commit touching index.js is 3624453f42eb (initial commit), then 9a5bef783fad (ready for first publish), which already contains the recursive calls. Raw blob reads confirm non-recursive parent versus recursive child.\",\n  \"fix_sha\": \"935d78f32f335b2ff76578e5c5e877d31ae9888c\",\n  \"direct_fix_sha\": \"935d78f32f335b2ff76578e5c5e877d31ae9888c\",\n  \"fix_granularity\": \"ATOMIC\",\n  \"fix_shas\": [\"1efee7c397c191da6287a78ec19512476a966a7b\", \"de84f144e9816f30e25fc8179e2e1249ab6df0db\"],\n  \"fix_note\": \"935d78f32f335b2ff76578e5c5e877d31ae9888c is the mechanism-closing change on default branch main (parent df7682f386cdf2d7fef6067bc78ed70d824e1f3f, the 5.0.10 tree, src/index.ts). It threads maxDepth (EXPANSION_MAX_DEPTH=1000) into expand_ and returns a group literally past the cap, bounding both recursion sites. 1efee7c397c191da6287a78ec19512476a966a7b (parent tree 1.1.19) and de84f144e9816f30e25fc8179e2e1249ab6df0db (parent tree 3.0.7) are the other branch landings of the same repair, released as 1.1.20 and 3.0.8. Released tarball content for 5.0.11, 3.0.8, 2.1.6 and 1.1.20 contains maxDepth; 5.0.9 and 1.1.18 do not.\",\n  \"remaining_gap\": \"The 2.1.6 backport commit object is not among the advisory's three referenced SHAs and is not pinned in this record, although 2.1.6 release content contains the maxDepth fix. Full local-object carrier enumeration and --repo verification were also not run because no local clone was provisioned; they do not affect the origin or repair conclusions but would complete marker-coverage.\",\n  \"flip_condition\": \"A recovered BIC object carrying an AI marker (agent/bot author, AI co-author trailer, or first-person AI disclosure at the recursive expand() hunk) would move the verdict off NOT_AI; a fixed release whose published content lacks maxDepth, or evidence that the 1000-level cap can still be driven to stack exhaustion, would reopen repair/mechanism.\",\n  \"ai_on_bic\": false,\n  \"ai_marker\": {\"state\": \"ABSENT\", \"sha\": \"9a5bef783fad507aed02242ce726f89640bf42d1\", \"note\": \"Author Julian Gruber <julian@juliangruber.com>, 2013-10-13; message 'ready for first publish' carries no AI trailer or disclosure.\"},\n  \"fix_ai_marker\": {\"state\": \"ABSENT\", \"sha\": \"935d78f32f335b2ff76578e5c5e877d31ae9888c\", \"note\": \"Repair commits authored by Julian Gruber with GitHub as committer; no AI co-author trailer or disclosure in any of the three messages (informational only).\"},\n  \"ai_admissibility\": \"No AI evidence was admitted: the causal object carries a recovered named human identity and no marker, and the repository-level/fix-side activity is not admissible for BIC attribution. The 2013 BIC date predates AI-coding-assistant disclosure conventions, so disclosure_convention is CONVENTION_NOT_OBSERVED.\",\n  \"identity_class\": \"human\",\n  \"disclosure_convention\": \"CONVENTION_NOT_OBSERVED\",\n  \"disclosure_convention_evidence\": \"Commit 9a5bef783fad507aed02242ce726f89640bf42d1 is dated 2013-10-13, before contemporary AI-assistant disclosure conventions existed; its message has no AI section or trailer and no repository policy from that date is recorded.\",\n  \"counterevidence\": \"The earlier CVE-2026-14257 fix (iterative tail) and GHSA-6j4f-fj2g-mc7p (parseCommaParts) address different recursion shapes; payloads for those shapes do not crash, so they neither refute nor explain this defect. The 2026 later releases 5.0.12/3.0.9/2.1.7/1.1.21 add EXPANSION_MAX_REWRITES for a separate quadratic-time rewrite defect, not a residual of this stack-exhaustion mechanism.\",\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"path\": \"evidence/commit-935d78f32f335b2ff76578e5c5e877d31ae9888c.json\", \"sha256\": \"b3a07260ec759d93216748581745b1570e746fb24881dc73890ae478570ff9d8\", \"note\": \"GitHub API commit + patch: maxDepth threaded through both recursion sites (5.x, src/index.ts).\"},\n    {\"path\": \"evidence/commit-1efee7c397c191da6287a78ec19512476a966a7b.json\", \"sha256\": \"029abba4c2811a171aed8281fbdef70af625c4346fd11c8cb394f5449abc66b2\", \"note\": \"1.x landing of the same maxDepth repair; added test/ghsa-qhr7-859c-m2p7.js.\"},\n    {\"path\": \"evidence/commit-de84f144e9816f30e25fc8179e2e1249ab6df0db.json\", \"sha256\": \"a737a8b390395e241a516017823f4c4ec3303e52f93909accac921bc37f603dd\", \"note\": \"3.x landing of the same maxDepth repair.\"},\n    {\"path\": \"evidence/raw-3624453f42eb.js\", \"sha256\": \"4402e79fc632ebde9d30fb534203dea0ce0f69cc7c51a76add7aeea0520f8c5b\", \"note\": \"Initial commit index.js (1768 bytes): no recursive expand call.\"},\n    {\"path\": \"evidence/raw-9a5bef783fad.js\", \"sha256\": \"9decd38e8b8d7a413ef046e7b98a2febc33cf47d204abf74ff9bfc2bc3b471e0\", \"note\": \"First-publish index.js (2332 bytes): recursive expand on comma members and tail.\"},\n    {\"path\": \"evidence/raw-030a0f89a118.js\", \"sha256\": \"43fe1d3474a0cf0ebe6a70867ca601d900272acfb13d7f9d5f5f44b4134fde23\", \"note\": \"2014-11-30 refactor showing the single-set re-wrap recursion derived from already-recursive code.\"},\n    {\"path\": \"evidence/idx-3.json\", \"sha256\": \"4027b03cbd8daadf3ed6ca11a1918f43e16a1e849ff718b6b33b7ddc840f9b91\", \"note\": \"Oldest commits touching index.js, including 9a5bef783fad and 3624453f42eb with authors/dates.\"},\n    {\"path\": \"evidence/npm-registry.json\", \"sha256\": \"d64701dc19c759f500c7db606ff5bef33cdc1061ef16e01db42549b202d2739a\", \"note\": \"npm registry: version list, publish times, dist-tags, tarball URLs.\"},\n    {\"path\": \"evidence/tarballs/be-5.0.11.tgz\", \"sha256\": \"67bb5a1b4d4a8ff497d845a0b891ffe6b7233aea2202641315cec88d0fff15eb\", \"note\": \"Released 5.0.11 tarball: dist/commonjs/index.js contains maxDepth (9 matches).\"},\n    {\"path\": \"evidence/tarballs/be-5.0.9.tgz\", \"sha256\": \"5d06001fddd25cbee90c96db4dc5b7b57711b984c3141e28d10f143deb52dbaf\", \"note\": \"Released pre-fix 5.0.9 tarball: no maxDepth.\"},\n    {\"path\": \"evidence/tarballs/be-1.1.20.tgz\", \"sha256\": \"3a82df44a08262edd8379d063131911c6b304f09e6a2db9c22133cd5d949dba6\", \"note\": \"Released 1.1.20 tarball: index.js contains maxDepth (8 matches).\"},\n    {\"path\": \"evidence/tarballs/be-1.1.18.tgz\", \"sha256\": \"a389b049d80469568280b906a8c6d210c7f7f1d2070d3e55cff9d59b526f69a4\", \"note\": \"Released pre-fix 1.1.18 tarball: no maxDepth.\"},\n    {\"path\": \"evidence/repo.json\", \"sha256\": \"5bb6806f13f455a84f8c7e99d0928b9329f781a3d4c13bb909af7e35153a71ca\", \"note\": \"Repository metadata: default_branch main, created 2013-10-13.\"}\n  ],\n  \"reasoning\": \"The GHSA describes two nesting recursion sites; the repair patches independently show exactly those two call sites being changed and nothing else, so the mechanism is confirmed by primary code. The origin is traced to the first published recursive implementation: the initial commit has a non-recursive expand, and the first-publish commit already recurses per comma member, so the nesting-depth recursion enters at 9a5bef783fad. Origin, exposure and repair are separated: the 2026-09-14 repair is a later, distinct change from the 2013 origin. AI attribution is judged on the causal object: a recovered named human author with no marker and a pre-AI-era date yields NOT_AI, not a gap. Later 2026 releases add an unrelated rewrite cap; they do not reopen this mechanism.\",\n  \"verdict_basis\": \"Human-origin uncontrolled-recursion DoS, repaired by a maxDepth cap; no AI marker on the causal object and the BIC predates AI-assistant disclosure conventions.\",\n  \"reviewed_dimensions\": [\"advisory\", \"mechanism\", \"bic\", \"ai\", \"fix\", \"versions\", \"residual\"]\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
