s to a\n    single part (`x{{a,b}}y` -> `x{a}y x{b}y`). Deep nesting exhausted the native\n    stack and crashed the process:\ncommit 33a5ef17b8d800bbfa8c52b14c39043b6aac1a96\nAuthor:     Julian Gruber <julian@juliangruber.com>\nAuthorDate: Mon Sep 14 23:55:44 2026 +0200\nCommit:     GitHub <noreply@github.com>\nCommitDate: Mon Sep 14 23:55:44 2026 +0200\n\n    Merge commit from fork\n    \n    Bash keeps a quirk where a brace group followed by a comma set still expands\n    (`{a},b}`). The parser implements it by rewriting the string and restarting\n    the scan, absorbing one `}` per pass, so `n` trailing braces cost `n` full\n    passes:\n    \n        expand('{a}' + '}'.repeat(128000) + ',z}')\n        // 27.7 seconds, 2 results\n    \n    `ms/n^2` is flat and each doubling of `n` costs 4x - quadratic. There is a\n    second multiplier: each rewrite replaces the group's closing `}` with the\n    `escClose` sentinel, ~25 characters, so the working string grows by ~25\n    characters per pass. A 1KB input becomes 26KB, and that ~26x factor multiplies\n    both the constant and peak memory.\n    \n    Neither `max` nor `maxLength` could bound it: the cost is in parsing, before\n    the result set exists, and the payload yields two results at any size.\n    \n    Bound the number of times the scan may restart (`maxRewrites`, default\n    `EXPANSION_MAX_REWRITES`, 1000). Past the bound the remaining string is\n    treated as non-expanding and returned literally, consistent with `max` and\n    `maxLength`, which truncate rather than throw.\n    \n  \"disclosure_convention_evidence\": \"Commit 9a5bef783fad507aed02242ce726f89640bf42d1 is dated 2013-10-13, before contemporary AI-assistant disclosure conventions existed; its message has no AI section or trailer and no repository policy from that date is recorded.\",\n  \"counterevidence\": \"The earlier CVE-2026-14257 fix (iterative tail) and GHSA-6j4f-fj2g-mc7p (parseCommaParts) address different recursion shapes; payloads for those shapes do not crash, so they neither refute nor explain this defect. The 2026 later releases 5.0.12/3.0.9/2.1.7/1.1.21 add EXPANSION_MAX_REWRITES for a separate quadratic-time rewrite defect, not a residual of this stack-exhaustion mechanism.\",\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"path\": \"evidence/commit-935d78f32f335b2ff76578e5c5e877d31ae9888c.json\", \"sha256\": \"b3a07260ec759d93216748581745b1570e746fb24881dc73890ae478570ff9d8\", \"note\": \"GitHub API commit + patch: maxDepth threaded through both recursion sites (5.x, src/index.ts).\"},\n    {\"path\": \"evidence/commit-1efee7c397c191da6287a78ec19512476a966a7b.json\", \"sha256\": \"029abba4c2811a171aed8281fbdef70af625c4346fd11c8cb394f5449abc66b2\", \"note\": \"1.x landing of the same maxDepth repair; added test/ghsa-qhr7-859c-m2p7.js.\"},\n    {\"path\": \"evidence/commit-de84f144e9816f30e25fc8179e2e1249ab6df0db.json\", \"sha256\": \"a737a8b390395e241a516017823f4c4ec3303e52f93909accac921bc37f603dd\", \"note\": \"3.x landing of the same maxDepth repair.\"},\n    {\"path\": \"evidence/raw-3624453f42eb.js\", \"sha256\": \"4402e79fc632ebde9d30fb534203dea0ce0f69cc7c51a76add7aeea0520f8c5b\", \"note\": \"Initial commit index.js (1768 bytes): no recursive expand call.\"},\n    {\"path\": \"evidence/raw-9a5bef783fad.js\", \"sha256\": \"9decd38e8b8d7a413ef046e7b98a2febc33cf47d204abf74ff9bfc2bc3b471e0\", \"note\": \"First-publish index.js (2332 bytes): recursive expand on comma members and tail.\"},\n    {\"path\": \"evidence/raw-030a0f89a118.js\", \"sha256\": \"43fe1d3474a0cf0ebe6a70867ca601d900272acfb13d7f9d5f5f44b4134fde23\", \"note\": \"2014-11-30 refactor showing the single-set re-wrap recursion derived from already-recursive code.\"},\n    {\"path\": \"evidence/idx-3.json\", \"sha256\": \"4027b03cbd8daadf3ed6ca11a1918f43e16a1e849ff718b6b33b7ddc840f9b91\", \"note\": \"Oldest commits touching index.js, including 9a5bef783fad and 3624453f42eb with authors/dates.\"},\n    {\"path\": \"evidence/npm-registry.json\", \"sha256\": \"d64701dc19c759f500c7db606ff5bef33cdc1061ef16e01db42549b202d2739a\", \"note\": \"npm registry: version list, publish times, dist-tags, tarball URLs.\"},\n    {\"path\": \"evidence/tarballs/be-5.0.11.tgz\", \"sha256\": \"67bb5a1b4d4a8ff497d845a0b891ffe6b7233aea2202641315cec88d0fff15eb\", \"note\": \"Released 5.0.11 tarball: dist/commonjs/index.js contains maxDepth (9 matches).\"},\n    {\"path\": \"evidence/tarballs/be-5.0.9.tgz\", \"sha256\": \"5d06001fddd25cbee90c96db4dc5b7b57711b984c3141e28d10f143deb52dbaf\", \"note\": \"Released pre-fix 5.0.9 tarball: no maxDepth.\"},\n    {\"path\": \"evidence/tarballs/be-1.1.20.tgz\", \"sha256\": \"3a82df44a08262edd8379d063131911c6b304f09e6a2db9c22133cd5d949dba6\", \"note\": \"Released 1.1.20 tarball: index.js contains maxDepth (8 matches).\"},\n    {\"path\": \"evidence/tarballs/be-1.1.18.tgz\", \"sha256\": \"a389b049d80469568280b906a8c6d210c7f7f1d2070d3e55cff9d59b526f69a4\", \"note\": \"Released pre-fix 1.1.18 tarball: no maxDepth.\"},\n    {\"path\": \"evidence/repo.json\", \"sha256\": \"5bb6806f13f455a84f8c7e99d0928b9329f781a3d4c13bb909af7e35153a71ca\", \"note\": \"Repository metadata: default_branch main, created 2013-10-13.\"}\n  ],\n  \"reasoning\": \"The GHSA describes two nesting recursion sites; the repair patches independently show exactly those two call sites being changed and nothing else, so the mechanism is confirmed by primary code. The origin is traced to the first published recursive implementation: the initial commit has a non-recursive expand, and the first-publish commit already recurses per comma member, so the nesting-depth recursion enters at 9a5bef783fad. Origin, exposure and repair are separated: the 2026-09-14 repair is a later, distinct change from the 2013 origin. AI attribution is judged on the causal object: a recovered named human author with no marker and a pre-AI-era date yields NOT_AI, not a gap. Later 2026 releases add an unrelated rewrite cap; they do not reopen this mechanism.