{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/zsh -lc 'numactl --cpunodebind=1 --membind=1 cat sealed-auditors/auditor1/REPORT.md'","aggregated_output":"# Independent audit: alias-3389297662643f29363dbb4b\n\nTask: incremental-causal-20261004-case-047-auditor1; independent attempt 1.\nBinding: 2e4f2e902a30c3963b40979e650927cd8d8ed41c5206e10c8e3e34e0324f69ae.\nRecipient: incremental-causal-20261004T081300Z-lead. Research handoff only; no ledger, database, claims, canonical or publication writes.\nProtocol 2026-09-27.3; research contract causal-work-v1. The six-dimensional work account is [research.json](research.json).\n\n## Finding\n\n**EVIDENCE_GAP for contribution-specific AI attribution and whole-cluster causal closure.** This is not a refutation of the vulnerability. Primary code supports a real trust-boundary failure: caller-controlled connection choices can be combined with server-owned credentials. Several distinct surfaces and historical changes are grouped in the advisory. The evidence does not justify treating one advisory-listed backport or maintenance merge as an atomic introducer or universal repair.\n\nThe inspected final patch blocks previously unchecked path destinations, reconstructs bracket-form metadata before checking it, and strips configured connection credentials before a health-test request overrides connection parameters. All nine named patched source releases contain these changes and their caller wiring. This is a source-content finding, not verification of every distributed wheel/container or a proof that every credential source is isolated.\n\nThere is a material remaining distinction: the health-test repair removes deployment-dictionary credentials, but its subsequent direct SDK call still has global/environment credential fallback. At v1.96.2, the retained source supports a **static residual path** when an authorized health-test caller chooses an endpoint, supplies no key, and the process