here.\n\nThe GHSA enumerates nine separate fixed maintenance lines. The CVE's affected-version data compresses the middle range to `>= 1.89.0, < 1.96.2`, while its prose emphasizes 1.88.6 and 1.96.2. That coarse CVE interval would include intervening patched maintenance versions, so it must not overwrite the more granular GHSA ranges. Official release metadata and source contents are retained to distinguish shipment from these labels. Sources: `evidence/retained-ghsa.json`, `cve.json`, `releases.json`.\n\n## Mechanism, authority and actual callers\n\n### Request-body and destination validation\n\nAt repair mainline parent `aa1180c0c9a89157635ad3b63f84165973dba19d`, `auth_utils.py:is_request_body_safe` checks a denylist at the root, selected nested config dictionaries, metadata dictionaries, and fallback targets. The root check is not a general recursive trust separation. `_check_banned_params` has explicit operator opt-ins: `allow_client_side_credentials` and per-deployment `configurable_clientside_auth_params`. Legitimate BYOK or an explicitly allowlisted destination must be distinguished from an untrusted caller gaining server credentials.\n\nThe parent `litellm_pre_call_utils.py` destination guard examines the body; `common_request_processing.py` subsequently chooses the effective model with precedence server completion_model, CLI user_model, path model, then body model. Thus a check of the earlier body representation cannot validate the later caller-supplied path value. PR #36011 adds the shared `reject_url_valued_destination(\"model\", model)` call before that assignment, and the Azure image route receives the corresponding guard. It leaves the intentional operator-pinned model route distinct. The guard honors `provider_url_destination_allowed_hosts`. This is an actual validation-order difference, not merely a new unused helper.\n\nThe earlier validator coerces metadata values already represented as dictionaries/JSON strings; it does not reconstruct flat bracket-form keys. PR #36011 invokes the same `extract_nested_form_metadata` helper used by endpoints before applying banned-parameter checks. This binds the validation representation to the representation consumed downstream. Source-bound evidence: `auth-parent.py`, `auth-merge.py`, `pre-call-parent.py`, `common-parent.py`, `proxy-parent.py`, and the source patches in `commit-c898.json`; all release counterparts are indexed in `release-content.json`.\n\nEarlier PR #34189 addresses provider-prefixed/comma-separated destinations, recursive fallback structures, and the vertex_ai_credentials alias. `commit-065.json` shows the actual fallback traversal and depth cap; PR text is supporting context, not a substitute for that diff. Member `5818eb261d6b106a8e0110b92c82eb4bfc911f88` and landing `065faf6e695be64a1a367601271839914e4a6621` share exact auth_utils blob `ecb37e67c14aa1b601fee386d0d17d591e4729b9`. The larger changes include provider-handler edits and are not automatically atomic merely because that PR has one listed member.\n\n### Health-test credential composition\n\nThe parent health endpoint looks up a deployment by ID or model name, reads its already-resolved `litellm_params`, and merges `{**config_litellm_params, **request_litellm_params}`. An endpoint override therefore changes the destination while omitted credentials stay inherited. The endpoint then calls `ModelManagementAuthChecks.can_user_make_model_call`, applies health-check parameters, and invokes `litellm.ahealth_check`.\n\nAuthorization is consequential: the process must have a DB connection; loaded deployment model_info takes precedence in the authorization check; non-team models require PROXY_ADMIN, and team models delegate to team-admin authorization. This route is not evidence that every ordinary virtual-key user can test arbitrary global deployments. See `health-parent.py:1792`, `model-auth.py:1085`, and the release-specific `v1962-model-auth.py:1073`. The request body's direct `os.environ/` references are rejected at the inspected revision, which is contrary evidence against treating every historical environment-reading feature as still exposed unchanged.\n\nThe repaired `_config_base_for_health_check` returns a filtered base when any banned request parameter occurs, unless the operator explicitly opts into client credentials. The excluded connection set combines the router's admin-clear set, clientside credential keys and `litellm_credential_name`. The last name matters because leaving a reference to stored secrets can reintroduce the same credentials without leaving the literal key in the merged dictionary. Requests that simply name a configured model retain its connection; complete caller-owned connections remain usable. The trigger set is wider than the stripped set, so unrelated banned pricing parameters can also remove endpoint credentials; PR #36494 explicitly reports this legitimate-use regression.\n\n### Residual source of credentials\n\nIn v1.96.2, `v1962-health.py:1907` filters deployment config and then merges the request, calls authorization, and calls `litellm.ahealth_check`. `v1962-main.py:8212` dispatches through `HealthCheckHelpers`; `v1962-helpers.py` routes chat to `litellm.acompletion(**model_params)`. The OpenAI dispatch in `v1962-main.py` independently resolves the API base and resolves a missing key from `litellm.api_key`, `litellm.openai_key`, or `get_secret(\"OPENAI_API_KEY\")` (around line 2376). `openai-handler.py:370` constructs AsyncOpenAI with both api_key and base_url. Removing the deployment dictionary's key is therefore not, by itself, a prohibition on using process-owned keys at a caller-selected endpoint.\n\nThis is a conditional static inference for an authorized health-test caller and an environment/global-key configuration; it is not a claim of observed theft, a public-target result, or the same low-privilege scope as the ordinary inference-route allegation. It also explains why the blanket workaround “allow_client_side_credentials=false” is insufficient evidence of closure across every route and credential source. A further check must establish whether any active wrapper policy prevents this combination before outbound client construction, and whether all relevant installed configurations satisfy that policy. No all-version residual claim is made from one release's dispatch code.\n\n## Origin and comparison with immediate parents\n\n1. `32d0277f0374e01bea384a8c445bb1ea9e3432ba`, parent `a1f0169fc18ecf097df2194780278041f1e60b33`, changes unconditional rejection of api_base/base_url into an exception when `check_complete_credentials` returns true. Its code treats the presence of api_key as completeness for selected model families. This is an earlier weakening of the invariant; it is not established as the origin of every nested/path/health mechanism in this cluster. It names Krish Dholakia, but that alone does not prove NOT_AI for the later chain.\n2. `47214be317f45a2d2ac08258362e0481411eb427`, parent `93faf321df159b1ef65a98cc29a70958a96d184a`, adds user_config to a then-short denylist and also changes internal-context and metadata plumbing. The author display name is `user` with a GitHub-noreply account, not a recovered named identity sufficient for the protocol's NOT_AI threshold. No attribution is inferred from the subject “harden.”\n3. `0e2fcde145e9ffb27812badbc314b1c6f2af74d9`, parent `9a7831dd33115e598defe3d423e6a1c52dc0d380`, introduces deployment lookup and config-under-request merging in health tests, alongside request environment-reference resolution. The raw parent lacks that config merge; the raw child has it before the authorization and SDK call. This establishes a specific health credential-inheritance origin, not a universal cluster BIC. The earlier endpoint-creation commit `c84717c9e08564d0119607688e6da797a3777c66` accepted a model and called health checking but did not contain that deployment inheritance logic. Receipts: `health-origin.json`, `health-origin-parent.py`, `health-origin-child.py`, `health-root.json`.\n4. AI-marked `0d4875dec978e7b458336e943b89c31efa92a4a2`, parent `934ecdca78daf7ec9514efd47df77bf7495c822d`, adds nested embedding-config validation but also repairs unrelated upstream polling URL checks and carries generated UI files. Follow-up `90fd791e0d4c794bd009ca0d84c558d9ff3d604a` replaces recursive validation with fixed single-level descent and changes hostname-error disclosure. These are multi-change objects. Their marker cannot be mechanically assigned to one omitted sibling or treated as proof of first introduction. The raw auth parent/child and both patches establish the inspected scope, while PR #26996's member table and bounded ref fetch did not expose a finer member for each logical change.\n\nThe chain must still distinguish earlier origin, later exposure and incomplete remediation per actual consuming operation. There is no justified single atomic introducer covering all of these surfaces. Thus introducer_sha/bic_granularity are explicitly unknown in the record, while the observed objects remain in evidence.\n\n## AI participation\n\nThe exact commit-local marker on both 0d4875d and 90fd791 is:\n\n> Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\nIt supports AI participation in those objects. It does not distinguish validation logic, polling repair, ancillary generated content, or review activity within the bundles. The current evidence has not bound an atomic flawed/exposing contribution to that marker. The later PR #36011 body identifies Cursor Bugbot review of head `298fb8ce56099774f310594c9970cc24a9b8f900`; that is review-side evidence, not proof that an AI authored the defect or the repair.\n\nThe local carrier scan enumerated **67,350 commit objects**, including objects not reachable from a ref, and compared exact auth_utils.py blobs for four candidates. It retained 1,426 matches and their full commit metadata. A follow-up transition scan distinguishes unchanged descendant