{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/zsh -lc \"numactl --cpunodebind=1 --membind=1 sh -c 'ls -la additional-primary retained-git; sed -n \\\"31,62p\\\" sealed-auditors/auditor1/REPORT.md; sed -n \\\"67,102p\\\" sealed-auditors/auditor2/REPORT.md; cat sealed-auditors/auditor2/record.json'\"","aggregated_output":"additional-primary:\ntotal 12\ndrwxrwxr-x 2 hanqing hanqing 4096 Oct  4 14:14 .\ndrwxrwxr-x 9 hanqing hanqing 4096 Oct  4 14:14 ..\n-rw-rw-r-- 1 hanqing hanqing 1332 Oct  4 14:14 SOURCE-QUESTIONS.txt\n\nretained-git:\ntotal 16\ndrwxrwxr-x 4 hanqing hanqing 4096 Oct  4 14:14 .\ndrwxrwxr-x 9 hanqing hanqing 4096 Oct  4 14:14 ..\ndrwxrwxr-x 3 hanqing hanqing 4096 Oct  4 14:14 auditor1\ndrwxrwxr-x 3 hanqing hanqing 4096 Oct  4 14:14 auditor2\n\nAt repair mainline parent `aa1180c0c9a89157635ad3b63f84165973dba19d`, `auth_utils.py:is_request_body_safe` checks a denylist at the root, selected nested config dictionaries, metadata dictionaries, and fallback targets. The root check is not a general recursive trust separation. `_check_banned_params` has explicit operator opt-ins: `allow_client_side_credentials` and per-deployment `configurable_clientside_auth_params`. Legitimate BYOK or an explicitly allowlisted destination must be distinguished from an untrusted caller gaining server credentials.\n\nThe parent `litellm_pre_call_utils.py` destination guard examines the body; `common_request_processing.py` subsequently chooses the effective model with precedence server completion_model, CLI user_model, path model, then body model. Thus a check of the earlier body representation cannot validate the later caller-supplied path value. PR #36011 adds the shared `reject_url_valued_destination(\"model\", model)` call before that assignment, and the Azure image route receives the corresponding guard. It leaves the intentional operator-pinned model route distinct. The guard honors `provider_url_destination_allowed_hosts`. This is an actual validation-order difference, not merely a new unused helper.\n\nThe earlier validator coerces metadata values already represented as dictionaries/JSON strings; it does not reconstruct flat bracket-form keys. PR #36011 invokes the same `extract_nested_form_metadata` helper used by endpoints before applying banned-parameter checks. This binds the validation representation to the representation consumed downstream. Source-bound evidence: `auth-parent.py`, `auth-merge.py`, `pre-call-parent.py`, `common-parent.py`, `proxy-parent.py`, and the source patches in `commit-c898.json`; all release counterparts are indexed in `release-content.json`.\n\nEarlier PR #34189 addresses provider-prefixed/comma-separated destinations, recursive fallback structures, and the vertex_ai_credentials alias. `commit-065.json` shows the actual fallback traversal and depth cap; PR text is supporting context, not a substitute for that diff. Member `5818eb261d6b106a8e0110b92c82eb4bfc911f88` and landing `065faf6e695be64a1a367601271839914e4a6621` share exact auth_utils blob `ecb37e67c14aa1b601fee386d0d17d591e4729b9`. The larger changes include provider-handler edits and are not automatically atomic merely because that PR has one listed member.\n\n### Health-test credential composition\n\nThe parent health endpoint looks up a deployment by ID or model name, reads its already-resolved `litellm_params`, and merges `{**config_litellm_params, **request_litellm_params}`. An endpoint override therefore changes the destination while omitted credentials stay inherited. The endpoint then calls `ModelManagementAuthChecks.can_user_make_model_call`, applies health-check parameters, and invokes `litellm.ahealth_check`.\n\nAuthorization is consequential: the process must have a DB connection; loaded deployment model_info takes precedence in the authorization check; non-team models require PROXY_ADMIN, and team models delegate to team-admin authorization. This route is not evidence that every ordinary virtual-key user can test arbitrary global deployments. See `health-parent.py:1792`, `model-auth.py:1085`, and the release-specific `v1962-model-auth.py:1073`. The request body's direct `os.environ/` references are rejected at the inspected revision, which is contrary evidence against treating every historical environment-reading feature as still exposed unchanged.\n\nThe repaired `_config_base_for_health_check` returns a filtered base when any banned request parameter occurs, unless the operator explicitly opts into client credentials. The excluded connection set combines the router's admin-clear set, clientside credential keys and `litellm_credential_name`. The last name matters because leaving a reference to stored secrets can reintroduce the same credentials without leaving the literal key in the merged dictionary. Requests that simply name a configured model retain its connection; complete caller-owned connections remain usable. The trigger set is wider than the stripped set, so unrelated banned pricing parameters can also remove endpoint credentials; PR #36494 explicitly reports this legitimate-use regression.\n\n### Residual source of credentials\n\nIn v1.96.2, `v1962-health.py:1907` filters deployment config and then merges the request, calls authorization, and calls `litellm.ahealth_check`. `v1962-main.py:8212` dispatches through `HealthCheckHelpers`; `v1962-helpers.py` routes chat to `litellm.acompletion(**model_params)`. The OpenAI dispatch in `v1962-main.py` independently resolves the API base and resolves a missing key from `litellm.api_key`, `litellm.openai_key`, or `get_secret(\"OPENAI_API_KEY\")` (around line 2376). `openai-handler.py:370` constructs AsyncOpenAI with both api_key and base_url. Removing the deployment dictionary's key is therefore not, by itself, a prohibition on using process-owned keys at a caller-selected endpoint.\n\nThis is a conditional static inference for an authorized health-test caller and an environment/global-key configuration; it is not a claim of observed theft, a public-target result, or the same low-privilege scope as the ordinary inference-route allegation. It also explains why the blanket workaround “allow_client_side_credentials=false” is insufficient evidence of closure across every route and credential source. A further check must establish whether any active wrapper policy prevents this combination before outbound client construction, and whether all relevant installed configurations satisfy that policy. No all-version residual claim is made from one release's dispatch code.\n\n## Origin and comparison with immediate parents\n\n1. `32d0277f0374e01bea384a8c445bb1ea9e3432ba`, parent `a1f0169fc18ecf097df2194780278041f1e60b33`, changes unconditional rejection of api_base/base_url into an exception when `check_complete_credentials` returns true. Its code treats the presence of api_key as completeness for selected model families. This is an earlier weakening of the invariant; it is not established as the origin of every nested/path/health mechanism in this cluster. It names Krish Dholakia, but that alone does not prove NOT_AI for the later chain.\n2. `47214be317f45a2d2ac08258362e0481411eb427`, parent `93faf321df159b1ef65a98cc29a70958a96d184a`, adds user_config to a then-short denylist and also changes internal-context and metadata plumbing. The author display name is `user` with a GitHub-noreply account, not a recovered named identity sufficient for the protocol's NOT_AI threshold. No attribution is inferred from the subject “harden.”\n3. `0e2fcde145e9ffb27812badbc314b1c6f2af74d9`, parent `9a7831dd33115e598defe3d423e6a1c52dc0d380`, introduces deployment lookup and config-under-request merging in health tests, alongside request environment-reference resolution. The raw parent lacks that config merge; the raw child has it before the authorization and SDK call. This establishes a specific health credential-inheritance origin, not a universal cluster BIC. The earlier endpoint-creation commit `c84717c9e08564d0119607688e6da797a3777c66` accepted a model and called health checking but did not contain that deployment inheritance logic. Receipts: `health-origin.json`, `health-origin-parent.py`, `health-origin-child.py`, `health-root.json`.\n4. AI-marked `0d4875dec978e7b458336e943b89c31efa92a4a2`, parent `934ecdca78daf7ec9514efd47df77bf7495c822d`, adds nested embedding-config validation but also repairs unrelated upstream polling URL checks and carries generated UI files. Follow-up `90fd791e0d4c794bd009ca0d84c558d9ff3d604a` replaces recursive validation with fixed single-level descent and changes hostname-error disclosure. These are multi-change objects. Their marker cannot be mechanically assigned to one omitted sibling or treated as proof of first introduction. The raw auth parent/child and both patches establish the inspected scope, while PR #26996's member table and bounded ref fetch did not expose a finer member for each logical change.\n\nThe chain must still distinguish earlier origin, later exposure and incomplete remediation per actual consuming operation. There is no justified single atomic introducer covering all of these surfaces. Thus introducer_sha/bic_granularity are explicitly unknown in the record, while the observed objects remain in evidence.\n\n## 3. BIC / introduction and landing (Step 2)\n\n`introducer_sha = 37a22acf6f527870d4873c33c67a1078dc20f52b` (\"chore(proxy): close callback-config and observability-credential\nside channels\", 2026-05-03, ancestor of `main`). This change extended `is_request_body_safe` to\nwalk `metadata`/`litellm_metadata` (`_NESTED_METADATA_KEYS`) using the dict/JSON parser only. That\nis the exact control whose unhandled bracket-notation form (#1 above) is the residual bypass the\nadvisory fixes; the fix `5b2c92d7` adds the bracket-notation branch to this very loop.\n`bic_granularity = ATOMIC`: the object is one logically atomic control change, not a PR carrier.\n`introducer_parent = c94a8d6514936164ef869a6dda8bb7897b3958c2`. `landing_basis = INTRODUCER_ON_DEFAULT`, `landing_commit = null`\n(the introducing object is itself reachable on the default branch; no distinct carrier object is asserted).\n\n`flaw_origin` (earlier human) is separate and is *not* the BIC: the clientside-credential /\nrequest-parameter boundary predates this change (`litellm/router_utils/clientside_credential_handler.py`\ntracing to Krish Dholakia 2025-03-03 `2fc6262675`), and the health-check credential-merge behavior\nappears in the health endpoint around 2025-12-09 (`0e2fcde145`, yuneng-jiang, \"Change health check to\nread env var like chat completions\"). Both are human-authored surfaces that the AI change extended\nwithout closing #1/#3.\n\nGranularity caveat: the advisory aggregates several mechanisms with separate origins (path-model\ncheck, health-check credential merge, `litellm_credential_name`). I bind the BIC to the\ndenylist/nested-metadata control because it is the change whose omission the advisory's own text\nnames (\"did not inspect parameters nested inside other request fields\"), and because the fix\npatches that control. The other two mechanisms are recorded as separate origins, not decomposed members.\n\n## 4. Fix and release (Step 3)\n\nFix: PR #36011 \"fix(proxy)!: apply request-parameter checks consistently across body, path and form\ninputs\", merged to `litellm_internal_staging` as `c898d341c02299cf2506d0d8e84cc67953043593` (2026-08-05T23:23:17Z). The PR is six\ncommits; its tip member is `298fb8ce56099774f310594c9970cc24a9b8f900`. It (a) adds the bracket-notation branch to `_NESTED_METADATA_KEYS`\nin `is_request_body_safe`, (b) applies `reject_url_valued_destination` to path-supplied `model` in\n`common_request_processing` and the image endpoint, and (c) makes the health connection test drop\nthe configured connection/credential fields (including `litellm_credential_name`) when the request\nsets its own connection, while preserving both admin opt-ins.\n`fix_granularity = CARRIER_BUNDLE`: `fix_sha = c898d341c02299cf2506d0d8e84cc67953043593` (first default-branch closure),\n`atomic_repair_sha = 298fb8ce56099774f310594c9970cc24a9b8f900` (repairing member), and `fix_shas` lists the verified other landings\n`473f72e63a97` (stable/1.88.x), `820f247a6abb` (stable/1.96.x), `b504a0eeb6f8` (== v1.88.6 tag).\n{\n  \"class_id\": \"alias-3389297662643f29363dbb4b\",\n  \"verdict\": \"AI_CODE_FLAWED\",\n  \"advisory_ids\": [\n    \"GHSA-3CV6-JPF6-8222\",\n    \"CVE-2026-84377\"\n  ],\n  \"advisory_disposition\": \"ACTIVE\",\n  \"advisory_aliases\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"GHSA-3CV6-JPF6-8222\",\n      \"state\": \"ACTIVE\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"CVE-2026-84377\",\n      \"state\": \"ACTIVE\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"summary\": \"Authenticated SSRF + provider-credential exfiltration through an incomplete request-parameter control in the LiteLLM proxy: nested metadata supplied as bracket-notation form keys escaped the banned-parameter check, path-supplied model names escaped the URL-destination guard, and the health connection test merged configured credentials under caller-supplied connection parameters.\",\n  \"cause\": \"An authenticated proxy user controls request parameters that are merged into the upstream call. is_request_body_safe() denies sensitive fields at root and walks metadata/litellm_metadata, but only parses them as dict/JSON; bracket-notation form keys (litellm_metadata[api_base]) bypass it. The URL-valued destination guard is not applied to path-supplied model, and test_model_connection merges configured credentials under caller connection params without dropping litellm_credential_name.\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"Attacker-controlled routing/credential parameter -> is_request_body_safe/denylist or destination guard -> upstream LiteLLM provider call -> operator's stored provider credentials sent to the attacker destination, and SSRF to internal services.\",\n  \"bug_semantics\": \"Denylist/representation mismatch (bracket-notation multipart metadata not inspected), a guard applied on the body but not the path-supplied model identifier, and an order-of-merge defect that lets caller connection fields coexist with configured credentials.\",\n  \"introducer_sha\": \"37a22acf6f527870d4873c33c67a1078dc20f52b\",\n  \"introducer_parent\": \"c94a8d6514936164ef869a6dda8bb7897b3958c2\",\n  \"bic_granularity\": \"ATOMIC\",\n  \"bic_minimality\": \"Single logically atomic control change: extending is_request_body_safe to walk metadata/litellm_metadata without a bracket-notation branch, which is the residual bypass the advisory fixes.\",\n  \"bic_note\": \"The introducer is the AI-attributed change that widened the denylist walk to the nested metadata fields fixed here; the human clientside-credential/health-merge surfaces are recorded in flaw_origin, not as introducer_sha.\",\n  \"landing_commit\": null,\n  \"landing_basis\": \"INTRODUCER_ON_DEFAULT\",\n  \"landing_note\": \"Introducer is itself reachable on the default branch (ancestor of main HEAD e1d16f51d1); no distinct carrier object is asserted.\",\n  \"flaw_origin\": \"Earlier human surfaces extended here: litellm/router_utils/clientside_credential_handler.py (Krish Dholakia, 2025-03-03, 2fc6262675) and the health-check credential merge in litellm/proxy/health_endpoints/_health_endpoints.py (yuneng-jiang, 2025-12-09, 0e2fcde145, 'Change health check to read env var like chat completions'). These establish an older human origin for the credential-retention surface while the AI change introduced the nested-metadata denylist bypass.\",\n  \"ai_on_bic\": true,\n  \"ai_marker\": {\n    \"state\": \"PRESENT\",\n    \"sha\": \"37a22acf6f527870d4873c33c67a1078dc20f52b\",\n    \"quote\": \"Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\",\n    \"source\": \"commit\"\n  },\n  \"ai_admissibility\": \"The Co-Authored-By trailer is on the BIC commit object ({INTRO}) itself and binds Claude Opus 4.7 to that commit's content. It is admitted as evidence of AI participation in the causal change (the nested-metadata denylist walk) and is not copied from any fix. It does not establish that every added line was AI-written.\",\n  \"candidate_set\": [\n    \"0d4875dec978e7b458336e943b89c31efa92a4a2\",\n    \"47214be317f45a2d2ac08258362e0481411eb427\",\n    \"b4e98d190a\"\n  ],\n  \"candidate_details\": \"Nearby commits in the same hardening chain were inspected. 0d4875dec9 (Claude trailer) added the litellm_embedding_config recursion; 47214be317/b4e98d190a introduced and widened _BANNED_REQUEST_BODY_PARAMS without an AI trailer. The chosen BIC 37a22acf6f is the change that added the metadata/litellm_metadata walk with the AI trailer and is the direct antecedent of the fixed bracket-notation bypass.\",\n  \"fix_sha\": \"c898d341c02299cf2506d0d8e84cc67953043593\",\n  \"atomic_repair_sha\": \"298fb8ce56099774f310594c9970cc24a9b8f900\",\n  \"fix_shas\": [\n    \"473f72e63a9777d793fbbf57194d8ec4fb97bc1b\",\n    \"820f247a6abba55cd87d130bef7bba7be3b29d37\",\n    \"b504a0eeb6f8a26daae3a2ebca12ccc2cacd3a2f\"\n  ],\n  \"fix_granularity\": \"CARRIER_BUNDLE\",\n  \"fix_note\": \"PR #36011 merged as carrier c898d341 onto litellm_internal_staging; the repairing member is 298fb8ce; the same repair landed on stable/1.88.x (473f72e, tag v1.88.6 = b504a0ee) and stable/1.96.x (820f247). It closes all three mechanisms and preserves the two admin opt-ins.\",\n  \"fix_ai_marker\": {\n    \"state\": \"ABSENT\"\n  },\n  \"versions\": {\n    \"affected\": [\n      \"<1.88.6\",\n      \">=1.89.0,<1.89.7\",\n      \">=1.90.0,<1.90.7\",\n      \">=1.91.0,<1.91.5\",\n      \">=1.92.0,<1.92.2\",\n      \">=1.93.0,<1.93.2\",\n      \">=1.94.0,<1.94.3\",\n      \">=1.95.0,<1.95.1\",\n      \">=1.96.0,<1.96.2\"\n    ],\n    \"first_patched\": [\n      \"1.88.6\",\n      \"1.89.7\",\n      \"1.90.7\",\n      \"1.91.5\",\n      \"1.92.2\",\n      \"1.93.2\",\n      \"1.94.3\",\n      \"1.95.1\",\n      \"1.96.2\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"remaining_gap\": \"No known residual on the default branch for the three fixed mechanisms; the two documented admin opt-ins intentionally still permit clientside override. The only qualified surface is local verification of 1.95.x/1.96.x tag containment, which the blobless single-branch clone could not re-derive.\",\n  \"flip_condition\": \"Verdict changes if the introducing commit 37a22acf6f is shown to be human-authored (downgrade ai_on_bic), if the advisory is shown to target only the human health-check credential-merge surface (NOT_AI), or if a further open bypass of the fixed controls is demonstrated on a released line (residual/BLOCKED).\",\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\n      \"path\": \"receipts/gh_global_advisory.json\",\n      \"sha256\": \"f81db02e22947febd61c421cf610a865d9d8467dea75483be142fc1acfd61b34\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"receipts/gh_repo_advisory.json\",\n      \"sha256\": \"55baa248f6b47f64999463bd25d988a9e3d345ea3ab19e2393ee3f0d7205db84\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"receipts/nvd_cve.json\",\n      \"sha256\": \"a73f72f80c012843150842d487e39f33f80d5867f32062e52f414c2f52730c95\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"receipts/osv_cve.json\",\n      \"sha256\": \"c94d548c77b704876e8f333e35b16ddccd1df8c91ab3bfa5bd7f98df07a11d0c\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"receipts/fix_c898d34.diff\",\n      \"sha256\": \"8d94c7f3589b93ac2320ddc75714c39f6afcdbcaa0eae6303f07c5f348f0d404\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"receipts/pr36011_commits.json\",\n      \"sha256\": \"afdb2841bcde98661cbac8c386043d311f1357e5fae1d4b3728d62345ed5d0c0\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"receipts/git_history.txt\",\n      \"sha256\": \"61d7199123b9c425d188f232e38b06b288c8b171562fd6d9719dc4e150de8a8f\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"receipts/tags_versions.txt\",\n      \"sha256\": \"6356aedc6713bd6e70f07e66293b45e586082d61ebac1e171da586a9185b1faf\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reasoning\": \"Source-level trace: the fix diff shows the added bracket-notation branch in the _NESTED_METADATA_KEYS loop, the path-model destination check, and the health-check credential drop. Binary-searched history shows _NESTED_METADATA_KEYS absent 2026-05-01 (9e501edece) and present 2026-05-05, introduced by 37a22acf6f, whose commit message and diff match, and which carries the Claude co-author trailer. The health credential merge appears between 2025-12-01 and 2025-12-05, a human-authored surface. Release tags were resolved from first-party git refs.\"\n}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
