 to a module-scope scalar `_routeEventHandler`.\n- `_routeEventHandler` is assigned inside `setNotFoundHandler()` to the not-found handler of whichever\n  encapsulated instance/prefix registers **last** (\"update route event handler\"); `arrange404()` also\n  rebinds `router.onBadUrl`/`router.onMaxParamLength` to that last instance.\n- A request target that no main-router route matches but that find-my-way classifies as a bad URL\n  (or over `maxParamLength`) is dispatched through `onBadUrl`/`onMaxParamLength` **before** normal\n  routing/decoding. Because the target is a single shared pointer, the handler of the last-registered\n  (possibly sibling-prefix) plugin runs, and the bare handler is invoked directly rather than through\n  the 4-oh-4 context that carries `setNotFoundHandler`'\"'s lifecycle hooks — its declared \"'`preHandler`\n  is skipped.\n- Effect: an unauthenticated caller that can reach a public prefix can obtain the full response of an\n  authentication-protected not-found handler registered under a different prefix. CWE-288, CVSS v3.1\n  7.5 (`AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N`). No privileges, session or TLS state are required; the\n  prerequisites are a malformed/short-circuited request target and at least two encapsulated\n  `setNotFoundHandler` registrations, the protected one not registered first. A global `onRequest`\n  hook does not mitigate because this path skips the normal lifecycle.\n\nCounterevidence / competing explanations checked:\n\n- The flaw is the **shared handler pointer**, not `onBadUrl` dispatch per se. Before\n  `39dbc80c` the 404 router'\"'s \"'`onBadUrl` was a self-contained local responder that wrote a generic\n  404 body and never invoked an application handler (parent tree inspected, `evidence/receipts.txt`),\n  so that ancestor behavior is not this defect.\n- `1c499748` later extended the *same* shared pointer to `onMaxParamLength`, widening the trigger\n  surface but not originating the encapsulation break (see §3/§5).\n\n## 3. Introduction (BIC)\n\n- `introducer_sha` = `39dbc80c58ac81d4d7a65a680a74514870e57905`; `introducer_parent` =\n  `650531f1c6c969e50145a1303ea050afb85f8571` (verified parent link).\n- Object: `refactor: update 404 onBadUrl behavior (#3813)`, author `KaKa <kaka@kakawebsitedemo.com>`,\n  committer `GitHub <noreply@github.com>`, dated 2022-04-02 (squash of PR #3813).\n- Its diff replaces `FindMyWay({ onBadUrl, ... })` with\n  `FindMyWay({ onBadUrl: createOnBadUrl(), ... })` plus `let _onBadUrlHandler = null`, and replaces the\n  local responder with a dispatcher calling `_onBadUrlHandler(request, reply)`, set to the last\n  not-found handler. Parent/child comparison shows the vulnerable dispatch is absent in the parent and\n  present in the child → this is the introduction.\n- Content-locating probe: `git log -S '\"'_onBadUrlHandler'\"'` (all refs) returns exactly `39dbc80c` (introduces)\n  and `1c499748` (renames/extends), confirming the earliest content transition.\n- `bic_granularity` = **ATOMIC**: a single-parent squash whose `lib/fourOhFour.js` hunk is one coherent\n  behavioral change (route bad-URL handling through a shared, last-writer-wins application handler);\n  the commit'\"'s test additions are collateral. It is not a decomposition of a larger bundle.\n- Landing: the introducer is itself on the default branch (\"'`origin/main`) → `landing_basis`\n  `INTRODUCER_ON_DEFAULT`, `landing_commit` null (no distinct carrier object).\n- Confirmed present in the earliest 4.x release: `merge-base --is-ancestor 39dbc80c v4.0.0` = true, and\n  `v4.0.0:lib/fourOhFour.js` contains `_onBadUrlHandler` — matching the advisory'\"'s \"'`>= 4.0.0` range.\n- Later exposure (kept separate from introduction): `1c49974805e57a17f1616f422678317b1d35b73f`\n  (`feat: add support of onMaxParamLength (#6716)`, human author KaKa) renamed the pointer to\n  `_routeEventHandler` and wired the same shared dispatch to `onMaxParamLength`, reaching release\n  v5.10.0 and later. This widened the trigger path; it did not create the origin.\n\n## 4. Repair and release\n\n- `fix_sha` = `d66ebd121c5a621ab0b03569fc75bd1cf829e52a` (`fix: reject malformed URLs before custom 404\n  handlers`, Matteo Collina, on `origin/main`). The repair sets\n  `onBadUrl: options.routerOptions.onBadUrl` and `onMaxParamLength: options.routerOptions.onMaxParamLength`,\n  deletes `createRouteEventHandler()` and `_routeEventHandler`, and removes the per-instance bindings.\n  It closes the mechanism because malformed request targets now go to the framework-level\n  `onBadUrl`/`onMaxParamLength` handlers (fail-closed) before any application not-found handler runs,\n  and the shared cross-prefix pointer no longer exists.\n- Other landing of the identical repair: `93c239a380f3f2778bb7565fcdf777e91cfe1fbc` on `5.x`\n  (the commit the advisory cites). Both produce the **same** `lib/four-oh-four.js` blob\n  `d994fd4309c64d5c391a9a853f186020d0b1c836` (content identity verified).\n- `fix_granularity` = **ATOMIC** (the fix object is the repair).\n- Release membership verified by content, not ancestry alone:\n  `v5.12.2:lib/four-oh-four.js` = `d994fd43…` (fixed) and `v5.12.1:lib/four-oh-four.js` =\n  `b9f727f05a4cead79dbed19bc03516d2f8b04387` (vulnerable); `93c239a3` is an ancestor of `v5.12.2`\n  but not of `v5.12.1`. This substantiates `first_patched_version = 5.12.2`.\n- Residual (partial repair): the advisory range includes `>= 4.0.0`. The 4.x line was never fixed —\n  the latest 4.x release `v4.29.1` still contains the shared `_onBadUrlHandler` pointer. So the repair\n  closes the 5.x line only; 4.x installations remain vulnerable with no released fix.\n\n## 5. AI participation\n\n- BIC object `39dbc80c`: author `KaKa <kaka@kakawebsitedemo.com>` (named human, a fastify\n  maintainer), committer GitHub noreply. Commit message and identity carry **no AI marker**. Full\n  metadata read; no trailer other than none.\n- Local-object coverage: `git log --all --find-object=e6e828046ccb5e3b312e89dd76f1f4dcb4c44c8d`\n  (the blob introduced by `39dbc80c`) returns only `39dbc80c` and `3fd294db` (a later human\n  `errorHandler` edit), i.e. no AI-attributed local commit carries that blob.\n  `git fsck --unreachable` is empty — no dangling/local-only iteration of the causal commit.\n- Later-exposure object `1c499748`: human author KaKa with human co-author `jean-michelet`; no AI marker.\n- Fix objects `d66ebd12` / `93c239a3`: human (Matteo Collina); no AI marker.\n- Disclosure-convention spot check at the BIC date: a scan of all 5141 local commit objects finds\n  **12** commits with AI markers (\"Generated with Claude Code\", `Co-Authored-By: Claude`,\n  \"Copilot Autofix\"), **earliest 2025-07-02** — none near 2022. At the BIC date (2022-04-02) the\n  repository had no AI-disclosure convention: `CONVENTION_NOT_OBSERVED`.\n- One AI-attributed commit touches `lib/four-oh-four.js`: `b7514b22` (2025-11-02, Claude marker).\n  Its diff only adds optional chaining to logger calls (`request.log?.info`, `childLogger?.info`,\n  `request.log?.warn`); `git log -S '\"'_routeEventHandler' b7514b22\"'` returns nothing, so it neither\n  introduces nor alters the shared-pointer hunk. It adds no causal exposure.\n- `ai_admissibility`: the only AI evidence is off-BIC (`b7514b22` logging refactor) and is not bound to\n  the introducer, later exposure or repair; it is not admissible for attribution. `ai_on_bic = false`.\n- Conclusion: the defect'\"'s introducer, its later exposure and its repair are all human-authored with no\n  AI marker on the causal objects, with a contemporaneous convention check. Verdict **NOT_AI**.\n\n## 6. Versions and residual\n\n- Vulnerable: \"'`>= 4.0.0, < 5.12.2` for the `onBadUrl` path; the `onMaxParamLength` trigger only exists\n  from v5.10.0 onward (via `1c499748`).\n- Fixed: `5.12.2` (content-verified). `5.12.1` is vulnerable.\n- Residual: 4.x (`>= 4.0.0`, latest `v4.29.1`) still carries the shared pointer; no 4.x fix object or\n  release exists. Recorded in `remaining_gap`.\n\n## 7. Flip condition\n\nThe NOT_AI verdict would change if (a) a local or reachable commit carrying the exact introduced blob/hunk\n(`_onBadUrlHandler` dispatch) were found with an AI marker, (b) a first-party disclosure bound the\nintroducing change to an AI tool, or (c) an AI-attributed change were shown to add the exposure path.\nThe residual would change if a 4.x backport were found outside the cloned refs.\n\n## 8. Checks and execution\n\n- Local-object and structural gates run with the frozen `contract/scripts/audit_record_gates.py`\n  (`--strict`, and `--strict --repo <clone>`); commands, exits and raw output retained in\n  `evidence/checks.txt`.\n- Checks **not** run: no ledger/Neon write, no live target interaction, no exploit execution (prohibited\n  by the binding). No `compare_slots.py`/`audit_reduction_gate.py` delivery run (leader/adjudicator\n  scope). `--repo` covers commit parents/quote shape only; it does not prove causality or AI absence.\nEOF\nwc -l $OUT/REPORT.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
