`scratch/v2101`, `scratch/v2130`, `scratch/v2140` from `git archive` of the
official tags; `scratch/check_rec.py` sends one unsigned token with a
200,000-deep `[` header and inspects the exception type at the public
`jwt.decode(..., algorithms=[...])` boundary):

- 2.13.0 (`evidence/regression_2.13.0.txt`): `RecursionError escaped: maximum
  recursion depth exceeded while decoding a JSON array` - not a `PyJWTError`.
- 2.14.0 (`evidence/regression_2.14.0.txt`): `PyJWTError: DecodeError Invalid
  header string: maximum recursion depth exceeded ...` - mechanism closed.
- 2.10.1 (`evidence/regression_2.10.1.txt`): also `RecursionError escaped`.

Counterevidence / scope check: the published range starts at 2.13.0, but the
same escape reproduces on 2.10.1 and 2.12.1, consistent with the advisory's own
text ("every version whose `PyJWS._load()` translates only `ValueError` is
affected"). The GHSA range understates the affected set; see Residual/versions.
No opposing explanation survived: the failure is exception-type translation in
`_load`, not signature verification, key handling or base64 decoding.

## Step 2 - BIC, landing and exposure

The flawed pattern is: header JSON parsing of untrusted input guarded only
against `ValueError`/`TypeError`, so `RuntimeError`/`RecursionError` escapes.
Parent/child comparison of the later code transitions shows the defect is not
introduced by the commits that are easiest to blame:

- `0e8dcb3` "Add decode segment errors" (2013-05-15, Andy Zhang
  `<andy@livefyre.com>`) gave the header parse its own `except ValueError as e`
  (`evidence/0e8dcb3_diff.txt`). Its **immediate parent** already parsed the
  header with `json.loads` inside `except (ValueError, TypeError)`, which also
  does not catch `RecursionError`. RecursionError behavior is identical in
  parent and child, so `0e8dcb3` is an error-granularity refactor, not the
  introducer (protocol step 2: compare immediate parent and child).
- `5f255f9` (2015-01-18) and `a3f2ec3` (2015-04-19) only **moved** the code
  (`jwt/__init__.py` -> `jwt/api.py` -> `jwt/api_jws.py`); `git blame` at
  `0657369^` attributes the `except ValueError` line to the move `a3f2ec3`.
  Moves carry content, they do not introduce it.

The earliest observable causal boundary is the repository **root commit**
`ab8821fefa1c481ece410305d03a1f21c0b4a207` (2011-02-24, Jeff Lindsay
`<progrium@gmail.com>`, "initial commit"; `evidence/bic_ab8821f_catfile.txt`),
whose `decode()` is:

```python
try:
    header = json.loads(base64url_decode(header_segment))
    payload = json.loads(base64url_decode(payload_segment))
    signature = base64url_decode(crypto_segment)
except (ValueError, TypeError):
    raise DecodeError("Invalid segment encoding")
```

`json.loads` on the untrusted header is already inside a handler that excludes
`RuntimeError`/`RecursionError`, so the RecursionError escape is present at the
root and is not observable anywhere earlier (`evidence/bic_ab8821f_jwt_init.txt`).
`bic_granularity = ATOMIC`, root-boundary case: the causal hunk is the header
`json.loads` inside the narrow `except` in `decode()`; it is the minimal
observable unit because no behavior-changing predecessor exists. There is no
separate merge/squash carrier (`landing_commit = null`,
`landing_basis = NO_LANDING_NAMED`); pre-root history and pre-root authorship
are not inferred from the boundary.

Carriers of the causal content (pickaxe `-S "except ValueError as e"` across
`jwt/api_jws.py jwt/api.py jwt/__init__.py`,
`evidence/pickaxe_except_valueerror.txt`): `ab8821f`, `0e8dcb3`, `5f255f9`,
`a3f2ec3`, plus typing/traceback commits. All are human-authored pre-2026
objects; none carries an AI marker.

## Step 3 - repair and release

- **Fix:** `06573692ebcdec8831c3927513b3e87c31fbbb62` "Handle recursive JWS
  headers as decode errors" (2026-09-09, José Padilla
  `<jpadilla@users.noreply.github.com>`, SSH-signed). Single one-line hunk:
  `except ValueError as e:` -> `except (ValueError, RecursionError) as e:` in
  `jwt/api_jws.py::_load`, plus a regression test
  (`evidence/fix_0657369_diff.txt`). This is the first mechanism-closing change
  on the default branch (immediate parent `e6f4840`), an ancestor of
  `origin/HEAD`, and it is contained in tags `2.14.0`, `2.15.0`, `2.15.1`
  (`evidence/tags_containing_fix.txt`). `fix_granularity = ATOMIC`.
- **Release:** tag `2.14.0` contains the fixed hunk
  (`except (ValueError, RecursionError)`; `2.14.0:jwt/api_jws.py`); `2.13.0`
  still has `except ValueError` (`git show 2.13.0:jwt/api_jws.py`). Affected
  scope per advisory `>= 2.13.0, < 2.14.0`; empirically the escape also exists
  below 2.13.0.
- **Related sibling (not a residual of this advisory):** the payload parse in
  `jwt/api_jwt.py::_decode_payload` had the same `except ValueError` pattern
  and was normalized to `(ValueError, RecursionError)` later, on 2026-09-22 in
  `5fde08a` (first released in `2.15.0`). That path parses the payload only
  after signature verification, so it is a distinct exposure, not an unclosed
  part of the unauthenticated header mechanism. Recorded as related hardening
  for the adjudicator; it does not move the verdict.

## Step 4 - AI participation (NOT_AI)

- BIC object `ab8821f` (2011) carries no AI marker; author and committer are the
  named human Jeff Lindsay `<progrium@gmail.com>` with a resolvable identity
  (`identity_class = HUMAN`).
- All other causal carriers (`0e8dcb3`, `5f255f9`, `a3f2ec3`, ...) and the fix
  `0657369` are human-authored with no `Co-authored-by`/`Generated-by` AI
  trailer (full metadata retained in `evidence/`; fix commit body is only the
  subject line, `evidence/fix_0657369_catfile.txt`).
- The repository does have an AI disclosure practice from 2026 (Claude-authored
  commit `74961f1` 2026-02-03, and `Co-authored-by: Claude` trailers on other
  2026 commits; `evidence/ai_commit_scan.txt`,
  `evidence/claude_commit_74961f1.txt`), but none of it touches the 2011 BIC or
  its carriers. That repo-level AI activity is **inadmissible** as
  attribution to this BIC (`ai_admissibility`).
- `disclosure_convention = CONVENTION_NOT_OBSERVED` scoped to the BIC date
  (2011): no AI disclosure convention existed to be used or omitted.

## Step 5 - evidence, residual and flip condition

- Decisive receipts are the raw files under `evidence/` (clone history,
  pickaxe, fix diff/cat-file, root commit, tag set, and the three isolated
  regression runs) with SHA-256 hashes recorded in `research.json`.
- **Residual / versions:** no code surface of the reported header mechanism
  remains open. The one qualified boundary is advisory scope: the published
  range `>= 2.13.0` understates the affected set (2.10.1 / 2.12.1 also escape).
  This is a version-range metadata cue, adjudicated NOT-RESIDUAL for the code
  defect; no additional closing commit is needed because the single 2.14.0 fix
  closes all of them once released.
- **flip_condition:** the verdict would change if the BIC object or a causal
  carrier were shown to carry a first-party AI disclosure bound to the flawed
  hunk (e.g. an AI co-author/bot identity on `ab8821f` or on a commit that
  changed the header `except` clause), or if a later exposure point were
  introduced by an AI-assisted change. Ordinal dates do not.
- Not run / limits: the strict `--repo` local-object check was run against the
  scratch clone (see Submission). No exploit was executed; the regression check
  is a bounded ordinary call at the public `jwt.decode` boundary in owned
  scratch. No live target was contacted.
