## 0. Advisory status

The keyed GHSA is live: the captured GitHub global advisory has a valid
`published_at` (2026-09-30T23:40:12Z) and `withdrawn_at: null`, so
`advisory_disposition` = ACTIVE. CVE-2026-102983 is published (2026-09-30) and
not rejected. Both identifiers describe one vulnerability at different label
levels, so they are not a false-positive duplicate pair. No statement in either
advisory contradicts its label; the mechanism is a real permissive-regex/SSRF
defect (CWE-625 + CWE-918). Receipt: R1 (`evidence/R1_input_hashes.txt`).

## 1. Reported behavior, mechanism and scope

`@astrojs/netlify` writes a Netlify Image CDN deploy config (`config.json`, key
`remote_images`) built from Astro's `image.domains` and `image.remotePatterns`.
For every configured domain it emitted the regular expression
`https?:\/\/<escaped-domain>\/.*` and for each remote pattern it built
`remotePatternToRegex(...)`, whose accumulator starts as `let regexStr = '';`.
Both expressions are unanchored at the start. Netlify evaluates each entry with
`RegExp.test()`, which returns true if the approved origin matches **anywhere**
in a candidate URL. An unauthenticated request to the public `/.netlify/images`
endpoint with a source such as
`http://127.0.0.1:6379/?url=https://images.example.com/image.png` therefore
passes the allowlist (the approved origin appears in the query string) while the
URL Netlify actually parses and fetches has the attacker-selected host. Impact is
bounded SSRF / internal-service probing: the transform pipeline limits direct
response exfiltration, and Netlify network egress protections may constrain
reachable targets. This matches the advisory and is confirmed by the fixing
commit, whose added regression test asserts both generated regexes reject
`http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/?url=https://example.net/image.jpg` and
`http://127.0.0.1:6379/?url=https://www.example.org/images/a.jpg`.
Receipts: R4 (`evidence/R4_bic_vuln_source.txt`), R5 (`evidence/R5_fix_diff.txt`).

Prerequisite/authority: the victim application must enable the Netlify Image CDN
(not `imageCDN: false`) with at least one `image.domains` or
`image.remotePatterns` entry, and the `/.netlify/images` endpoint must be
publicly reachable. The attacker needs no privileges or session.

