ure; this is not an inference from its three-file size or single parent.\n+\n+Member 1ee83035 and 3.x landing e7d7e59e have exactly the same `lib/fourOhFour.js` blob, **`6397712984b818e8939617fa2c39ef400b340354`**. The record uses SQUASH_DECOMPOSED to preserve the reconstructed aggregate and names the actual introducing member rather than attributing the aggregate'\"'s earlier safe work. The 3.x landing is explicitly not called a default-branch landing.\n+\n+The later main/v4 member d794367a has parent **\"'`650531f1c6c969e50145a1303ea050afb85f8571`**, where the fixed-response behavior likewise precedes its direct callback dispatch. Its `lib/fourOhFour.js` blob **`e6e828046ccb5e3b312e89dd76f1f4dcb4c44c8d`** matches landing 39dbc80c exactly. PR 3813 calls this a follow-up providing the same v3 behavior. The causally relevant function/assignment diff matches the earlier member, with version-specific surrounding context. This is an import of the previously introduced defect onto the main/v4 line, not evidence that the 2026 repair introduced it.\n+\n+The original member declares find-my-way ''^4.5.0; main/v4 member declares ''^5.1.0; v4.0.0 declares ''^6.3.0. I inspected these exact dependency versions rather than borrowing 9.6.0'\"'s implementation. All have the method-tree early return. In 4.5.0, decoding occurs as param/wildcard values are consumed, and \"'`_onBadUrl()` still calls the supplied global callback rather than the prefix-selected route handler. The original Fastify caller also forwards to the internal 404 router and registers all-method prefix/wildcard routes. Thus the earlier origin is causally supported at its own version, even though the official affected range starts at 4.0.0.\n+\n+Evidence: `origin-diff.txt`, `v3-origin.txt`, `causal-members.txt`, `origin-caller-proof.txt`, `bounded-pr-fetch.txt`, `v3-members.body`, `origin-members.body`, `source-comparisons.json`, and full parent/child snapshots.\n+\n+## Later changes and AI attribution\n+\n+The introducing member'\"'s exact identity is \"'`KaKa <kaka@kakawebsitedemo.com>`, with the same committer. The recovered PR and first-party account bind the contribution to `climba03003` (profile name KaKa; the product'\"'s later contributors list identifies KaKa Ng). No AI trailer or disclosure appears on either 2022 causal member. A contemporaneous spot check covers repository commit metadata from 2022-03-15 through 2022-04-15, plus the origin PR checklist. Human/DCO and Dependabot disclosures appear; an AI disclosure convention is not observed. This supports \"'`identity_class: human` and `disclosure_convention: CONVENTION_NOT_OBSERVED`, rather than treating every bot as an AI author or claiming undisclosed assistance is impossible.\n+\n+Carrier coverage is explicit. After member recovery, `git cat-file --batch-all-objects --batch-check` enumerated **5,147 local commit objects**, independent of reachability. Each commit was read for full metadata and both historical 404 paths; **37 distinct blobs** were inspected. **1,818 commits** carry either the original `_onBadUrlHandler(request, reply)` hunk or its `_routeEventHandler(request, reply)` continuation. The full matches and metadata are retained, including original member objects that were absent from the initial 5,141-object clone. These are source-content observations, not just `git log -S` transitions. The broad first marker search also hit signature bytes; the focused review excludes signatures and inspects identities/messages and causal diffs.\n+\n+Several unchanged descendants contain real AI markers on unrelated contributions. `ai-candidate-review.json` retains the full marked metadata, changed filenames and relevant diffs. In particular, `2ae3e1d7...` validates configured log levels, `5f4871f9...` optimizes logging/response/validation overhead, and `b7514b22...` changes optional logger calls. None adds the shared pointer, changes prefix selection to that pointer, bypasses authentication hooks, or claims to rem