{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/zsh -lc \"numactl --cpunodebind=1 --membind=1 python3 -c 'import json,re; records=json.load(open(\\\"output/alias-360123c1550c32ce5077e4bc/evidence/marker-candidates.json\\\")); [(print(row[\\\"sha\\\"],row[\\\"metadata\\\"].split(\\\"\\\\n\\\\n\\\",1)[-1])) for row in records if re.search(\\\"claude|codex|copilot|llm|ai.assist\\\",row[\\\"metadata\\\"].split(\\\"\\\\n\\\\n\\\",1)[-1],re.I)]'\"","aggregated_output":"1451d70eca2059bc472703692f0bb0777bc0fe93 fix: do not store reference to algorithms dict on PyJWK (#1143)\n\n* fix: do not store reference to algorithms dict on PyJWK\n\n* Update CHANGELOG.rst\n\nCo-authored-by: Copilot <175728472+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Asif Saif Uddin {\"Auvi\":\"অভি\"} <auvipy@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Copilot <175728472+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>\n384c945065ad1f1d7e5a0331c7377c7f278532f5 fix: return a parsed PyJWKSet from the JWK Set cache (#1208)\n\n* fix: return cached PyJWKSet from PyJWKClient.get_jwk_set\n\n`JWKSetCache.put()` documents `PyJWKSet` as the cached value, so callers\ncan pre-populate the JWK Set cache (e.g. from an externally stored JWKS)\nto skip the network round-trip. `get_jwk_set()` only accepted a `dict`\nfrom the cache, so a cache hit holding a `PyJWKSet` raised a misleading\n`PyJWKClientError(\"The JWKS endpoint did not return a JSON object\")`,\neven though no endpoint call was involved.\n\nReturn cached `PyJWKSet` values directly, and widen the cache type hints\nto `PyJWKSet | dict` so they describe what is actually stored -\n`fetch_data()` caches the raw JSON payload it fetched, while callers\npopulating the cache themselves store a `PyJWKSet`. Other invalid cache\nvalues still raise `PyJWKClientError` as before.\n\nFixes #914\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Vat3P5EcKjHcRUzSCTtyyu\n\n* refactor: always store a parsed PyJWKSet in the JWK Set cache\n\nThe previous commit made `get_jwk_set()` tolerate either a `PyJWKSet` or\na `dict` coming back from the cache, which fixed #914 but left the cache\nitself holding whichever type the caller happened to put in.\n\nNormalize on write instead: `JWKSetCache.put()` accepts a `PyJWKSet` or\nthe raw JWKS payload and always stores it parsed, so `get()` is typed and\nbehaves as `Optional[PyJWKSet]` with no union leaking to callers. Cache\nhits no longer re-parse (and re-load through `cryptography`) every key,\nat the cost of returning one shared `PyJWKSet` instance while it stays\ncached.\n\nParsing the payload on write hid the endpoint-response check behind a\nvaguer \"Invalid JWK Set value\", so that check moves into `fetch_data()`,\nnext to the response it is actually about. Callers going through\n`get_jwk_set()` see the same error as before; a direct `fetch_data()`\ncall now raises instead of returning a non-object payload.\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Vat3P5EcKjHcRUzSCTtyyu\n\n* docs: link PR #1208 from the changelog entries\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Vat3P5EcKjHcRUzSCTtyyu\n\n* test: cover clearing the JWK Set cache with put(None)\n\nRestructuring `put()` made codecov count its cache-clearing branch as a\nchanged line, and it had no test. Clearing the cache is part of the\npublic behavior, so cover it rather than exempt it.\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Vat3P5EcKjHcRUzSCTtyyu\n\n* fix: cache the key set get_jwk_set() actually returns\n\n`fetch_data()` caches the payload it received from the endpoint, but a\nsubclass may filter or transform that payload before returning it. Since\n`put()` now parses on write, the cache kept a snapshot taken before the\nsubclass ran, and the next cache hit served the pre-transform key set —\na key the subclass had removed came back.\n\nOn master this happened to work for an override that mutates the dict in\nplace, because the cache held a reference to that same dict; an override\nreturning a new dict was already broken. Cache the value `get_jwk_set()`\nis about to return instead, which fixes both.\n\nAlso makes the returned set and the cached set the same object on the\nfetch path, so repeated calls are identical rather than merely equal.\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Vat3P5EcKjHcRUzSCTtyyu\n\n* fix: treat malformed JWK Set members as unusable keys\n\n`PyJWKSet` skips keys it cannot use and raises `PyJWKSetError` when none\nremain, but two malformed inputs escaped that contract with exceptions\nthat are not `PyJWTError`, so callers catching PyJWT's own exceptions saw\nan uncaught crash instead:\n\n- a member that is not a JSON object (`{\"keys\": [null]}`) reached\n  `PyJWK.__init__` and raised `AttributeError`\n- a key whose components have the wrong type\n  (`{\"kty\": \"RSA\", \"n\": 1, \"e\": \"AQAB\"}`) raised `TypeError` out of\n  `Algorithm.from_jwk`, which the surrounding handler only caught for\n  `ValueError`\n\nBoth are reachable from `PyJWKClient`, whose payload comes straight from\na JWKS endpoint. Skip non-object members like any other unusable key, and\nwiden the `from_jwk` handler to `TypeError` so a bad component type\nbecomes `InvalidKeyError`. A single bad entry no longer fails an\notherwise usable JWK Set, and a set with nothing usable left raises\n`PyJWKSetError`.\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Vat3P5EcKjHcRUzSCTtyyu\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: José Padilla <jpadilla@users.noreply.github.com>\n412cb67a93363812ae4029d6a95f5d4d40ab2609 fix: close HTTPError to prevent ResourceWarning on Python 3.14 (#1133)\n\n* fix: close HTTPError response to prevent ResourceWarning on Python 3.14\n\nOn Python 3.14, urllib.error.HTTPError objects that are not explicitly\nclosed produce a ResourceWarning during garbage collection. Since\nHTTPError is both a URLError subclass and a response-like object\n(inheriting from tempfile._TemporaryFileWrapper via addinfourl), it\nmust be closed to release the underlying resource.\n\nThis adds an explicit e.close() call in the except handler of\nPyJWKClient.fetch_data() when the caught exception is an HTTPError.\n\nFixes #1128\n\n* [pre-commit.ci] auto fixes from pre-commit.com hooks\n\nfor more information, see https://pre-commit.ci\n\n* fix: use BytesIO for HTTPError fp in test to fix Python 3.9 compatibility\n\nOn Python 3.9, HTTPError(fp=None).close() triggers a KeyError in\ntempfile.SpooledTemporaryFile.__getattr__ because the internal 'file'\ndict key doesn't exist. Using io.BytesIO(b\"\") as the fp argument\nprovides a valid file-like object that can be closed on all Python\nversions.\n\nAlso adds CHANGELOG.rst entry for the HTTPError close fix.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.6 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.6 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n74961f165765300dc6b366c6c2b3dc0d11b92bde fix: preserve microsecond precision in JWT time claims (exp, iat, nbf)\n\nPer RFC 7519, NumericDate values are JSON numbers representing seconds\nsince epoch, and \"non-integer values can be represented.\" PyJWT was\ntruncating sub-second precision in two places:\n\n1. Encoding: `timegm(dt.utctimetuple())` discards microseconds since\n   `utctimetuple()` only returns whole seconds. Replaced with\n   `datetime.timestamp()` which preserves full precision.\n\n2. Validation: `int(payload[\"exp\"])` (and iat/nbf) truncated any\n   fractional seconds in tokens from external issuers. Changed to\n   `float()` conversion to accept RFC-compliant non-integer values.\n\nFixes #1126\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01RmtQWAc1ZegeGNZq2ojN92\n\n793a30279584fcf2db889363b0db1f6d38d74e97 fix: raise PyJWTError (not TypeError) for non-numeric exp/nbf/iat claims (#1186)\n\n* fix: raise PyJWTError instead of TypeError for non-numeric exp/nbf/iat\n\n_validate_iat/_validate_nbf/_validate_exp call int(payload[claim]) and\nonly catch ValueError, which int() raises for non-numeric strings. A\nlist, dict, or null claim value makes int() raise TypeError instead,\nwhich leaks out uncaught -- so a crafted token crashes any caller that\nonly handles jwt.PyJWTError, bypassing the exception contract.\n\nBroaden the handlers to except (ValueError, TypeError) so these values\nraise the intended DecodeError / InvalidIssuedAtError.\n\n* Potential fix for pull request finding\n\nCo-authored-by: Copilot Autofix powered by AI <175728472+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>\n\n* fix: catch overflow in numeric claim validation\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Asif Saif Uddin {\"Auvi\":\"অভি\"} <auvipy@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Copilot Autofix powered by AI <175728472+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>\na4e1a3d1218b01c5806420b8f16d9308ac4adc30 Add typing_extensions dependency for Python < 3.11 (#1151)\n\n* Add typing_extensions as dependency for Python < 3.11\n\nPyJWT imports typing_extensions.Never (Python < 3.11) and\ntyping_extensions.TypeAlias (Python < 3.10) at runtime in\njwt/algorithms.py, but typing_extensions was never declared as a\ndependency.  This causes a ModuleNotFoundError on Python 3.9/3.10\nwhen importing the library.\n\nFixes #1150\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_013ZvbKEyVkcUx8xHrqt8v2F\n\n* [pre-commit.ci] auto fixes from pre-commit.com hooks\n\nfor more information, see https://pre-commit.ci\n\n* Add minimal install CI job to catch undeclared dependencies\n\nThe existing install-dev job always installs with [dev] extras, which\nbundles cryptography and pulls in typing_extensions transitively via\ntest tooling. This masked the missing typing_extensions dependency\n(#1150).\n\nAdd an install-minimal job that tests bare `pip install .` and\n`pip install .[crypto]` on Python 3.9 and 3.13, ensuring undeclared\nruntime dependencies are caught immediately.\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_013ZvbKEyVkcUx8xHrqt8v2F\n\n* Merge install-minimal into install-dev and test all install modes\n\nConsolidate into a single job that tests progressively: bare install,\ncrypto extra, then dev extras. Tests Python 3.9 and 3.13 to cover\nboth the typing_extensions fallback and native typing paths.\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_013ZvbKEyVkcUx8xHrqt8v2F\n\n* Bump version to 2.12.1\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_013ZvbKEyVkcUx8xHrqt8v2F\n\n* Add explicit permissions block to CI workflow\n\nRestrict GITHUB_TOKEN to read-only contents access, as flagged by\ngithub-advanced-security[bot].\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_013ZvbKEyVkcUx8xHrqt8v2F\n\n* Fix RST underline length in CHANGELOG\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_013ZvbKEyVkcUx8xHrqt8v2F\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-authored-by: pre-commit-ci[bot] <66853113+pre-commit-ci[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>\nbd9700cca7f9258fadcc429c1034e508025931f2 Use PyJWK algorithm when encoding without explicit algorithm (#1148)\n\n* fix: use PyJWK key algorithm when encoding without explicit algorithm (#1147)\n\nWhen a PyJWK object is passed to jwt.encode() without specifying an\nalgorithm, the key's embedded algorithm is now used instead of\ndefaulting to HS256. This is achieved by using a sentinel default value\nso the code can distinguish \"no algorithm specified\" from an explicit\nalgorithm parameter.\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_016Ekc2jQzpuiDpBvnMAMnUB\n\n* [pre-commit.ci] auto fixes from pre-commit.com hooks\n\nfor more information, see https://pre-commit.ci\n\n* update changelog\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-authored-by: pre-commit-ci[bot] <66853113+pre-commit-ci[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>\nCo-authored-by: José Padilla <jpadilla@users.noreply.github.com>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
