{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/zsh -lc \"numactl --cpunodebind=1 --membind=1 sh -c 'cat inputs/00-github.json; cat inputs/01-nvd.json; wc -l contract/docs/AUDIT-PROTOCOL.md contract/docs/DATA-SCHEMA.md contract/scripts/README.md contract/scripts/audit_record_gates.py; sed -n \\\"1,95p\\\" contract/docs/AUDIT-PROTOCOL.md'\"","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"ghsa_id\": \"GHSA-4233-jc72-56c5\",\n  \"cve_id\": \"CVE-2026-102983\",\n  \"url\": \"https://api.github.com/advisories/GHSA-4233-jc72-56c5\",\n  \"html_url\": \"https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-4233-jc72-56c5\",\n  \"summary\": \"Astro: Netlify Image CDN allowlist bypass enables SSRF\",\n  \"description\": \"## Summary\\n\\nThe `@astrojs/netlify` adapter generated regular expressions for Netlify Image CDN remote-image allowlists without anchoring them to the beginning of the URL. Netlify evaluates these expressions with `RegExp.test()`, so an allowed image origin appearing anywhere in a URL, including its path or query string, could satisfy the allowlist.\\n\\nFor example, an application allowing `images.example.com` could accept a source URL such as:\\n\\n```text\\nhttp://127.0.0.1:6379/?url=https://images.example.com/image.png\\n```\\n\\nThe allowlist matched the approved origin in the query string, but Netlify parsed and fetched the URL whose actual host was `127.0.0.1`.\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\nAn unauthenticated attacker can bypass the configured `image.domains` or `image.remotePatterns` allowlist through the public `/.netlify/images` endpoint and cause Netlify Image CDN to make requests to attacker-selected URLs. This may allow probing or reaching internal services, depending on Netlify's network-level egress protections.\\n\\nThe Image CDN attempts to transform responses as images, which limits direct response exfiltration. No confidentiality or integrity impact has been demonstrated.\\n\\n## Affected versions\\n\\n`@astrojs/netlify` versions from 5.2.0 through 8.2.3.\\n\\n## Patches\\n\\nFixed in `@astrojs/netlify` 8.2.4. Generated remote-image allowlist expressions are now anchored to both the beginning and end of the source URL.\\n\\n## Workarounds\\n\\nUpgrade to `@astrojs/netlify` 8.2.4 or later. Before upgrading, disable Netlify Image CDN by setting `imageCDN: false` in the adapter configuration, or remove remote image allowlist entries.\\n\\n## Credits\\n\\nReported by @pacocartones.\",\n  \"type\": \"reviewed\",\n  \"severity\": \"medium\",\n  \"repository_advisory_url\": \"https://api.github.com/repos/withastro/astro/security-advisories/GHSA-4233-jc72-56c5\",\n  \"source_code_location\": \"https://github.com/withastro/astro\",\n  \"identifiers\": [\n    {\n      \"value\": \"GHSA-4233-jc72-56c5\",\n      \"type\": \"GHSA\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"value\": \"CVE-2026-102983\",\n      \"type\": \"CVE\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"references\": [\n    \"https://github.com/withastro/astro/security/advisories/GHSA-4233-jc72-56c5\",\n    \"https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-102983\",\n    \"https://github.com/withastro/astro/pull/17752\",\n    \"https://github.com/withastro/astro/commit/e362d4cf540b27730482455c8fc02efe57d16702\",\n    \"https://github.com/withastro/astro/releases/tag/@astrojs/netlify@8.2.4\",\n    \"https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-4233-jc72-56c5\"\n  ],\n  \"published_at\": \"2026-09-30T23:40:12Z\",\n  \"updated_at\": \"2026-09-30T23:40:13Z\",\n  \"github_reviewed_at\": \"2026-09-30T23:40:12Z\",\n  \"nvd_published_at\": \"2026-09-30T15:22:22Z\",\n  \"withdrawn_at\": null,\n  \"vulnerabilities\": [\n    {\n      \"package\": {\n        \"ecosystem\": \"npm\",\n        \"name\": \"@astrojs/netlify\"\n      },\n      \"vulnerable_version_range\": \">= 5.2.0, <= 8.2.3\",\n      \"first_patched_version\": \"8.2.4\",\n      \"vulnerable_functions\": []\n    }\n  ],\n  \"cvss_severities\": {\n    \"cvss_v3\": {\n      \"vector_string\": null,\n      \"score\": 0.0\n    },\n    \"cvss_v4\": {\n      \"vector_string\": \"CVSS:4.0/AV:N/AC:L/AT:P/PR:N/UI:N/VC:N/VI:N/VA:L/SC:N/SI:N/SA:N\",\n      \"score\": 6.3\n    }\n  },\n  \"cwes\": [\n    {\n      \"cwe_id\": \"CWE-625\",\n      \"name\": \"Permissive Regular Expression\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"cwe_id\": \"CWE-918\",\n      \"name\": \"Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"credits\": [\n    {\n      \"user\": {\n        \"login\": \"pacocartones\",\n        \"id\": 253313177,\n        \"node_id\": \"U_kgDODxlAmQ\",\n        \"avatar_url\": \"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/253313177?v=4\",\n        \"gravatar_id\": \"\",\n        \"url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/pacocartones\",\n        \"html_url\": \"https://github.com/pacocartones\",\n        \"followers_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/pacocartones/followers\",\n        \"following_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/pacocartones/following{/other_user}\",\n        \"gists_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/pacocartones/gists{/gist_id}\",\n        \"starred_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/pacocartones/starred{/owner}{/repo}\",\n        \"subscriptions_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/pacocartones/subscriptions\",\n        \"organizations_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/pacocartones/orgs\",\n        \"repos_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/pacocartones/repos\",\n        \"events_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/pacocartones/events{/privacy}\",\n        \"received_events_url\": \"https://api.github.com/users/pacocartones/received_events\",\n        \"type\": \"User\",\n        \"user_view_type\": \"public\",\n        \"site_admin\": false\n      },\n      \"type\": \"reporter\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"comments\": 2,\n  \"epss\": {\n    \"percentage\": 0.0031,\n    \"percentile\": 0.21687\n  }\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"CVE-2026-102983\",\n  \"sourceIdentifier\": \"security-advisories@github.com\",\n  \"published\": \"2026-09-30T15:22:22.800\",\n  \"lastModified\": \"2026-09-30T19:38:27.293\",\n  \"vulnStatus\": \"Awaiting Analysis\",\n  \"cveTags\": [],\n  \"descriptions\": [\n    {\n      \"lang\": \"en\",\n      \"value\": \"Astro is a web framework for content-driven websites. From 5.2.0 until 8.2.4, the @astrojs/netlify adapter generates regular expressions for Netlify Image CDN remote-image allowlists without anchoring them to the beginning of the URL. Because Netlify evaluates these expressions with RegExp.test(), an allowed origin appearing only in a source URL's path or query can satisfy image.domains or image.remotePatterns while the URL's actual host remains attacker-controlled. An unauthenticated request to the public /.netlify/images endpoint can therefore cause the Image CDN to request attacker-selected URLs and may probe or reach internal services. Netlify egress protections may constrain reachable targets, and image transformation limits direct response exfiltration; no confidentiality or integrity impact has been demonstrated. This issue is fixed in version 8.2.4.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"affected\": [\n    {\n      \"source\": \"security-advisories@github.com\",\n      \"affectedData\": [\n        {\n          \"vendor\": \"withastro\",\n          \"product\": \"astro\",\n          \"versions\": [\n            {\n              \"version\": \">= 5.2.0, < 8.2.4\",\n              \"status\": \"affected\"\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"vendor\": \"@astrojs\",\n          \"product\": \"netlify\",\n          \"versions\": [\n            {\n              \"version\": \">= 5.2.0, < 8.2.4\",\n              \"status\": \"affected\"\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"metrics\": {\n    \"cvssMetricV40\": [\n      {\n        \"source\": \"security-advisories@github.com\",\n        \"type\": \"Secondary\",\n        \"cvssData\": {\n          \"version\": \"4.0\",\n          \"vectorString\": \"CVSS:4.0/AV:N/AC:L/AT:P/PR:N/UI:N/VC:N/VI:N/VA:L/SC:N/SI:N/SA:N/E:X/CR:X/IR:X/AR:X/MAV:X/MAC:X/MAT:X/MPR:X/MUI:X/MVC:X/MVI:X/MVA:X/MSC:X/MSI:X/MSA:X/S:X/AU:X/R:X/V:X/RE:X/U:X\",\n          \"baseScore\": 6.3,\n          \"baseSeverity\": \"MEDIUM\",\n          \"attackVector\": \"NETWORK\",\n          \"attackComplexity\": \"LOW\",\n          \"attackRequirements\": \"PRESENT\",\n          \"privilegesRequired\": \"NONE\",\n          \"userInteraction\": \"NONE\",\n          \"vulnConfidentialityImpact\": \"NONE\",\n          \"vulnIntegrityImpact\": \"NONE\",\n          \"vulnAvailabilityImpact\": \"LOW\",\n          \"subConfidentialityImpact\": \"NONE\",\n          \"subIntegrityImpact\": \"NONE\",\n          \"subAvailabilityImpact\": \"NONE\",\n          \"exploitMaturity\": \"NOT_DEFINED\",\n          \"confidentialityRequirement\": \"NOT_DEFINED\",\n          \"integrityRequirement\": \"NOT_DEFINED\",\n          \"availabilityRequirement\": \"NOT_DEFINED\",\n          \"modifiedAttackVector\": \"NOT_DEFINED\",\n          \"modifiedAttackComplexity\": \"NOT_DEFINED\",\n          \"modifiedAttackRequirements\": \"NOT_DEFINED\",\n          \"modifiedPrivilegesRequired\": \"NOT_DEFINED\",\n          \"modifiedUserInteraction\": \"NOT_DEFINED\",\n          \"modifiedVulnConfidentialityImpact\": \"NOT_DEFINED\",\n          \"modifiedVulnIntegrityImpact\": \"NOT_DEFINED\",\n          \"modifiedVulnAvailabilityImpact\": \"NOT_DEFINED\",\n          \"modifiedSubConfidentialityImpact\": \"NOT_DEFINED\",\n          \"modifiedSubIntegrityImpact\": \"NOT_DEFINED\",\n          \"modifiedSubAvailabilityImpact\": \"NOT_DEFINED\",\n          \"Safety\": \"NOT_DEFINED\",\n          \"Automatable\": \"NOT_DEFINED\",\n          \"Recovery\": \"NOT_DEFINED\",\n          \"valueDensity\": \"NOT_DEFINED\",\n          \"vulnerabilityResponseEffort\": \"NOT_DEFINED\",\n          \"providerUrgency\": \"NOT_DEFINED\"\n        }\n      }\n    ],\n    \"ssvcV203\": [\n      {\n        \"source\": \"134c704f-9b21-4f2e-91b3-4a467353bcc0\",\n        \"ssvcData\": {\n          \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-30T16:22:22.386566Z\",\n          \"id\": \"CVE-2026-102983\",\n          \"options\": [\n            {\n              \"exploitation\": \"none\"\n            },\n            {\n              \"automatable\": \"no\"\n            },\n            {\n              \"technicalImpact\": \"partial\"\n            }\n          ],\n          \"role\": \"CISA Coordinator\",\n          \"version\": \"2.0.3\"\n        }\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"weaknesses\": [\n    {\n      \"source\": \"security-advisories@github.com\",\n      \"type\": \"Secondary\",\n      \"description\": [\n        {\n          \"lang\": \"en\",\n          \"value\": \"CWE-625\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"lang\": \"en\",\n          \"value\": \"CWE-918\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"references\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/withastro/astro/commit/e362d4cf540b27730482455c8fc02efe57d16702\",\n      \"source\": \"security-advisories@github.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/withastro/astro/pull/17752\",\n      \"source\": \"security-advisories@github.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/withastro/astro/releases/tag/@astrojs/netlify@8.2.4\",\n      \"source\": \"security-advisories@github.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/withastro/astro/security/advisories/GHSA-4233-jc72-56c5\",\n      \"source\": \"security-advisories@github.com\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n  133 contract/docs/AUDIT-PROTOCOL.md\n  411 contract/docs/DATA-SCHEMA.md\n  282 contract/scripts/README.md\n  745 contract/scripts/audit_record_gates.py\n 1571 total\n# Audit protocol\n\nContract version: `2026-09-27.3`. Existing batches retain their frozen contracts.\n\nN auditors independently research the same cases. Each auditor completes every step\nbelow; do not divide the research chain between auditors. Work in a fresh context per\ncase and save your findings before reading any other auditor's report or prior verdict.\nUse neutral primary sources, not another agent's conclusions. Retain every case.\n\nFor each step, state **what you conclude, what you observed, and where the evidence is**.\nIf a decisive fact is missing after the bounded probe, name it and the next question;\nnever fill a field by guessing. Execution failure is not a researched EVIDENCE_GAP.\n\n[Data fields](DATA-SCHEMA.md#audit-records) define the output.\nThe local [research-work contract](DATA-SCHEMA.md#research-work-contract) separates\nsupported conclusions from unfinished investigation; record it in the owned sidecar.\n[Audit commands](../scripts/README.md) cover claiming, dispatch and submission.\nOne adjudicator subsequently reviews all reports and completes unresolved research\nunder [ADJUDICATION-PROTOCOL](ADJUDICATION-PROTOCOL.md).\n\n## Research steps\n\n0. **Advisory status:** read the advisory record itself (CVE JSON in cveawg, OSV, GHSA)\n   before the mechanism. State governs: a `REJECTED`, withdrawn or duplicate advisory is\n   `FALSE_POSITIVE` and gets no mechanism work, and a live advisory whose own text\n   contradicts its label is not thereby dead. A dead alias does not reject a live keyed\n   advisory, and two identifiers referring to one vulnerability are not by themselves a\n   false positive. A refuted mechanism or wrong-product report needs affirmative primary\n   evidence and `false_positive_ground`; inability to establish the mechanism is\n   `EVIDENCE_GAP`, never proof of `FALSE_POSITIVE`.\n   For a supported FALSE_POSITIVE, causal introducer/repair fields are explicitly\n   null (not applicable), as defined by DATA-SCHEMA. Preserve examined objects in\n   evidence without assigning them vulnerability roles. An excluded advisory's\n   disposition does not change the verdict of its broader live alias cluster.\n\n1. **Cause:** trigger → vulnerable code → security impact, with preconditions and\n   counterevidence. Separate distinct mechanisms.\n   For resource-exhaustion claims, trace resource accounting from the controlled input\n   through allocation or acquisition to release or reuse. Distinguish requested and\n   returned lengths from allocated or retained capacity; inspect active limits,\n   allocation tiers or pool classes and lifetimes at the affected revision. Bound\n   per-operation and repeated-operation costs against legitimate use, without\n   inventing runtime measurements.\n\n2. **BIC:** the smallest logically atomic introducing change, which is not always the commit\n   it landed in. `introducer_sha` is the atomic change, `landing_commit` the merge/squash\n   object that carried it; compare its immediate parent, trace moves, and separate\n   introduction from later exposure. Declare `bic_granularity`:\n\n   - `ATOMIC`: the object is a logically atomic causal change, not its own `landing_commit`.\n     One parent, an initial commit or a bulk-import label does not establish atomicity.\n     A root tree can be the earliest observable boundary: identify the causal path/hunk and\n     explain minimality in `landing_note`. Do not infer pre-import origin or authorship\n     from that boundary. A known aggregate follows the next two rules.\n   - `SQUASH_DECOMPOSED`: members reconstructable; list their 40-hex shas.\n   - `AGGREGATE_MEMBERS_UNREACHABLE`: members still unavailable after one bounded PR-ref\n     fetch. That probe is the terminus, not the start of a hunt: name the unreachable object\n     (subject, path, epoch, first public appearance) and what would flip the read, then land\n     the row as `BLOCKED`. For an import without a PR, record the authoritative source-history\n     probe and its limit. Push counts and missing public events alone cannot establish\n     content identity, atomicity or the absence of earlier history.\n   - `NON_GIT_BOUNDARY`: the change predates VCS, so `introducer_sha` is null.\n\n   Membership is located by content identity (`--find-object`, pickaxe on the introduced\n   blob), never by PR number or title: the object that landed the content is a carrier, not a\n   member, and a member whose added lines the landing tree does not carry is a candidate from\n   another epoch. An aggregate's trailers, co-authors and badges belong to the aggregate, not\n   to a hunk: they are never BIC attribution. If history is unavailable, isolate the causal\n   diff and say what that costs commit-level minimality.\n\n   Compare behavior in the immediate parent and child, not just the first pickaxe hit.\n   Check shallow boundaries before treating a root or a failed ancestry walk as evidence.\n   Missing local objects are not proof that public history is unavailable: distinguish a\n   commit, its trees/blobs and the product repository from a reporter or umbrella clone.\n   Recover missing objects through one bounded authoritative probe in an owned scratch\n   clone, preserving shared read-only clones; retain its command and output. If the BIC\n   itself is unknown, use the explicit nulls allowed for open records by DATA-SCHEMA.\n\n3. **Fix:** the minimum repairing hunks/commit set and why it closes the defect; follow\n   partial repairs through the residual defect to closure; verify affected/fixed versions\n   separately, and substantiate an unpatched state plus a proposed repair when no fix exists.\n   `fix_sha` is the first mechanism-closing change on the default branch, not whichever\n   landing a lane named. If a side-branch repair reaches the default branch through a\n   merge, identify that integration and compare its default-branch parent with the merged\n   content. Later ancestry alone does not make the side-branch commit the default-branch\n   closure; retain its member and other landing roles separately. Fix-side AI is informational: it never substitutes for BIC-side\n   attribution, and a fix-side marker alone does not move the verdict. If the remediation is\n   partial, or a claimed patched release does not contain the fix, `remaining_gap` names the\n   still-open surface and its closing commit, or none.\n\n4. **AI:** tie AI evidence to causal changes across the history, within its disclosed scope,\n   and record how it was admitted (`ai_admissibility`): first establish that the named object\n   carries the causal change at the claimed granularity, then read its actual marker.\n   A first-party disclosure bound to that causal change is admissible once the\n   record says what binds it; repository-level AI activity, changelog habits, labels and org\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
