{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/zsh -lc \"numactl --cpunodebind=1 --membind=1 sh -c 'sed -n \\\"1,58p\\\" contract/docs/AUDIT-PROTOCOL.md; sed -n \\\"55,100p\\\" contract/.agents/skills/radar-audit/SKILL.md; rg -n \\\"Submission|research.json|strict|check-record\\\" contract/scripts/README.md; wc -l contract/docs/DATA-SCHEMA.md'\"","aggregated_output":"# Audit protocol\n\nContract version: `2026-09-27.3`. Existing batches retain their frozen contracts.\n\nN auditors independently research the same cases. Each auditor completes every step\nbelow; do not divide the research chain between auditors. Work in a fresh context per\ncase and save your findings before reading any other auditor's report or prior verdict.\nUse neutral primary sources, not another agent's conclusions. Retain every case.\n\nFor each step, state **what you conclude, what you observed, and where the evidence is**.\nIf a decisive fact is missing after the bounded probe, name it and the next question;\nnever fill a field by guessing. Execution failure is not a researched EVIDENCE_GAP.\n\n[Data fields](DATA-SCHEMA.md#audit-records) define the output.\nThe local [research-work contract](DATA-SCHEMA.md#research-work-contract) separates\nsupported conclusions from unfinished investigation; record it in the owned sidecar.\n[Audit commands](../scripts/README.md) cover claiming, dispatch and submission.\nOne adjudicator subsequently reviews all reports and completes unresolved research\nunder [ADJUDICATION-PROTOCOL](ADJUDICATION-PROTOCOL.md).\n\n## Research steps\n\n0. **Advisory status:** read the advisory record itself (CVE JSON in cveawg, OSV, GHSA)\n   before the mechanism. State governs: a `REJECTED`, withdrawn or duplicate advisory is\n   `FALSE_POSITIVE` and gets no mechanism work, and a live advisory whose own text\n   contradicts its label is not thereby dead. A dead alias does not reject a live keyed\n   advisory, and two identifiers referring to one vulnerability are not by themselves a\n   false positive. A refuted mechanism or wrong-product report needs affirmative primary\n   evidence and `false_positive_ground`; inability to establish the mechanism is\n   `EVIDENCE_GAP`, never proof of `FALSE_POSITIVE`.\n   For a supported FALSE_POSITIVE, causal introducer/repair fields are explicitly\n   null (not applicable), as defined by DATA-SCHEMA. Preserve examined objects in\n   evidence without assigning them vulnerability roles. An excluded advisory's\n   disposition does not change the verdict of its broader live alias cluster.\n\n1. **Cause:** trigger → vulnerable code → security impact, with preconditions and\n   counterevidence. Separate distinct mechanisms.\n   For resource-exhaustion claims, trace resource accounting from the controlled input\n   through allocation or acquisition to release or reuse. Distinguish requested and\n   returned lengths from allocated or retained capacity; inspect active limits,\n   allocation tiers or pool classes and lifetimes at the affected revision. Bound\n   per-operation and repeated-operation costs against legitimate use, without\n   inventing runtime measurements.\n\n2. **BIC:** the smallest logically atomic introducing change, which is not always the commit\n   it landed in. `introducer_sha` is the atomic change, `landing_commit` the merge/squash\n   object that carried it; compare its immediate parent, trace moves, and separate\n   introduction from later exposure. Declare `bic_granularity`:\n\n   - `ATOMIC`: the object is a logically atomic causal change, not its own `landing_commit`.\n     One parent, an initial commit or a bulk-import label does not establish atomicity.\n     A root tree can be the earliest observable boundary: identify the causal path/hunk and\n     explain minimality in `landing_note`. Do not infer pre-import origin or authorship\n     from that boundary. A known aggregate follows the next two rules.\n   - `SQUASH_DECOMPOSED`: members reconstructable; list their 40-hex shas.\n   - `AGGREGATE_MEMBERS_UNREACHABLE`: members still unavailable after one bounded PR-ref\n     fetch. That probe is the terminus, not the start of a hunt: name the unreachable object\n     (subject, path, epoch, first public appearance) and what would flip the read, then land\n- **Introduction and exposure.** Compare the causal change with its immediate parent\n  under the same conditions. Separate an earlier origin, later exposure and the landing\n  that imports either one. Establish content identity and supported granularity; an\n  initial commit, bulk import, single parent or PR number does not establish atomicity\n  or authorship. A public root is an observable boundary, not necessarily the origin.\n  Assess logical changes rather than file counts: a coherent feature can span many files,\n  while one commit can also contain unrelated changes requiring decomposition.\n  An exhausted member search does not turn a separable bundle into an atomic change.\n- **Repair and release.** Explain which supported change closes the reported mechanism\n  and how that differs from its integration landing and shipment. Preserve qualifications\n  for partial repairs and distinct operating modes. Follow the repair through actual caller\n  wiring, sibling operations and shipped defaults. A changed helper that is not called,\n  an ineffective default or a guard after the unsafe operation cannot establish closure.\n  Verify the relevant released content, not just ancestry or a patched-version label.\n  Resolve actual tags from official release\n  metadata; a guessed tag's404 is not evidence that no release exists. Separate publication\n  from creation and stable releases from prereleases. A source-release finding need not\n  establish every binary or platform, but narrowing scope needs evidence.\n- **AI participation.** Bind a disclosure to the causal contribution and its activity:\n  coding, design advice, review and test generation are different facts. Read introduction\n  and repair attribution separately. Aggregate PR assistance, a human signature or a\n  successful AI-assisted fix does not settle AI's role in the defect. A later policy\n  discussion does not by itself attribute an earlier code change. Before NOT_AI, meet\n  the frozen threshold for named identity, object-local marker absence and contemporary\n  disclosure conventions. Scope absence claims to inspected carriers; apply the carrier\n  coverage of this batch, not a newer contract.\n\nResearch methods remain within the assigned permissions. This skill grants no authority\nto execute exploits or interact with targets.\n\n## Resolve gaps with proportionate evidence work\n\nStart with retained primary material and relevant local history. For shared Git reads,\nuse GIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH=1 and GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0. A non-shallow clone can still lack objects;\na history search selects changes, not every carrier. Verify the content of objects used\nin a conclusion rather than inferring it from a search hit.\n\nUse the frozen recovery rules for each missing introduction or repair fact, retaining\nactual commands, responses and failures in owned scratch space. Prefer authoritative\nraw files and public advisory, contribution or release records; use APIs for facts the\navailable sources cannot settle, and retain responses for reuse. Distinguish a tool's\ninternal retrieval failure from an explicit source access denial. Diagnose and repair\nordinary retrieval faults within existing permissions; a tool error alone does not end\nthe case. Honor exhausted probes, actual access denials and safety refusals without\nrerouting the stopped investigation.\n\n57:read restrictions/mounts when available and record instruction-only isolation otherwise.\n222:## Submission\n235:python3 scripts/audit_record_gates.py --strict <case.json>\n236:python3 scripts/audit_record_gates.py --strict --repo <clone-dir> <case.json>\n249:Submission, research acceptance and publication are separate states in the batch index.\n411 contract/docs/DATA-SCHEMA.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
