## Actual caller, authority, representation and recipient

At repair parent 0f74eca337508e006052a0ae48fb2754c076ec76, middleware selects a built-in/string format via getFormatFunction/compile or accepts an application format function. logRequest executes immediately or after onFinished, may be suppressed by skip, and writes the result plus newline to configured stream/stdout. Object-mode custom output is a separate path. The protected asset is downstream access-log interpretation, not template execution.

User-Agent and Referer/referrer callbacks return request headers directly. URL returns originalUrl or url without percent decoding. Basic-auth's credentials.name supplies remote-user without Morgan authenticating that identity. Registered callbacks pass through tokenValue; string results are escaped. compile JSON-escapes the application's format to construct JavaScript, then concatenates runtime token values. That template escaping does not neutralize log delimiters in those values.

The parent helper neutralizes C0, DEL/C1, U+2028/U+2029 and backslash; it preserves double quote. combined/default quote the request field, referrer and user-agent. common has the quoted request field but no User-Agent/referrer. Built-in remote-user is unquoted. A quote-delimiter remote-user example requires a custom quoted format. URL exposure requires an actual quote to reach the logged URL; percent-encoded quotes are not decoded here, and upstream parsing/proxy normalization can constrain this route.

An unauthenticated requester can influence a logged quoted header, but the application must use the affected token/format and a consumer must assign syntax to quotes. Those prerequisites distinguish log-integrity impact from ordinary plain-text viewing. In combined/default, status precedes the header fields: arbitrary replacement of built-in status through User-Agent alone is unsupported. Direct raw-request custom format functions bypass the registered-token guarantee and require application-specific handling.

These are static source observations and causal inferences corroborated by the CVE, not measured deployed behavior. No hostile input, exploit, HTTP server or target interaction was executed. Receipts: receipts/repair-parent-source.out, first-hardening-diff.out, second-hardening-diff.out, quote-repair-diff.out and their command receipts.

